      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0004" n="art0004">
        <head>WARRINGTON EVENING SCHOOLS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON EVENING SCHOOLS.</p>
        <p>A ROUND OF PRIZE-GIVINGS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0005" n="art0005">
        <head>Indifference of Parents and Children Deplored.</head>
        <p>Indifference of Parents and Children Deplored.</p>
        <p>The Craze</p>
        <p>The work done in the Wan ington evening schools last session has been recognized this week by the presentation of prizes and certificates to the students. A departure from the custom of recent years is the holding of a separate prize distribution for each school, instead of having one large gathering for all the evening classes under the control of the Education Committee. Heatlaside ate' Beamont schools were visited on Monday evening, Holy Trinity and Fait field schools on Tuesday evening, and the proceedings in connection with the Commercial Institute, Arpley- street, took place in the Gymnasium on Wednesday evening. The students at the School of Art and Technical Institute will receive their awards at the Gymnasium en Wednesday, December 6th. The distribution in most canes has been undertaken by members of the Education Committee and the concentration of attention on the individual schools should have the desired effect of 'stimulating the interest and enthusia,nl of the students.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0006" n="art0006">
        <head>HEATHSIDE SCHOOL.</head>
        <p>HEATHSIDE SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>The Modern Craze for Amusements.</p>
        <p>The Rev. Nrs-ILLE WILSON distributed the prizes and certificates at Ileatbside School on Mondoy evening in the presence of a large gathering of students, parents, and friends. Councillor %V. H. J. Jenkins presided and was accompanied by Mr. J. Moore Murray (director of Education) and Mr. 11. B. Roberts (assistant director.) The Cif A I RNIAN impressed upon the students the fact that the assistance they derived from the ir teachers was by no means the most important element for their consideration. The master could only point the way. However strenuous his endeavours might be, the results depended upon the response of the students. it was not always the prize winner at school who was the prize-winner in after-life, so that those who had not been successful had still the opportunity of sooting. One of the dullest boys he ever knew ass schoolboy became president of the House of Representatives in South Australis. The Rev. Father WILSON pointed out that in that school 241 students began the course of study tut only 59 preeented themselves for the examination of the Lancashire and Cheshire Institutes, 42 securing parses in a group of subjects. Ile was afraid those figures were not very cheering or encouraging. He did bot mean that they were worse or better then the results in the other continuation schools—they were much on a par—or that Wart ington was below other mean in regard to the success of its evening classes. It was a very COMMON COMPLAINT THROUGHOUT THE CoUNTRT that sufficient adventage was not being taken of the facilities and opportunities offered by the continuation schoole. The Education Committee could not feel satisfied with the resnits and he was sure the general public ought to feel very discontented about them because it was quite true that, es was commonly stated, there was no tight and adequate return for the</p>
        <p>I M M ENSE F.XPE`DITURE of money, time and energy in the elementary schools. Personally he could not see how it was possible to get a satisfactory return when they remembered that educa- tion in the elementary schools came to an end, for the great number of children, at the age of about thirteen. The School Attendance Committee exempted about I,foo each year between the ages of thirteen and fourteen—much nearer thirteen than fourteen. If, on leaving the day school, they did not attend continuation schools, their education ceased. Very few could con- tinue it by private tuition or self-instruction, and he did not think they could he expected to do no, considering the circumstances in which they were placed and the hard work and long hour. they often had to undergo. At the same time, it was a lamentable atate of affairs. The indifference to the opportunities afforded in the evening schools was shared equally by the parents and the scholars. Some of the pxrents seemed to think very little of the ultimate welfare of their children and were in a great hurry to get them to work, regardless of their fitness to take spa career which would bring its proper advantage and remuneration. The great idea was to get some immediate return, no matter how paltry. The fault was not wholly with the }mean, although they might be expected to show more foresight than the children. Another proof of indifference was that of those who had won prizes in that achool end were asked if they would attend the distribution, thirteen did not give any answer and nine said they were not coming. He attrituted theciomparatively small degree of interest in thy evening chasm' very largely to the RACE FOR ALL RINI'S OF AMUSEMENTS.</p>
        <p>After the work of the day a youth naturally sought recreation, and consequently he was often occupied with football in the daylight and with picture palaces in the winter evenings. A great number did not concern themselves with either, but vacantly paraded the streets. That kind of pleasure and amusement would not last for any great length of time. The day would come when such things would bore then ; when they could not take much interest in the results of football matches and when they would tire of picture palaces. Then how would they recreate themselves ? If they pursued their education further they would (pen to themselves many avenues of amuse- ment, recreation and pleasure. If they left school at the age of thirteen or fourteen they were only just beginning their education and could not appreciate literature. If they went to classes for several years longer they would prepare their minds sufficiently to continue their own education, which should last all through life. There were great advantages in store for those who would persevere, in spite of the drudgery and dryness of learning. Complaints were heard in these days of the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0007" n="art0007">
        <head>DULNESS OF LIFE,</head>
        <p>DULNESS OF LIFE,</p>
        <p>espec'ally for those who had to woris with their hands. So far as be knew those were only two things that were calculated to sweep away the clouds of dulness and allow sunshine to pour down upon the life of the toiler —relig:on and education. Personally, he believed education included religion and could not exclude it, but, as popularly understood, education was the only other thing which would relieve the monotony and dreariness of the toiler's life. It enabled him to take an intelligent interest in his work. Some kinds of employment turned workers into parts of a machine, but they would be able to find much that was edifying and interesting apart from their occupation. One of the subjects taught in the evening schools was English, and he ardently hoped they would carry out the sugges- tion that part of the course should he reading, explana- tion and discussion of passages from a stsndard author. If he could only get some mastery of the English language and literature, the life of the veriest toiler would not be by any means so dull as it usually was. The whole of English literature was within their easy reach. For a few shillings they could provide themselves with the best of reading through- out the year. They could then enter into communica- tion with the greatest heroes, the noblest spirits, and the best thoughts and finest sentiments that English- men had expressed throughout the whole poriod of our history. (Applause). Mr. EDDLN (aperintendcnt) in propcoinga vote of thanks to the Chairman and Father Wilson, pointed out that only those students who bad taken a third year course were eligible for the examination of the Lanca- shire and Cheshire Institutes and that accounted to some extent for the great disparity between the numbers of those joining the classes and those present- ing themselves for examination. Mr. G. NEWBURN seconded and the vote was heartily passed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0008" n="art0008">
        <head>PRIZE LIST.</head>
        <p>PRIZE LIST.</p>
        <p>The following students were given prizes for regular ' attendance and progress :—George Fleming, George Payton, Charles Robbins, Thomas R Atkinson, Thomas Brimelow, Wm. M Cartwright, James Dagnall, Thomas Evans,John Glom, Leonard Gough, John E Hill, blurry Holt, Peter Leicester, Fred Williams, William Birchall, Charles Forster, Robert Hutchinson, Bertie Jones, William Jones, Samuel Knight, harry Lows, Francis O'Toole, Lawrence Astley, Thomas Bradbury, Ernest Clarke, John Faulkner, Jack Furnival, Joseph R Holding, Thomas McGarrv. The following studenta have been successfnl in passing the First Year Preliminary Technical Course Examination of the Union of Lancashire and Cheshire Institute, and were given certificates therefor. They have also been awarded scholarships to the Technical Institute where they are now attending, viz Adams, h iobard Braodwood, John Broadhurst, Richard Butler, James E Darbvshire, Wolter Eccleston, Thomas Hankinson, Alfred Harrison, George If Hazelhorat, Robert E Jones, Robert J Jones, Daniel Lennan, Thomas Millar, John James Millington. Arthur Moores, James 0 Rafter,George A Stevenson, William II Welsh, Harry Whittaker, Harry Wood.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>ror Pleasure.</head>
        <p>ror Pleasure.</p>
        <p>TL. following students have been succersful in passing the First Year Commercial Group C one Examination of the Union of Lancashire und Cheshire Institutes and were given certificates therefor they have also been awarded acholarships to the Commercial Institute where they are now attending, viz. :—Wilfred Clark, John It Flanaghan, Stanley Hill, Herbert [Hodgson, Harold Maims. Herbert Seymour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>BEAMONT SCHOOL.</head>
        <p>BEAMONT SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>After distributing the prizes at the Hest:bide I School the Rev. Father Wilson visited Beamont Schools where he delivered a similar speech. The Rev. James Davies, who presided said that evening schools were doing an excellent work in fitting the boys with the education that was necessary for future life. It would, however he no use their going to school and learning so much unless they turned it into ' practical account. He urged all to give their best attention to the work and they would be sure to reap the benefits in the future. The Rev. Father WiLSON said that school bad an attendance of 193, at the presious opening of the session and out of 67 sitting for examination 42 had been successful in passing. H LONOSHAW (superintendent) in proposing a vote of thanks said it was gratifying to koow that the hard work accomplished last year thr, ugh the excellent attendance and good conduct of the boys had reaped its due reward. This year they had made a good start, for out of an entry of 150 they still had 00 per cent. in attendance and be hoped that they would maintain that to the end. Let them banish all thought of passing pleasures and think only of the lasting benefits that would arias from education.</p>
        <p>Mr. DAINTITH teconded and the vote was warmly passed.</p>
        <p>PRIZE LIST.</p>
        <p>The following students were given priors for regular attendance and progress —Ernest Daniels, Itiulliam Loch, William Pendlebury and Samuel Swanton. The following studints have been successful in pinn- ing the Fir,t Year Preliminary Technical Croup Course Examination of the Union of Lancashire and Cheohire</p>
        <p>. - - Institute and were given certificates therefor. They have also been awarded scholarships to the Technical Institute where they are now attending :—Sydney Allender,`William Henry Brookes, Frank Dillon,lhomas Fallon, William Forster, Charles Helsby, Alfred Higham Rowland Hill, John Stanley Jackson, Henry Lea, Joseph Martin, John Primly Rigby, Ernest Smith, Thomas Warburton, Edmund W Wilkinson, James Vernon Wood,. George Yates. The following students have been successful in passing the Fast Year Commercial Group Course Examination of the Union of Lancashire and Cheshire lost it Butes and were given certificates therefor. They have also been awarded scholarshit s to the Commercial Institute, where they are now attending, viz. :—Hyrold Clayton, Albert John Gibbs, Charles Hanson, Peter S McKay, James Milner, Arthur Topping.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>HOLY TRINITY.</head>
        <p>HOLY TRINITY.</p>
        <p>The Importance of Housework.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday evening the prizes and certificates pined by the pupils at Holy Trinity branch technical school were distributed by Miss Ferriman, of the Warrington Training College. Mrs. C. Jackson Holmes presided and amongst others present were : Miss Janet Bibby (superintendent), Mies D ivies (head mistress of the day school),Mr J Moore Murray (director of education) and Mr R Roberta (assistant director). _ _</p>
        <p>Mrs. ilommEg impressed upon the young people the importance of being in earnest In every day things and of taking life seriously. By no doing they would make better women and better citizens, for they most not forget that they were the rising generation and ought to take the place of those who would be gone when they were grown up, in whatever town they might be placed. It was absolute folly and waste of time to live only for gimes awl pleasure. She would like to recommend them to hang up in their homes Long fellow's beautiful poem" A Psalm of Life," so that they might he spurred on by those sentiments. It was a great source of disappointment that more young people did not make it a duty to attend at least some of the manifold evening dames mranged for their beneth.And would those who were present try and influence others to attend? They would only be young once and now was the time to improve themselves in every practical way. "I appeal very especially to you girls " continued Mrs. Holmes " to fit yourselves out first of all to be practical house wives. lam convinced there would be more harmony In the home if the girls did their duty in lite and studied in a practical way all the branches of domestic science. I have found girls wh3 demanded high pay, very deficient in such simple things as damping and folding clothes,boiling eggs and potatoes and washing and ironing. It does not matter what station of life a girl may be placed is, it is her supreme duty to be able to do everything in a house, no that she may be able to help herself and family in time of need or otherwise. I speak with feeling on this point, and hope very much that you will all take advantage of these opportunities placed in your way to make yourselves perfect in household management and control, which is your highest form of labour. You can do this in the first instance by attending evening classes. Don't ever be no foolish as to say or think that you will never need to do this, that or the other. No one knows what will be needed of them as time goes on. The one thing in life is duty." (Applause) Miss FERRI NI AN delivered an eminently practical sun homely address. She pointed out that the evening classes gave girls the opportunity of fitting themselves for VAT iOU3 walks of life but whether they took up business study or domestic science, she wished to impress upon them that every girl should devote some part of her time to home duties. They must not dismiss such duties as " mother's work" but always he willing to lend a helping hand. They might be inclined to say" Anybody can do housework," but they could not it they had not learnt to do it. Let them train for their own particular work as milliners, dressmakers, clerks' or workers in factories, but let them train also for home . . . .</p>
        <p>work. They should all familiarise themselves with such tasks as keeping the house clean, mending clothes, and cooking. For girls who had a decided liking for housework, it was really a good thing for them to go out as servants. They got excellent wages, good food and good rooms, and a good servant was always able to Improve her position. They must always bear in mind that cork should come first and pleasure and recreation second. These recreations should also be really helpful ones, good for the health and for physical development. Nowadays young people were tending to pay too much attention to places of amusement, but she thought they could find good recreation in a less exciting fashion. They should also ha careful not to wants time or money. She particularly advised them not to spend money on cheap finery which did them no good and did not last, whilst in some cases they no doubt hid to deny themselves of something that woo really necessary is order to obtain it. A great deal of the poverty they saw in the streets of Warrington and other large towns was due not so much to the fact that people were very poor as to their neglect to take care of what they had. (Hear, hear), If the young people wasted pennies, they would probably waste pounds when they became older. Finally, Miss Ferriman advised the pupils never to fly in the face of the advice of their parents end others who were older and more experienced than themselves. (Applause). . A vote of thanks was passed to Mrs. Holmes and Miss Ferriman, on the proposition of Min BIBBY. PRIZE LIST. The following were the awards :—Annie Burke and Dori. May Collins, first dans certificate:tin needlework; Gladys It Cotterill and Maud Duracell, first class certificates in needlework and second.class in ccokery ; Amy Holt and May Hawthorne, first class certificates in needlework ; Hannah James and May Jefferson first class certificates in needlework and . .</p>
        <p>second class in practical cookery ; Bessie Jolley Rose Lau ton and Catherine Mahoney, first class certifi- cate in needlework : Laura Murray, first class certificate in needlework and second class in principles of and practical cookery ; Jessie Newton, first class certilicite in needlework ; Mary Frances Overend, first class certificate in needlework and second class in practical cookery ; May Sankey and Lillian Sutton, first class in needlework ; Ada Wallington, second class certificates In principles of and practical cookery ; Eliasbeth Willcock, first class certificate in needlework : Nellie Yatee, second class certificate in practical cookery. The following students have been successful in pass- ing the Preliminary DLmestio Group Course Examina- tion of the Union of Lancashire and Cheshire Insti- tutes and were given certificates therefor. They also I have been awarded Domestic .cholarships to the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>Plea for Politeness.</head>
        <p>A Plea for Politeness.</p>
        <p>The prizes and certiiic .ten gained by students of the Commercial Instituto,rArpley-street,were distributed by Mr. I I olmen Kingdon at the Gymnasium on Wednesday evening. Coenclllor Hellion presided and amongst others present were Mrs C Jackson Holster, Mr .1 Moore Murray (director of educatien), Mr R B Roberts (assistant director) and Mr S W Jeffery (auperiutendent of tee institute). The Cu AI not.tx said the average cumber of attend- ances and passes secured by the students at the evening classes was quite equal to those of any other town in Lancashiie. It was, however, regtettable that in proportion to population, the number of students enrolling themselves was not Up to the star.dard of some towns in the country. With regard to the suitability of the facilities provided by the Education Committee, he believed that if any section of the inhabitants required any breech of educrtion which was not at present included in the syllabus, the authority would be anxious to meet the demand, if a sufficient number of scholars could be guaranteed. There were indications in these days that amusements were distracting the attention of the young from self-improvement. As an old athlete be would be the last to deprecate healthy pleasures and recreations, but he thought parents ought to study the interests of their children nod adopt a methodical plan of study, sport and pleasure. The manufacturers of Warrington could materially assist in remedying the shortage of pupils if they would give a bonus in the form of extra wages to those of their employees who obtained certificates. He believed one firm had already set an example in that direction and if others would follow suit it would haves great effect in equipping us for the industrial struggle which became more severe every year. (Applause). Mr. KINGDOM, lathe course of a 'racy and informing address, spirted Aristotle's definition of education as "an orneruent in prosperity and a refuge in adversity." lAs a businessnean be (the speaker) thought the greet advantage of education was that it enabled one to appreciate one's own ignorance. It was not until they had studied a question and were entitled to express an opinion, that they folly realized how little they knew about it. The man who was the leant ready to express an opinion was very often the man who knew the most about s questiens and cenversely the man who was readiest to give his views often knew the least about it. Mr.Eingdon a ent on to emphasise the necessity for appre- ciation of education. Of course they could not expect to put old heads on young shoulders, but he sometimes wondered whether we in this country had quite that appreciation of educetion which existed elsewhere. In Scotland there was undoubtedly a higher appreciation of Education than in England. In travelling he had been struck with the keenness of other nations, and particularly of America, in that direction. The speaker instanced a poor washerwoman in America who told him that the great burden she hal to bear was the eucation of her daughter. She was sending her to the best school in the town and had to strain every nerve to mire the necessary money for [het purpose. He wondered whether the youth of to-day realized the extraordinary opportunitier they possessed, compared with the students of a comparatively short time back ? Attendance at evening classes involved .n certain amount of self-sacrifice but it ought really to he regarded as a form of investment which would bear a rate of interest in after life that was unknown in the world of finance. They must make up their minds not to be deterred from the goal they had in view by the necessity of goring lip some spare time to continue their education after leaving the day schools. One word of warning he wonld give t " Don't try to run before you can walk." The instruction at the Commercial Institute was only the foundation for the final erection of a , higher knowledge. With regard to the interest of employers in the question, one firm in the town made its condition of employment that a boy should have attained a certain standard and, if ha was in the works, he must attend evening classes until he was seventeen. That might seem a hardship to some, but he did not think it was. It would be a great advantage if every employer would adopt a similar policy, , although he could not say that that the firm in gees' ion ' would welcome it, because at the present time they flattered themselves that they got hold of the best boys. If an employee patio 85 per cent, of attendances and sat for ex ernination, the fee was returned to him and he received a small monetary prize. There was an extraordinary feeling in the minds of some people that anything that was novel and compulsory ought to be avoided at any cost. Why should the compulsory element condemn a thing? The whole of our lives was compulsion. Personally he gloried in a bit of compul- sion ; he thought it was a jolly good thing for us. The speaker next touched upon one matter In which all could obtain prizes—he referred to the great room for improvement in the MANNERS OE TIM RISING .DEMERLTION. . .</p>
        <p>(Applause). Fortunately we had passed the time when on ringing up somebody on the telephone in Warrington, a voice at the other end exclaimed " Art a mo." (Laughter) Re WAS not, bo mover, quite sure that we had got over the., time when a voice occasionally said," Old 'ard a minute "—which was almost as bad. (Laughter.) Manners were a great asset in life, but he did not think the mode of life and general education of to-day enabled tkern to grasp the importance of manners. Consequently th 3 deficiency was not always apparent. Politeness cost them nothing but it enabled them to get on in the world. A good address, good presence, good Mal2lloll and politeness developed throughout life would carry a man through where many a better man Ind failed for the lack of them. The general knowledge imparted in our schools was, he believe], improving and Ire hoped the young people would make the best use of the few years during which they were forming habits that would be difficult to break in after-life. (Applause.) On the proposition of Mr. S. W. JEFFERY, seconded by Mr. W. H. CR&amp;GG, a vote of thanks was passed to Mr. Kingdon and the Chairman.-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>PRIZE LIST.</head>
        <p>THE PRIZE LIST.</p>
        <p>The following is a list of the awards :—Joseph Banjos, prize for the fifth year course certificates in book-keeping, and vcond place in the Union of Lancashire and Cheshire Institutes in commercial law ; Alfred Bromley, prize for the third year course and certificates in arithmetic, book-keeping and business routine ;William J Corner, prize for the fourth year course and certificates in arithmetic, book-keeping and business routine ; James A Dutton, prize for the third year course and certificates in business routine and bbortband ; Lionel V Hainswortb, prize for the third year course and certificates in arithmetic, book-keeping and business routine ; Herbert E Sankey, prize for the third year course and junior commercial group course certificate; Arthur Oldfield, prize for the third year course and certificates in book-keeping, business routine and shorthand ; Stanley C Owen, prize for the third year coarse and certificates in arithmetic, book-keeping and business routine ; John W Weir, • prize for the third year comes and Junior commercial group course certificate ; Harry T Ashley, Gilbert C Crossley, Sydney Egerton, John H J Sampson, and Thomas Howliston, prize for the second year course and second year commercial group course certificate ; Lewin Jones, prize for the second year course, which, at the request of student, has taken the form of remission of school fees, and second year commercial group course certificate ; John E Kelly and John R Lumpard, prize for the second year course and second year commercial group course certificate ; William Welsby, prize for the second year course, which, at the reqii cut of the student, has taken the form of remission of school fees, and second year commercial group course certificate; Frances 0 Corbett prize for the second year course and second year com- mercial group course certificate ; Eleanor Ratcliffe, prize for the second year course. The following students have been successful in passing the First Year Csmmercial Group Course Examination of the Union of Lancashire and Cheshire Institute arid were givrn Certificates therefore. They</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>•:, ~,..r•-, \ ,--,v•-,.., \ ..‘,.-,5.N,--'l,-•,-"V•A..e c'.. CHILDREN'S CORNER, Conductcd by IJECLE CALEB ~,--\.—\.,,_,,,—,N.,‘,.---.,•—••-••-•...4-s,.•.-,/,.i</head>
        <p>•:, ~,..r•-, \ ,--,v•-,.., \ ..‘,.-,5.N,--'l,-•,-"V•A..e c'.. CHILDREN'S CORNER, Conductcd by IJECLE CALEB ~,--\.—\.,,_,,,—,N.,‘,.---.,•—••-••-•...4-s,.•.-,/,.i</p>
        <p>WHY THE HARES NOSE IS SPLIT. A charming feature in "The State S' for October is a pair of stories out of South African folklore. The first is a Hottentot legend to explain why the hare's nose is split. This is how the Hottentot tells it : Long, long ago, when the world was quite young, the Lady Dlr on wanted someone to take a message to Men. She tried first one creature and then another, but no ! they wete all to , busy, they were all too busy, they couldn't go At last she called the Crocodile. He is very elow and not much good, but the Lady Moon thought she would pinch his tail and mate him go quickly. S, she said to him: "Go down to men and give them this message: ' As I die and, dying, live, to shall you die and, dying, live.'" As the Crocodile went on its way, quickly while the Moon watched him, slowly when he was out of her sight, the Little Hare I arrived, and asked, "Where are you going in such a hurry, Oom Crocodile?" "The Lady Moon has sent me with a message to men, As I die, and, dying live, so also shall you die, and, dying live.'" The Hare replied "You are so slow. Give me the message, and I will take it." " Very well," said the lazy Crocodile. So the Hare went off like the wind At last be came to Men, and he called them together and said, "Listen, Sons of the Baboon, a wise man comes with a mess.age. By the Lady Moon am I sent to you: 'As I die, and, dying perish, so shall you also die and come wholly to an end.'" Than Men looked at each other and shivered. All of a sudden the flesh co their arms was like goose-flesh. And while the poor human race was in this state of abject terror, the Hare returned to the Moon and told her the Message he had given, and laughed with glee to think that men were all stiff with fright. Whereupon the Lady Moon was very angry and took it big stick and struck at him. But he ducked and slipped away, and it caught him only on the nose. Then the Hare" forgot that the Moon was a lady, and scratch, scratch, scratch, he was kicking, and hitting, and clawing the Moon's face till the pieces flew"</p>
        <p>And that is why to-day he goes about with a split nose, and golden face of the Lady Moon has long dark scars.</p>
        <p>A JAPANESE STORY.</p>
        <p>Once upon a time a peasant went to heaven, and the first thing he saw was a long shelf with something very strange upon it. " What is that?" he asked. "Is it some- thing to make soup of ?" (The Japanese are very fond of soup.) "No," was the reply, "those are ears. They belonged to persons who, when they lived on earth, heard what they ought to do in order to be good, but they didn't pay any attention to it, so, when they died, their ears came to heaven, but the rest of their bodies c3uld not." After awhile the peasant saw another shelf with very queer things on it. " What is that ?" he asked again. "Is that something to make soup of?" "No," he was told, "these are tongues. ' They once belonged to people in the world who told people how to live and how to do good, but they themselves never did as they told others to do; so, when they died, their tongues came to heaven, but the rest of their bodies could not."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>HOCKEY</head>
        <p>HOCKEY</p>
        <p>FRIENDLY MATCHES.</p>
        <p>GREAT S ANKEY LADIES' CLUE V. CLEB3IOOR by. Great Sankey journeyed to Clubmoor on Saturday and a hard game ended in a win for the visitors by two goals to one. The game opened briskly and Miss Borleigh (centre forward) was successful in scoring both goals for Sankey. Weaste Hyde 7 Cheadle 3 Brookfield 4 Sale Moor Urmaton Crmston Leigh Ladiee Chorlton Brooldands LATCHFORD.</p>
        <p>The First Eleven won by 4.1 at Widnes last week having a bit in hand all the game. The Widnes hacks were outpaced by the visitors forwards, Pearson and Dale on the right wing making much headway for the latter to get some fine centres across. The Latchford goal-keeper kept out several good shots, but on the 'whole he was well protected by his backs, Allanson removing the ball from dangerous positions on several occasions. The halves were also up to the usual standard. Pearson (2), Parker (I), Albinson (I) scored for Lstchford. The Second Eleven won at Bebbington by 5-1. The home team had the assistance of Poole the Lancashire County goalkeeper. Latchford made little progress in the first half. Poole robbing the forward.; many times in daring fashion by running out to the edge of the circle. The Bebbington becks were somewhat weak. The second half resolved itself into a duel between Poole and the opposing forwards and although beaten 4 times the goalkeeper played a One game. Latchford were pretty good all round and their goals were scored by W. Fletcher (3), Starkey (2). The First Eleven v. Alderley Edge Ist at Alderley this afternoon is :—T Dixon, goal, P Albanson, G 11 Heald, backs ; F W Isherwood, J B Wilkinson, A Knight, halves ; R A Dale, J Pearson. D Travis, If Parker, E Banks, forwards. Referee, Mr W B Cobb. Train from Arpley Station 1.14 pm. Tbe Second Eleven v. Ashley at Hale iv Wright. goal W H Pickles, R Parkinson, backs : 11 Fortes, A E Bradshaw, T Banks. halves H Fletcher, A Starker, W Fletcher, W H Minard., H Colton, forwards. Train from Central Station 1.35 p.m.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>NAVAL COSISIANDER DROWNED.</head>
        <p>NAVAL COSISIANDER DROWNED.</p>
        <p>.\ wireless message received by Imperieuse at Portland from 11.111.5. St. Vincent. states that Commander Frederick Cl. Brine, of the latter ship, was washed overboard on Wednesday where the ship was on her way from Portland to Berebaven. The St. Vincent and other battleships of the lit Division searched for the commander, but were unable to find any trace of</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>TOO MANY SUBJECTS.</head>
        <p>TOO MANY SUBJECTS.</p>
        <p>Class Teachers and Modern Educatioual Methods</p>
        <p>Criticism of modern educational methods were forth- coming from the president (Mr. W. Glover) at the annual meeting of the Certificated Teachers Class Association which was held on Saturday morning in the Wycliffe Memorial Rooms. At the outset Mr A Knowles presided over a moderate attendance. NATIONAL FEDERATION OF ASSISrANT TEACHERS.</p>
        <p>The conference of the above was held at Norwich on the 26th and 301.6 September, and Messrs. A. Knowles and G. Newburn had attended as local delegates. They reported that" the Holmes-Morant Circular was con- demned," and that the proposition relative to Equal salaries for both male and female teachers doing similar work was defeated. It was also recommended that. when conferences take place between his Alsjesty's inspectors and head teachers, class teachers should be present. Their report was adopted and a cordial vote of thanks was tendered to them as delegates.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>FINANCE.</head>
        <p>FINANCE.</p>
        <p>Mr.l'.Eddeo (treasurer) submitted the balance sheet, from which it was noted that the finances of the association were in every way satisfactory and a balance of £5 18s. remained in hand. The financial statement as submitted was approved. THE SECRETARY'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>Mr. GEORGE NEwErRN said that the association had now completed its ninth year and he was pleased to be able to report further progress. They had a membership of 185, and practically every local certia- cated teacher was within their ranks. Respecting the Lancashire Federation of Assistant Teachers they had now two class teachers for Lancashire on the N.U.T. Executive, The National Federation of Assistant 'Teachers was putting forth rather an extensive pro- gramme, one of the outstanding features being a general revision and amendment of scales of salaries through- out the country. ELECTION OF OFFICERS.</p>
        <p>Presidelt,Mr M Glover ; vioe-presidea,,Mias Kitchen; tree surer, Mr T Eddon ; secretary,Mr George Newborn; committee, Misses Bolton, Brookes, Ewart and Raffery, and Messrs F 11 Longshaw, J. Armstong, S Price, R E Bortt and J E Whitebouss. PRESIDENTS ADDRESS.</p>
        <p>The new PRESILIF.ST moved that a hearty vote of thanks be accorded to Mr. A. Knowles for his services during the past year and this was carried, Mr. Knowles suitably responding.</p>
        <p>TEACHERS DIFFICULTIES.</p>
        <p>Mr. GLOVER then said that the lage class was undesirable, and the President of the Board of Educa- tion who could solve rthe difficulty would have his name inscribed on the roll of honour by the teaching profession. The time table, before which all class teachers were abject slaves, should be made more elastic, so that the subjects might be interchanged at the discretion of the teacher, or even new subjects subotituted as occasion might justify. (J-leir, hear).</p>
        <p>TOO MANY SUBJECTS?</p>
        <p>That wonderful contrivance contained far Leo many subjeots. They must give so many minutes per week to certain necessary subjects, but they must alsongive so many to other subjects like brushwork science, or home management. Boys must go to manual classes and girls to cookery cmsses. It was good for the children to learn these things ; tut, after all, were not a few things well known, better than a smattering of many ? (Applause.) The day was not far distant, however, when another yes, perhaps two, would be added to the school life of the child. The teachers would then have more time at their disposal for teaching those extra subjects. They aimed at preparing the child for leading a useful life, for getting smelling out of him which would be useful to the world, and in that connection a new school of thought wan arising which appeared to recognize that fact. But inspectors and others knew only too well that it was more easy to assess the value of the teacher's work on the old system than on a new one, when the results would only be apparent after many years. With regard to themselves, they were subject to numerous regula- tions (though in Warrington they had been rather fortunate compared with some other large towns!). They had to carry out the whims •,..nd fancies of every Government Inspector who might happen to look in. For instance they all rememberel the recent desire for teachers to•make notes of lessons. No every teacher worthy of the name must spew' a csrtEin portion of his leisure time in preparing for future lessots. But that was a serious attempt to curtail the teacher's leisure almost to the vanishing point in many cases. In a pro- fession where, during the whole time from entering to leaving the school the teacher's attention most be con- centrated on his work, a certain amount of leisure was absolutely necessary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>MR. F. NEILSON ON THE CONSERVATIVE PARTY.</head>
        <p>MR. F. NEILSON ON THE CONSERVATIVE PARTY.</p>
        <p>Mr. P. Neilson, M.P., speaking on Saturday night after the opening of a new Liberal Club at Iligh-lane, in the Hyde division, said that ashen he read in Mr. Balfour', speech the other day of the disease which sometimes came upon people like palsy, he thought that was a true commentary on the Tory party of to-day. No Liberal ever made such a bitter attack on the Conservative party as Mr. lislfour unconsciously did when he said the work was to be left to younger men than himself. They were told that Mr. Bonar Law was to be elected as lender of the Opposition in the House of Commons. Imagine his leading the opposi- tion in the House of Commons to Home Rule, the Disestablishment of the Welsh Church and the establish- ment of Manhood Suffrage. If Mr. Balfour was there, Mr. Law's position would be invidious and absurd. Mr. Balfour had led the Tory party as no other man could have done after three defeats in the field, but now they had hounded him cut. No party could exist on a cry. A party to exist must have a MM. (Hear, hear . And the Tory party had no cause. The reason why the Liberal party had been successful was because it had a cans*. (Applause).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>COMPULSORY TRAINING IN AUSTRALIA. Difficulties in the Working of the Scheme.</head>
        <p>COMPULSORY TRAINING IN AUSTRALIA. Difficulties in the Working of the Scheme.</p>
        <p>(FROM TIIE " MANCHESTER GUARDIAN " CORRESPONDENT.) MELBOURNE., OCTOBER 11</p>
        <p>Compulsory military training for IS.ds between the ages of 14 arid Is is not working very satisfactorily in Australia. Many anomalies and difficulties are arising. and even the friends of the principle are getting uneasy las to how the system will work out. A minority of the trade unions still hold out against the system altogether, and in some cases both passive and active resistance to the new system is openly encouraged. In order to be classed as efficient the boys—now all, nominally, enrolled in a gigantic Senior Cadets Corps 100,000 strong—are under an obligstitn to do 90 hours' drill a year—four whole day drills of CM hours each, twelve half-day drills of three hours each, and twenty- four night drills of ali hour and a half each. Trouble has arisen over the distribution of these drills over the year and the application to young meals different classes of society of the regulations enforcing the obligation. In the big secondary schools concessiono have been allowed which have varied the monotony of three hours' continuous drill ; but working lads who have to take their training after many hours' duty in factory, orice, and workshop ccrsider the canditions hard.</p>
        <p>The Melbourne Age," always a consistent advocate of the principle of compulsory training, publishes an article which is unusually critical of the working of the scheme and which rstiects the temper of the industrial population. After recounting the ccnces- alone made to the secondary schools, the "Age" says : " The case is, however, quite different with the working lads. With them Saturday afternoon drills of three solid hours are still, in hundreds of centres, compulsory. . . . Is it any wonder that the whole system is becoming hateful to the lads? They see their more fortunately placed friends—who either attend a big secondary school or university, or have somehow managed to escape registration altogether— rush off to their cricket, football, lacrosse, or tennis, while they, after their mid-day meal on Saturday, have to drill for three hours in the roads of their tuburbs or in some paddock. The area officers say they ale practi- cally helpless in the matter, as they are compelled to seize on Saturday afternoons as the only time when the lads are free. If the necessary drills prescribed by law are to be put in within twelve months they simply must adopt this apparently harsh policy. . . . The time has come fur a reconsideration of the whole question of Saturday afternoon drills. Why shotild it be necessary that they should last three hours stall? A reconsideration of the entire pcsi. ion should be ordered by the Minister of Defence before the end of the present session of Parliament, so that, if necesaary, amending legislation may be introduced. . Sacrifices for their country all healthy-minded Auuralian lads will cheerfully make, liot they must not be burdened with demands which implant within them u rankling sense of injustice and a consciousness of unnecessary hardship." A great experiment is being tried. It may be ruined if the Government blindly follows the advice of "experts' who counsel the placing of the youth of the nation under intolerable and unnecessary burdens.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>WRECKED NERVES and Chronic Indigestion Cured I y</head>
        <p>WRECKED NERVES and Chronic Indigestion Cured I y</p>
        <p>- - DR. CASSELL'S TABLETS For Weak and Nervous People.</p>
        <p>Mr. 0. Abbott, a Police Court Missionary, at Gipsy- road, Leicester, writes : —'• I suffered dresafully from paralytis of the nerves and chronic dyspepsia. I was very weak and thin, and quite unable to walk, and I was unable to walk, and I was confined to my lied for over six months. I tried many different medicines, tut all to no use. At last I tried Dr. Cassell's Tablets for Weak and Nervous People, and soon I was able to walk acme, the bedroom with the aid of is stick, and DOW am in the beat of health." Dr. Cassell's Tablets for Weak and Nervous People will cure all forms of nerve organic and physical weakness in children or adults. Price MA., in. 11d. end 2s. gd. of all chent'sts. Send 2d. to Dr. Cassell's Co. Ltd., King St. W., Marchester for a free trial arta.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>UNSOLD LANDS.</head>
        <p>THE UNSOLD LANDS.</p>
        <p>By A. Duganne.</p>
        <p>A billion of acre. of unsold lands Are lying in grievous dearth ; And millions of men in the image of Cod</p>
        <p>Are starving—all over the earth Oh! tell me, ye sons of America! . .</p>
        <p>How mucCmen's lives are worth! Ten hundred millions of acres good, That never knew spade or plough:— And a million of souls, in our goodly lind, Are pining in want I trow : And orphans are crying for bread this day, And widows in misery b)w ! To whom do these acres of land belong? And why do they thriftless lie" —And why is the widows' lament unhear,l—</p>
        <p>And stifled the orphans' cry? And why are the poor-house and goal so full— And the gallows-tree built high? Those millions of acres belong to Man ! And his claim is—that he needs ! And his title is sealed by the hand of God— Our God! Who the raven feeds: And the starving soul of each famished man At the throne of justice pleads ! Ye may not heed it; ye haughty men, Whose hearts as rock are cold— But the time will come when the fiat of God In thunder shall be told ! For the voice of the great I AM bath said, Mkt "the land shall not be sold."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <p>0.. • ; ... ~...._ w-'-- Alse44o - e.'4. itry, . '... •'. 4-.42, • 4114_ .41....... ...... •...- .• co- . „woe Wr. •••144,0 A, • ....,, Vret .41i- 07- e. -11!. .6- *gf• 00. • ^ .6.80. ...„%' ..0 .it War .01 e.: 4..- 6'40 - 044 - :Mr iii 117 el ititif , 60. ... ~ .- M !LW= • ... • ~.-! 4-: ...., . .Ik ' ' •• 4.., . . .itofi e 4. Oe 0' (V Ak.grt e 10* 4eOA. 6- 0.-4-IFt- :ip. ea, .. .f.e 45 411. •• 4,- ciel, • vue e. . ee Co. WO. •4?.*, • e _,,,,Sl4lzetao• '6:6 og t •ii4tistit: 44•112? • • • .• • 0 - ••• • •'••• • • • •'6</p>
