      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>TEMPERANCE SUNDAY.</head>
        <p>TEMPERANCE SUNDAY.</p>
        <p>PUBLIC MEETING IN THE PARR HALL.</p>
        <p>A Call to the Churches.</p>
        <p>On the initiative of the Warrington Band of hope Union, Sunday last Nan °lnward as Temp'erance Sunday, and vt several places of worship reference was made to the drink evil, and a strong appeal addressed to Christian workers to combat it. In the evening a public meeting was held in the Pere Hall, the Rev. W. Cooper presiding over a fairly large attendants.. Amongst others present on the platform were the Revs E Morgan, .1 S Chedbarn, B Ogden, G Curtis, Mrs Dodds, Mrs Simpson, Mrs Curtis, Messrs P Gandy, .1 Griffiths, J Owen, W Wright, It Pew, Tani. nod T Dale. A children's choir, under the conductorship of Mr A Spruce, sang to mperanee hymns. Mr. Tanner was at the organ. A recitation " Our folks' was effectively given bv Mr. 11. t hedlurn. The Cut AIRMAN referred ith satisfaction to the fact that the powers or one great bsrrier to temperance reform—the House of Lords—bud been curtai ed. (Applause.) Ile hoped the temperance party would take full advantage of all that would accrue to them through the restriction of the Veto. Another hopeful feature to which the rev, gentleman called attention was the t xcellent effect which the earlier closing of public• houses had In Liverpool during the strike troubles, the number of caw of drunkenness being convideral ly dirninithed thereby. Ile expressed approval of the recommendations of the Licensing Committee of the Liverpool that the hours of opening should be perma- trendy curtailed and raid that if that policy were carried out, the strike would have had at least one good result. (Hear, bear.) Steps were being taken -</p>
        <p>TO BRING WARRINGTON INTO I,INP. Wl7ll LIVERPOOL I. the m 'tier. Ile appealed to the public to back up the committee which was going into the question, no that the temperance workers in Warrington might not be left behind. They must try in that may to make the town a bit more sober than it bud been. (Applauve). lie sighed publn opinion could be ripened in other directions. That day was Temperance Sunday, and he hoped thoss chwana where temperance or total abstinence had been preached would be quite consistent, and that if they had • sale of work, or laver or some other money- raising object in view, they would taboo the brewer and the publican. (Applause). An things DOW stood, they rendered as much honour to those people as they possibly could—in feet in eomecaaes it was a race as to who could get there first It wan time they branded those people in their right colours, and gave them to understand that they were social e utcasts. The Churches should lead in the matte-, seeing that the trade worked such great havoc in one it idst. Let them set an example of plain speaking and call a spade a spade. Let them call the people he had ad to outcasts of society and treat them as such. Then when tiny f,und the position they had attained largely through the weakness and failures of other people was not as they would like it to be, some of them would perhaps not go to the lengths they had done in carrying on their trade. He hoped the day was cot far distant when temperance workers would take full advantage of their powers and the drunkennees of the day would become a thing of the peat. (applause). The Rev. J. S. CREDIIURN, in the course of a vigorous and earnest address, said he wished to sound • call for personal individual effort and activity on behalf of the temperecce cause. It woo many years since Cowper sing Blares cannot breathe in England, if their lungs Receive om air, that moment they are free ; They touch our country and their shackles fall. And yet he (the speaker) feared the poet was as much mistaken then as anyone would be who uttered a 'Mailer sentiment to-day. If it were true that we as a nation enjoyed more physical, political and religions freedom than any other nation, it was equally true that the fetters of the huge, hideous moral tyranny of drink were more firmly rivetted upon our necks than upon any other nation in the world. Of all forms of slavery, that was the most cruel and degrading. The most pitiable of all slaves was the slave of lust and miser- , ably wretched, umpeakably pitiable as wee the ! condition of the descendants of the Patriarchs under the thrall of the Egyptians' of the poor galley slaves , of chemical time., or of the poor Degrees in the American cotton fields and sugar plantations, their con- dition ass more than paralleled by that of our own nation under the heel of the drink evil to-day. Debilitating and exhausting of all the best, highestand noblest qualities of manhocd as was the enslavement of the hody, it did not so readily demoralize rad degrade the homan soul as the voluntary slavery into which vast multitudes of our fellow-countrjmen bad sold themselves for the gratification of an appetite and made themselves over, body and soul, to a tyrant whose only wages were shame, poverty, disease and death. It was enough to make angels weep to see men wearing out their lives sith toil, spending their strength and bodily substance for the sake of producing means for ernbruting themselves and indirectly enriching those in whom they had no interest and who had no interest in them except as they were the sources of their ill-gotten wealth and luxury—the publican with his brilliant bar, ostentatious magnificence rind plethoric abundance, the brewer with his seat in Parliament, his lordly mansion In the country, his deer foreeta and game COTPre. Whilat working indirectly to enrich them, men were being crushed under a cruel and degrading slavery. That grim tyrnnny ciPI not only claim its tribute from the hard-earned wages of the artisan and the labourer and indirectly from the food and clothing of their starving wives and families. It exacted itS toll from there who comprised what were known as the upper dresses. It robbed the wisest of their wirdom, the purest of their purity, and the most gently nurtured of their culture and refinement. There was not as individual in that hall amongst whose circle of personal friends, rehstivee or acquaintances there were not to be remembered some poor victims who were being driven to their doom by that over- mastering lust. The prayer 'When wilt non MVO the paople, 0 God of mercy when " had been going rip from numberless hearts through numberless years, and the dey of emancipation would assuredly come. But God depended in some measure for the working out of that deliverance upon human rm nay, upon the loce,sympatily,coorage and earnestness of human hearts. Ile had been reading lately ahout the</p>
        <p>LIOKRATION OF ITALY - from the tyranny of the petty princes and the Church and Rome within and the oppression of foreign nations without by Garibaldi and his brave tend of patriots. Ile could hardly avoid saying in passing that the new Italy which Garibaldi helped to make was hardly in these days proving herself worthy of her great and noble traditions. (Hear, hear.) Ile had been struck on reading that history with the many Points of resemblance it bore to the Scottish war of independ- ence and, indeed to almost every war of independence he had read about—every war that had for its end the breaking down of oppression and the mtablishreent of man's eternal rights of freedom. In both those apecific Instances the armies of the oppressed were infinitely smaller, their reeouroes and equipment far poorer, end their training very me eh leas complete than those at the cppres3or. Victory wits won not by the superiority of the artillery and the engines of war, not by the force of nombets or the power of organization, but by the personal zeal and courage, enthuriaam, earnestneas and self- sacrifice of the individual soldiers. Roth those historic enterprises afford a lesson to those who had joined the standard of Jesus Christ end engaged themselves in His,</p>
        <p>Kidney disease in its worst form comes on almost unnoticed, and often reaches an incurable stage before it is found out.</p>
        <p>That is why it is so serious. You me.; feel as if you have no kidney trouble,—there may be no symptoms except an occasional twinge of pain in the loins and back, a heavy, drowsy feeling, or little watery swellings under the eyes or in the ankles. And yet if the water were chemically tested the kidneys might b?, found to be in a seriously diseased state, calling for long and patient treatment.</p>
        <p>In other patients, however, the symptoms may be more distinct: there may be sharp pains in the back, gravel, urinary disorders, or obstinate dropsy. Uric acid poison, left in the blood by weak kidneys, may settle in the form of sharp jagged crystals in the muscles and</p>
        <p>campaign for the breaking down of the r ',preemie° power and the liberation of the suffering captive. "Our enemy" continued Mr. Chedburn t' is far richer, I otter equipped and organised for the war than we are. It hen far greater and more extensive influence, oven in matters of legislation, than we have and if we depended on such things as those we cou'd never conquer. But we do not. We have something that gives on</p>
        <p>AN IMMEASURAIIICADVAN-TACK over the enemy, in spite of all be posstsses.We have the consciousness in our own souls that no have right and therefore (I rd on our aide,and we are inzpired, enthused and animated by something of which the enemy knows nothing—the greatest forte, the mightiest dynamic in the weo Id—the power of sympathy and love. Ido not want to say a word in depreciation of organization and legislation. I welcome act of government, every step of legislation, every organiza.ron and concrrted movement that lon for its end the curtailment of the tynnt's power. But Ido say that the flnal victory will not he won by organization and logi.lation, but by individual effort, individusl testimony, influence, example and self.sacrillce." (Applause.) . . . .</p>
        <p>Mr 1;-. DODDS, as president ofthrerfland.Of hope Union, proposed a vote of thanks to the chairman and speaker. She sail she felt a better time was coming for the temperance worke-s of Warrington. (I-lear, hear.) They bailed with the greatest delight the coming into the town of a number of new ministers of the Gorpel—men who were not afraid of the frown of sty patron but who were in full heart and sympathy with the temperance came and would raise their voices against the terrible drink evil at every opportunity. One heard a great deal abont heredity and environment, but they brought to those who were hemmed in by those influerces a Gospel that was stronger than either. (Applause'. They rejoiced to think that the old Gospel had I'dl its ancient power. Men and women were being lifted out of hope- if 000053 and helplessness, rut of lives of sin and temp- tation on to the platform of redeemed humenity,with a new song in their mouths, a new hope in their hearts, and one,. purpose in their lives. ;Applause). The vole was heartily accorded and the meeting closed with the hymn' Fight the good 460</p>
