        <head>WARRINGTON WOMAN</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON WOMAN</p>
        <p>Alt s II Lau ton, of I 1,, Wellfield Street, Bank Quay, Wu, rirgum, says :—" I have been troubled with a ntoty languid feeling, and also with pains in the small of my back ; tat the wotst trouble we with the kidney excretions, which were much discrdered.</p>
        <p>"1 lewd Dosn's backache kidney pills and they have given me perfect relief; they cleared and regulated the urinary system, and have driven away the pains in the back, and the constant tiredness in now a great deal better. "Dorm's pills are really splmdid. I've recommeneei a good many prople to try them (S;gned) Mrs. H. Lawton."</p>
        <p>Pearly wan, later, Mrs. Lawton said I hate seen my testimonial in the papers, and ea.-7 word of it is quite true. I have cften advised people to ti y Dean's pills, and I stand by all I have si id for the medicine."</p>
        <p>*/17'' Like Mrs. Lawton had.</p>
        <p>of this mental attitude, but it also keeps down wages because it keeps down results. ' It limits production and therefore limits the satisfaction and enjoyment which arises out of the things produced. We have probably the best artisans in the world, but their best heart and best effort have not been enlisted in creating industrial efficiency and economical production. There are amongst the workers dormant possibili- ties of wealth production, undeveloped faculties, that have not so far been awakened. The British employer and his workers spend too much time and energy in fighting about money and wages, the division of the slander margin: which time and energy would be far better employed if they would together concentrate on the question of output and its value with a view to increase the margin. Workmen should be encouraged to realise their responsibility, in the making of their country's prosperity, and at the same time invited to take an active interest in the success of the undertaking with which they are associated. With this should be some reliable guarantee, a certainty that they will share in the prosperity. The conditions and prejudices (the prejudices as much as the conditions) associated with our industial system do not call forth and encourage the skill, energy, and ingenuity of the workers to any appreciable extent. Employers make ' little intelligent effort, no whole-hearted effort, to secure the co-operation of the thought of their workpeople, and the best activities of the brains of the workers are directed into other channels. Whoever, heard of any Federation of employers, Chamber of Commerce, or like body, seriously setting to work to consider the best means of securing the interest of the workers in its relation to efficiency of production? And whoever heard of any Trade Unions or other workers' associations seriously discussing "how can we best increase the margin out of which our increase of remuneration must come" ? The workman, or master, as the case may be, who proposed such questions for serious con- sideration would be looked upon as a theorist, and the subject side-tracked on the ground that there was only time at such gatherings to discuss what they called "practical " questions. _ .</p>
        <p>And yet what an unworked mine of dexterity there is dormant amongst British workers only waiting to be called forth. The livelihood of all, depends on the products of labour, and the amount and utility of the product depends on the efficiency of labour. One of the most vital problems of the hour is how to switch on this idle power, how to secure the co-operation of the workers in developing British manufactures for the benefit alike of master and man, and for the commonwealth and commonweal. Were it possible to get the thought of those engaged in industrial pursuits sentred in the cheapen- ing of the cost of production, in other words increasing the output without proportionately inereasing the cost as a whole, and in improv- ing the value of the manufactured article, there would be such a surplus to divide that the reward of labour, both for the manage- ment and the manual worker, would be greatly increased. We are slow to realize that the maximum of production means the maximum of reward to all. Too many thoughtlessly adopt the false idea that a maximum of work always produces a maximum of products and therefore a maximum reward. If masters would take the trouble to find out means of encouraging their men to become effective producers, take the same trouble as they do to make effective their inanimate machines, they would find they had a margin worth dividing, and if need be worth quarrelling about. Some may be disposed to say this is ii very well, that probably there is some tru in it, but how is it to be achieved? B have we quite niched the time for practic plans? Before it is worth while discussin plans must there not first be a preparation of mind on both sides? We have plans in abundance, some have succeeded, some have failed, but the latter have failed, not because the plans were bad, but because there has not been the proper spirit to work them. We require to change the attitude of mind in both masters and men. I am sanguine enough to believe that if both sides could realise the value of the interest and brain power of the workers, method would be found to give these ample scope. We talk about the laws of supply and demand; of Protection and I. ree Trade; of the efficiency of machines ; of the power that can be got out of a pound of coal; and many other like economic and practical details,but we neglect the greatest factor of all, the human worker in relation to efficiency of production. A man is more than a machine, can develope greater possibilities, has greater capacities. If he is treated by his employer or indeed treats himself merely as a machine, a thing with a fixed quantity of muscular tissue capable of giving forth in a given time so many units of work, then one of the greatest factors in production is ignored, namely human nature with its unlimited power.</p>
        <p>MOTOR-CAR MURDER. NEW TRIAL REFUSED.</p>
