      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>INDUSTRIAL STRIFE AND INDUSTRIAL EFFICIENCY.</head>
        <p>INDUSTRIAL STRIFE AND INDUSTRIAL EFFICIENCY.</p>
        <p>A MORE EXCELLENT WAY.</p>
        <p>,By Charles E. Parker.)</p>
        <p>In every quarter the noise of industrial strife is heard in the land. There are demands from all sides for increased remuneration for labour, and better condi- tions of life. Amidst the din and conflict a plea for industrial efficiency may be listened to by few, and yet I venture to make it because only through increased efficiency can the reward of toil be permanently and progressively enhanced. We ever have to be reminded that to one likes work, and no one works " for the joy of the working," Rudvard Kipling notwithstanding. The cry we often hear about" making work," and , not alone from Tariff Reformers, contains a great fallacy if work is considered as an end in itself. Work of itself satisfies no need, it is only the product of work that makes it endurable and even pleasant. Put a man on a bicycle and he will go merrily for hours; put him on a treadmill, entailing the same output in labour, and he will be dead beat in twenty minutes. On a bicycle he gets some- where ;on a treadmill no gets nothing done, gets nowhere. Political economy in so far as it limits itself to impersonal forces and laws is good and useful, for every depart- ment of life is subject to law, and the , individual or community who knows not the laws, or knowing them obeys them not, pays the penalty, but economics if so limited cover the least important ground in the field of human activity. In considering work and its reward far more attention should be given to such things as motive influenc- ing the interest, spirit, and zest of the worker. The demand made on all sides for a fuller share of the good things produced by industry is met by the manu- facturing masters by the general response that their profits do not admit of any appreciable increase of wages, because such will add to the cost of production and put them out of the market. This reply in many cases may be perfectly honest. If the cost of production were appreciably increased many industries would have to close their works, but it does not necessarily follow that if wages were increased costs would of necessity rise. But the point I want to get at is that there is a great amount of latent intelligence among the workpeople themselves that our present system of industrial adjustment and remuneration does not call forth, and which if called forth, without any proportionate, or indeed any actual increase in physical exertion, would add immensely to the value of the output from which the reward of both capital and labour must come.</p>
        <p>GRIT IN THE INDUSTRIAL MACHINE, I spoke of the man on the cycle and the man on the treadmill, the former giving forth effort cheerfully without exhaustion solely becauv, of the mental effect of accomplishing something in contrast to the negative result and consequent exhaustion of the man on the treadmill. But the result of the work and the effect on the cycle rider, physical or mental, may vary much under different conditions. Suppose the cycle is out of order, the driving chain dirty, wants oil, and is full of grit ; then the effect of the exertion approaches that of the man on the treadmill. If the rider wants with comfort to get any- where, he must clean and oil his chain. Useless work dwarfs the intellect and deadens the spirit of the worker and any effort which relieves the strain of life and work is produc- tive. Now it is in the direction suggested by this illustration that industrial efficiency must be attained. Instead of increasing mental and physical strain we want to diminish it, , and at the same time to accomplish more. We require every link of the chain cleaned , and oiled,—the grit which at present makes , the strain of driving so heavy taken out. It may be argued that this is nothing new, that it is our constant habit, one of our instincts, to reduce labour in every way we can ; we are all on the look out for means to increase the product of our effort, and at the same time to diminish the effort. This is doubtless true as a general rule and where the product is intimately related to the effort. But in our industrial system in the , mind of the ordinary workers, and in the fact, the effort and its result are not closely enough related. In the wage system there is little direct or proportionate relation between the work and its product. There is no palpable inducement for the individual to , take the grit out of his own link of the chain. Work then becomes to him an end in itself, and this is the secret of the decay of indus- trial energy in so many workers. The worker having given forth a certain amount of effort (a considerable proportion of which may be spent in overcoming the effect of the grit), considers he has done his part, done all he ought, and wonders why his share of the total result is not greater, forgetting that so large a proportion of his effort produces no result, is wasted in friction caused by the grit that ought not to be there. His business he takes it is not to think about grit ; he accepts without question the idea that in the division of labour, he has to do his share in the driving, and the thought which would bring about the removal of the grit belongs to the. boss or his deputy. Nor is he altogether to blame. The conditions of our manufacturing industries favour the adoption</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>" NONE SO DEAF," Ac.</p>
        <p>At the Mayoial banquet here the other night his Worship of Widnes sled " There are questions put sometimes, I think it is wise not to hear." In the days of the old School Boards the supereession of which I for one have not ceased to regret— a deaf clergyman was chosen as chairman of one whose meetings I attended pretty regulerly. At the close of the piweedings, I vetetueed, putting my mouth to his ear, to shout my c engratulstions, adding that I feared his physical disability would prove a heavy hat dicap. Ab, tut, my dear fellow," raid he, " have there not been occasions in this place when deafners on the part of the chairman would have been a great advan- t ege? In the heat of debate, thirgi are no netimes uttered which it were better for one in this position ne Ito hear. When an,' thing of that sort happens in the future, depend upon it I shall be very deaf indeed' Sa long as we have pain railing, I think y. u will find that toy affliction will not be nearly so severe, even it be notceatile." And it was so. The rev, gentleman, now Canon 'is apparently as deaf, and certainly as sly, as ever. THE ' MIAS.</p>
        <p>The Mayor of the borough which he kimself admitted to have been described as the black ep.t on the Mersey, claimed that Widnes bead gardener had neverthele.s secured a prize at Liverpool for chrysan- therr.ums,but several of those thus left to infer that the suceeseful productions in question were borne grown. promptly denied the possibility, and even went so far as to suggest that the " mums " had been " grown inside." However, that is by no means " the only way." I have an acquaintance who specialiixs in a similar line, and generally comes out well at tie leading floral exhibi ions in the North. In view of one imputant show, he had prepared for corn- petiti n a particulaily brilliant ep.cimen which he was assured would secure a first, hut, ta his dismay, the bloom was accidentally kirpked ar within 48 Inure cf the time alp,intcd. His gardener sent an earnest message to a firm in the South and obtained by return a " bloom " equal if not superior to the one inadvertently destroyed, and " faked " it no skilfully that the experts were completely deceived, and the gross offender was awarded the coveted prize! By-the-way, it is interesting to note that at so advanced a stage of the function above referred to, at least cne distirguished speaker was not afraid to tackle so trying a word as ehrysanthemuu s. HALF A CENTURY IN HARNF...Ii.S.</p>
        <p>And so the Rev. John Yonge, pastor of the Wycliffe Ccogregational Clutch, is in the 50th year of bin ministerial career ! Ilia present charge, from which ere long he will soon voluntarily withdraw for bonrurnble and well-earned rest, he hos held for the protracted period of 35 years. is an unusually long pastorate. Personally, I have known it equalled by only two Nonconformist miniatere—Baptist. Curiously enough, they were brothers, and one of them, after retiring on accatot of impaired health, in course of time sufficiently recovered strength to accept a call to a Lancashire Church and is again I ard at work. Although thoroughly broken down L,r a while, he now bids fair to reach Mr. Yonge's record This is just double that of the average police term of service. I have not met more than one police officer who has topped the half ceotnry. I refer to the late Mr. Edwin Fearnside. When I made his acquaintance he was Chief Coostab!e of Pontefract, in the West Riding and I understood that he had filled that office for some 33 years. Fawn yearn prior to that, he had joined the Lancashire Grittily Constabulary, ' therefore Ire had completed 40 years' service when the Iced] authorities decided to disband their borough force and arranged to be policed by the county. Mr. Fearnside might immediately have retired on a substantial pension, but he preferred the alternative of a county superintendency, and con- tinued in roonection therewith for ten more strenuous years. What a tale either parson Cr " bobby "who has been no long at work cr old unfold ! The late Rey. Charles Garrett, the vererable Wesleyan minister whose memory Liverpool in particular may be trusted to keep green, need to have a popular lecture entitled " The sky is brightening," in which be shooed at what an extraordinary rate the country had 1 progreseed in all respects during the latter half of the nineteenth century, and Mr. Yonge's story of the conditions obtaining at the outset of his ministerial life would be worth the telling. ESPERANTO.</p>
        <p>Like the reinstated butler of the Pharaoh who exalted the ill-used Joseph to his right hand, I do remember shy faults this day. However, according to the proverb, it's never too late to mend. lam reminded of my limitations !Tan account of thedoings a repreventaiive gatheiing of Esperantists at War- rington a little over a week ago- which when I beheld, as Banyan bath it, I did wish myself among them Nay, more, I actually happened up' n a moat entl usie antic section of such while travelling by rail the other night. Every man Jack and woman Jill of them was psi adly adorned with the attractive emblem of the " Rare lingro," and I was puzzled when one whom I suspected of leadership covered the little green token with Lis hand. The reason, however, was soon apparent to a man—and woman—these people are zealous missionalies, and the leader in our compatt- ment speedily got to work. Said the one who had concealed his tail-tale little ornament, " What are tilt se things you good folk are westing ? What does all this display mean? Tell on about it ?" Of course, I and the other children of darkness who were with me pricked our ears, and the Eaperantists made the most of the opportunity to enlighten us. They imparted a good deal of useful information which I shall preserve for future reference, and finally threw out a kind of challerge as to the ease and rapidity with which any person of ordirary intelligence might acquite a working knowledge of this wonderful international language. I was so impressed that, stricken in years though I be,l there and then revolved to " have a shot at it." Having little else to do this week I will devote the next few days toss examina- tion of the system, and will duly report progress. WARRINGTON SHAKESPEARE SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>Abbtugh the one with which I was previously associated ended ignominiously if not disastrourdy (we went in fur two pubbe performances in the reason, and the same set out of a membership of 70 always wanted the leading parts), I was delighted with the resolution to form a Shakespeare Society here—especially as the promoters are modest and their programmes not too ambitious. Evidently their object is not anything in the nature of self-nlorifica- tion, but the encouragement of serious study of the plays of cur great national poet—not amusement merely, but education. They hare certainly taken a judicious step and have the right to expect labile appreciation and practical sympstby.</p>
        <p>PEDESTRIANISM. Mark All, of whom I wrote in thia column when Schilling rased through Warrington, called upon me last week in the course of a walk n.tind the 13ritieh Isles, which he in t) traverse twice this trip. Ho has undertaken this 10,000 mile tour for a wager of £3OO offered Ly " Health and Strength." Ho started on the 20th ult, from the G.P.0., London, and an he in to complete the walk in 200 &amp;ye, he will need to main- tain an average of 50 milee per day. BEN TROVATO.</p>
        <p>One evening last week I was present at a largely- attend( d meeting under the auspices of tho Nattonal Union of Women's Suffrage Societica, the principd speakers at which were Mrs. Philip Snowden, whose husband, the Member of Parliament, writes the weekly Labour al tide for the" Examiner," and Miss Margaret I24bertson, B.A. Ihe laver stated that not very long ago a gentleman, coming out of • polling booth, to'd her that he had voted for "traffic riform," and when she asked him why, he said "I do ba'is those motor cars so." Another man, equally pleased with himself, said he had voted for Free Trade and Protection. Close to the same polling booth two men were looking at • poster advising them to vote for the Budget, and one said to the other "Partington I know, and Profumo I know, but who is this Budget they they are asking us to vote for ?"</p>
        <p>EMBARRASSING. In last week's" Nt tea and Notions " the awkward proximity of certain significant items on • local Band of Hope programme was commented upon. We have all known of such things. For instance siren there is an unusually long-minded person in the pulpit, the solo is pretty certain to be "It is enough," and the choir will follow with " Let us go," or something not less appropriate. Over many years I attended certain Parish Church organ recitals given after evening service OD the first Sunday of each month throughout the in inter, and the vicar invariably closed try announc- ing " Ptaise God from shorn all blessings flow." The funny eide of the rii nation iriuld obtrude itself, and I was glad that it struck the succeeding vicar in like mariner, and we were no longer called upon to give thanks, as it were, that we had beta brought safely through another programme, and that we had got to the end of it. Our former vicar lacked one thing—like the brother cleric sho caused to be inscribed over ths aide entrance to his church : "This is the house of God, this is the gate of heaven. Please go round the other way." To revert for a moment to I the " urconecious humour" of " paragraphists." Once, as sub editor of a country weekly, I received from one of these an item to the effect theta child had tumb'ed into a shallow well by the roadside, and the writer added—" If be had fallen into the deeper one a little lower down on the other aide of the toad, the effects would co doubt have been more fences."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>BABIES BRONCHITIS and Terrib'e Hacking Cough Cured by VENG'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE.</head>
        <p>BABIES BRONCHITIS and Terrib'e Hacking Cough Cured by VENG'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. J. Fick, 07, Westfield Road, East Smiths's, Portsmouth. a Bandmaster in the Royal Navy, writes: "My little boy (11 months old), had a severe attack of bronchitis, midi a terrible hacking cough. He was very sick and ia awful agony, hie Uice ine purple as he crashed, and the many medicines we tried did no goad. Then I gave him Veno's, and although the bottle is only hal empty the lad is an right as rain. I would go before any magistrate and swear on oath that my boy was completely cured by Veno's Lightning Cough Cure, and that medicine alone." Veno's Lightning Cough Cure was awarded the Grand Prit and Gold Medal at the recent Paris Health Exhibition, it is safe ter the 3oungeet child, and is the ideal remedy for all diseases of cheat, lenge end throat. Pros 91d., 1/11 and 2/9 of all chemists.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>DEATH IN CUSTODY.</head>
        <p>DEATH IN CUSTODY.</p>
        <p>Wigan Football Enthusiast's Sad End</p>
        <p>Singular evidence wars forthcoming at an inquest held at the Police Station on Monday by Coroner S Brighouse. The deceased wee an unknown men who was in the custody of the Borough police on • chsrgo of drunkenness.</p>
        <p>When charged it appears that the roan gave the name of John Barry and stated that he had corne from Wigan to lee the football match.</p>
        <p>It was gathered from the evidence that about 10.10 no Saturday night P.C. Bowlful whilst on duty in Dellam-lace noticed a men lying on the footpath with his lack against the brewery wall and shouting " I will flgt,any man in Warrington." The officer assiated him to hie feet end noticed that be was drtink. Ile told the man to go home but when Le had walked about lets yards he fell down and was unable to get up. The constable then obtained the assistance of a civilian named Arthur James Bouchier and they conveyed deceased to the Police Station on a handcart. Ile wan placed in a cell, but instead of putting him on the bench the officer allowed him to remain an the floor for safely. During the night he was several times visited by Sergeant Ilesp and he then appeared to be in a drunken sleep. Upon a further visit at 8.10 on Sunday morning the Sergeant noticed that the men was froth- ing at the rut uth. Dr. Smith wan at once telephoned for and upon him snivel ordered the deceased', removal to the Workhouse Hospital, where he died at 7 30 the following morning, without recovering 000nCiounneal. 'l'ie Wigan police wcre communicated with, but up to the time of the Inquest deceased had not been Identified. Mary Robinson, • widow, 22, Portland-street raid that about a quarter to ten on Saturday night whilst in Tenners-lane, she saw the deceased lying on the ground Another man was trying to lift him up. She went to hie assistance end noticed that both were drunk while the deceased acid in a joking manner that his legs were drunk.</p>
        <p>Dr. Taylor, house sorgeon at the Workbouge hospital stated that when the deceaaed was admitted be was unconscious and showed signs of cerebrial compression. Ile seemed to get worse and an operation was per- formed. He then slightly improved but later relapsed and died it 7.306 in. In his opinion death was due to the compreask n of a blood clot on the brain, caused by a fracture. It was quite easy for the fracture to have teen ristained by his falling on the back of his head.</p>
        <p>P.C. Rowlee said that the deceased htd to be held down on the handcart when being conveyed to the Police Station.</p>
        <p>The jury returned • verdict that death was doe to hemorrhage on the brim due to a fractured skull but there was no evidence to show:bow such fracture ray brought about. Tbe following in a description of the decemed : About 35 3ear., of age, height :ift. Sins., clean 'haven and dark hair, three false teeth at the front and two at the aide, in working clothes with • collar and tie. The following tattoo marks were also found on the body : Two birds, Union Jack, Stars and Stripe., lightship, thistle, rose and other flowers, on the chest, • ballett girl and a heart with a bond of friendship on ties right arm, soda sold Cr, snake charmer and sashes heart and dagger on the left arm.</p>
        <p>MORE HEMLOCK! (The Adult Suffrage Bill, which Mr. Asquith has promised to bring forward next yenr. will not only widen the franchise by sweeping away many iniquitous disqualifications which at present rob the workers of (heir right to vote, but will completely abolish, once for all, the plural voter.)</p>
        <p>pp . ,</p>
        <p>century which really saw the clearacce of a number of vital questions. At the beginning of it there was a band of men of genius, the grratest critics, perhaps, who had ever written in English—Cole, idge, Charles Lamb, Hazlitt, and de Qnincey who had left creative work concerning Shakespeare. The enthusiastic student however, should not over-do himself with critics, neither should be neglect them. A few critics, and those of the hest—creative writers themselves —were the best companions. The study of Shakespeare in England was really started about 100 years age by the pubic lectures of Samuel Tay IJr Coleridge and William llazlitt, b..,th of them princely critics. Their works were inspired interpretations which might be very readily obtained. In the Viet wian times also there were admirable critics, and in addition they might regard it as the age of scholarship. The questions those men of genius had more or less to decide were n,w tackled with immense cars and research by a band of scholars—such matters, for instance, as the probable order in which the plays were written and the light they threw on the growth of the poet's mind. All that was the patient work of a generation and of a syndicate of sch, lars who pieced the evidence. Frederick James Furnival, who died last year at the age of eighty, gave much of his best to the elucidation of those questions. No man better illustrated the fact that learning and life were not separate things. He was a man of the keenest vitality, of Shakespearian character, and learning in his hands bore all ita graces. Those who intended to to take the shyly of Shakespea,e seriously must realise that he was</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>SHAKESPEARE SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>SHAKESPEARE SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>SUCCESSFUL INAUGURAL MEETING.</p>
        <p>Professor Elton's Hints to Students.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Shakespeare Society was launched on its career en Tuesday evening under the happiest auguries. Seldom has the founding of a new society aroused ouch keen interest as is 'being taken in this effort to encourage the study of the works cf the immortal bard, and the Council Chamber at the Town Hall proved quite inadequate to accommodate all who wished to attend the inaugural meeting. The room was uncomfortably crowded and even then numbers were unable to gain admission. As Professor Elton, the principal spaker, wittily put it, the audience was large enough to represent the whole population of Shakespeare's plays. The new society has found an admirable president in the Rev. Canon Morley Stevenson and the committee cons swot Dr. Law, Dr. Haden, Messrs. J. Dolby, It. Garnett, E. J. Hall, 0. Lofthowse, J. Moore Murray, ' and W H Woodcock. Mr. 11. Grosvenor (Union Rank) is the Iron. treasurer, and the duties of hon, secretaries are being undertaken by Mr. F. S. Rossiter, York Drive, Grappenhall, and Mr. It. Madeley, Museum, Warrington. The monthly meetings are to be devoted to the reading of papers on Shakespearian subjects, to discussions on the same, and to readings from the works. The inaugural meeting was presided over by the Mayor (Councillor Dr. Joseph) who was supported by Professor Elton, professor of English literature in the University ol Li mrpool, and the Rev. Canon Stevenson. . .</p>
        <p>The MAYOR, in an introductory address, referred to the crowded state of the room as an argument for the enlargEment of the Council Chamber which be hoped to see carried cut during his Mayoralty. (Hear, hear.) l'roceeding to speak of Shakespeare, he s3id his writings exposed to view every phase of human thought and emotion—joy and sorrow, satire, wit, humour, and moral and mental philosophy of the deeps at and meat abstruse kind. Marl' of his sayings had become household words to guide us in the various phases of our every day life real commonsense and logic such as we required in order to avoid errors and choose the right path in whatever pursuit we might be engaged. Shakespeare had given vent to the utmcst thoughts and feelings not only of one nation but of the whole world. ' Supposing a m3n in any country, civilized or uncivilized, were to read some quotations from Shakespeare he would say in his mind and heart: " I Just thought that but I could not express it." Shakespeare seemed to have known the inmost recesses not only of the civilized mind but of the intelligent savage mind. There had been many discussions as to a hether Hamlet was really insane or feigning insanity. Physicians, logicians and pecychologista had argued the subject but had never arrived at a conclusion, which showed how deep and mystic Shakespeare's writings were. Much had been said on the subject of the</p>
        <p>Atioonsnir of VIE PLAYS AND- POEMS.</p>
        <p>Some considered that Bacon wrote them. It did not matter whether he did or not. (lAughter). It was true flea Shakespeare, as they knew him, did not seem to trace been the kind of man to produce such transcend- ent works of genius. It was marvellous that a strolling ne'er-do-well actor, who was not of well-regulated habits and might have been considered a mere mountebank in these days, should have risen to such heights and have beceme the greatest man who ever lived. (Applause). It was a lesson to us that we often received what we did not expect and might be entertaing angels unawares. Although he seemed to be a very obscure man the many instances in history of men rising superior to their staion and environment showed that we had no need to doubt that such a wonderful achievement was possible in Shakespeare'. case. His Worship concluded by stating that he had very great enthusiasm and admiration for the s-ciety, and he hoped- to do his utmost to support it. (Applause). Professor ELTON delivered a most interesting address full of round advice to students of Shakes- peare. He eaid he took it that in that society one had to deal with a mass of honest, genuine and informed enthusiasm to begin with, though necessarity with various grades and degrees ii special knowledge. The difficulty confronting those who sought to direct . . ,</p>
        <p>the study of Sliakesp7are along the— right lines increased every year. One thing that struck cue on the very threshold, if one were at all concerned as a reader or writer with these matters, was the</p>
        <p>ENORMOUS MAsS OF MATERIAL,</p>
        <p>leaned and unlearned, wise and fooli,h, ant had been collected and was still accumulating like a snowball— the material of Shakespearian lore. If they went into . . . . .</p>
        <p>the great memorial liLary at Stratford-Oil-Avon they would see the shelves piled to the ceiling with books on or editionslof Shskespeare—an amount of know- ledge with which no human mind, not eves the most desperate specialist who worked fifteen hours a day, could possibly hope to cope. What was to be done with all that, and how far were they to allow them- selves to beat Shakespeare as a matter cf lore— net simply as a means of enjoyment to all cultivated Femme. How far were they to treat him as somethirg which the professors, editors and the like had made their prey? How far were they to study him in the ordinary sense of the term? During the nineteenth century the mass of Shakes- perian learning became very great. It was that</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>NOT AN EASY_ AUTHOR.</head>
        <p>NOT AN EASY_ AUTHOR.</p>
        <p>He eeerned to ha;;Written, almost as if in malice, in a double spirit. He wrote what in its general lines must be perfectly well followed by any audiecce. The pathos, power, tragedy or comedy of each work was apparent. But he also filled in between those lines in a fashion which would always task the commentators. His very English, though it often seemed ours, was not ours at all. Shakespeare required a dictionary, almost as much as Virgil or Chaucer, though we were hardly accustomed to think so. I.?arned Germ ms had given their lives to making dictionaries of Shakespeare end had thought themselves well rewarded. Shakes- peare was much harder to understand verbally than the Old Testament or than other English author of equal note. " I believe myself," continued the pro- fessor, " that the habit of having him drummed into us at too early an age, being forced to learn him by heart, is a kind of</p>
        <p>PAR tLYSIS OF THE MIND, and prevents us from understanding him. The art of mere learning posip-nes for a long time the true understinding of the author. Many people who can reel off Hamlet's soliloquies have extremely little notion what they are about. (Laughter.) It is all due to early misguidance or to teaching without comprehension." Continuing, the Professor advised his hearers to secure an edition of Shakespeare with explanations, such as that which Professor Herford had compiled. Other works which he recommended were Professor Cunliff's Shakespeare Dictionary and the " Leopold " Shakespeare. In addition to personal study, he took it that the society would encourage some kind of concerted wot kin groups Shakespeare " leading "—a rather ambltsuous term, because there were so many ways of doiog it—would no doubt play its port. There was one way which might make fcr considerable weariness. He did not think the mete gathering together of a band of friends, the more or less chance allotting of parts, and read- ing round without much thought or prep tration, though pleasant and good in its way, was not the most that could be made of the matter. There should be a certain amount of agreed-upon discipline. In an affair of that kind he did not believe in its being left to drawing atraws for the allotment of porta. (Laughter.) Nor should it be done by a vote or plebiscite. He did not believe in those democratic principles when art came into the question. (Laughter.) He believed in tyranny in that ca-s. (Laughter.) The best way was for each group to select a tyrant, by common consent, and agree to obey his or still more her orders. (Laughter.) The allotment of parts should be done on rigid principles, without any regard to the age, sex or importance of the persons concerned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>TIIE MUNI</head>
        <p>TIIE MUNI</p>
        <p>should test the tei;,- exactly as if they were to engage in some sport, and appropriate them as well aspossible to tl.eir respective parts. They might have an elderly and important person who was quite inappropriately cast for light comedy, whereas he or she would be excellent in the part of heavy tragedy, such as Hamlet's father or mother. (Laughter.) On the other hand, they might have an extremely youthful reader, whose line was p'easant pastoral characters, cast for the part of the third murderer. (Laughter.) Such readings, which had been a source of pleasure to many students, were only really effective and delightful with a great deal of bald, formal Ir.utice. 'lf you were w. ming for ordinary private t' eatricals," cam- mented the Professor " for some third-rate farce or drawing-room comedy, you would take an enormous amount of trouble to get it right. And why not for Shakespeare ? I am not suggr sing the acting of parts in costume, but the rime reading, the utterance of the thing and the bringing it home. If you eau get an instructor who will tra'n the voice and speech quite brutally,—as thoroughly as the coach of a football team—you will profit by it in the end." The speaker next addressed himself tl the suitable themes for and scope of papers to be read to a Shakespoare Society:, Questions had arisen in holes aid corners of litera- ture which could no longer be considered obscure, as to the authorship of Shakespeare's plays. He did not want to waste their time by talking about the</p>
        <p>BACONIAX- THEORY-</p>
        <p>but since it had been mentioned, he would Fay one</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>From Reynolds' Newspaper.</head>
        <p>From Reynolds' Newspaper.</p>
        <p>hing, regardless of the feelings of ar.y possible supportr of it. A number of people, some of them quite well known in du ir respective calling., had advocated the view that the dramatist who wrote the plays and the man Shakespeare of whom we had a number of definite records were not the earns person. They went on to say that Bacon was the author. All he would say was that thee was nobody at all who was an English scholar, who was respected at y where by anyone who understood English scholarship, who believed that the notion was any- thing bat the m st absolute ruhbi dr. (Applause.) 1' he topic, however, wculd trot be a bad one for dis- cussion in the soci, ty. The actual evidence would takes long time to recite but it was abs lutely con- clusive. There could be no better exercise than to find the foundation of the faith that was in them. But as a serious matter they might entirely dismiss such a supposition, just as they would dismiss a supposition that the works of Tennyson were -written by the Lard Chancellor of his day. (laughter) The study of Shakespeare bad fallen from time to time into the hands of many who were only t chnically sane. (Laughter.) kl.a.y lunatics a: d cranks were at large —(laughter)— and the literature emanating from such sources was larger than one liked to think. So great a body of work and 60 immense an intellect as Shakepeare's seemed to have the power of attracting fantastic theories of all kinds and extremes. For instancn, these was a mass of literatu,e which aimed at bring- ing Shakespeare into some special profession or particular class of tenets or belief. It was amazing what an extraordinary pyramid of egg shells could be built up in that way, and be hoped the sochty would , not he led away by such a " will-o'-th'-wisp." The commonest fault of the commentator, which they must avoid, was to built up a great edifies on something that was not emphased in any way in the text of the plays. To inquire what Hamlet had been doing or reading before the play began was mere midsummer madness. (Laughter). The professor concluded by ladvising his hearers not to read Shakespeare as the prey of the teacher or the professor, as the sayer cl hard se ings or the provider of a doubtful livelihood for dictionary makers - (launhter)- but to approach him with true eothusialn and enjoyment, regardmg him as " the p.ioce of all, the wonder the world." (Applause). The Rev. CANON Srta ENSON, who was cordially received, ssid the promoters of the meeting did not exp-ct such a splendid result as that which was seen [ that evening. Why had they not formed a society before ? (Hear, hear and applause). He had lived in Warrington over 30 years and it seemed that their lives had been partly wasted. They might have had 30 years of Shakespeare. (Laughter). Instead they were only just neginning. The meeting carried him back in memory to the little Shakesperian revival they had many years ago in Warrington. A course of lectures was given by Pr ,fessor Moulton and some of those present would no doubt recollect , the very energetic, graphic and dramatic ' way in which he declaimed passages from the works Since then there had been no local effort of a , Shakespearian character until within the last few Imonths. Theo, largely owing to the coming to War- rington of a gentleman who was an enthusiastic istudent and interpreter of Shakespeare, they bad a I very remarkable performanco of " Macbeth." (Applause.) That rerformance, for w hich they were largely indebted to Dr. Law—(applause) —was a great step in the rekindling of an interest in Shaken- pa ire in the town and it had led to the formation of that soc'ety. Its regarded the society in the light of a remarkably fine child, having every sign of health and vigour and with proper nourishment (laughter) - there wan no reason why it should n t do well. (Applause.) His experience of societies, how- ever, was that the rate of infantile mortality in them was very great. (Laughter.) Although they started well, the difficulty came not so much at the birth of the child as when he got two or three years old. The task before the com- mittee of the society would be to keep up the interest and enthusiasm which had undoubtedly been kindled and to give the members such a bill of fare and such helpful meetings and good performances as would keep alive the earaestnets of which they bad had a Imre that evening. He hoped their study of Shakespeare would lead them into some of the</p>
        <p>MORE UNTRODDEN PATHS</p>
        <p>of the poet and dramatist. There were certain plays which were very famili to them all. They had had to learn them at school and some of them, for their sins, had had to teach them to other peOple—(laughter) —and to listen to recitatiodh from people more or less capable of reciting. (Laughter.) They bad borne a great deal which was purgatorial in connection with Shakespeare. (Laughter.) On the other hand they had had the pleasure of seeing excellent representa- tions of cent tin plays but, as far as his experience went there were certain plays which they constantly saw upon the stage, whilst others were hardly ever staged at all. It was natural that p.ople should mostly study wad read those plays which were familiar to them, and which were the great masterpieces of the great master. But a society of that kind afforded a great opportunity to explore some of the lees-known plays which surely had their beauty, and well repaid all the study that could be given to them. They had all seen, beard, read, recited and studied " The Tempest," but which of them could say the same of " Love's labour lost"? Personally, he had never seen it on the stage. With regard to the method of study, he hoped they would not make it too much in the direction of a dry scholastic business. It was quite possible to find out the Dumber of lines in a play that had got a syllable more than the laws of meter said they ought to have. (Laughter) That was an cccupation for a wet day (Laughter) He was not decrying that kind of thing, for it had its legitimate use for the learned, thorough</p>
        <p>student of Shakespeare. He was, however, referring to thee who, like himself, did not pretend to be more than ordinary readers. He felt anxious they ought to try to get hold, as much as possible, of the real depth of the beauty and wisdom of the great poet—(applause, —and see for themselves something of the dramatic power which lay behind it all. Trey should get some- thing of that wonderful spirit whim inspired the youth brought up with what, in these days, we should not consider a great edurati.n, with no very greatt surroundings, but still brought up iii th a lovely Warwickshire country, breathing in from his very birth the beauties rf its woods, its river and its peaceful scenery, and sur- rounded by those great historio buildings—such as Kenilworth and Warwick Castles—which no-roe could look at without feelings of vide and veneration If they could catch something of the true spirit of poetry and the charna which inspired Shakespeare, they would drink at the fountain head and fill their own orbits with much tlrit was great, wise and almost Divine. (Applause) . . .</p>
        <p>, A choice programVse of Moat appropriate music was submitted by a party of vocalists from Walton under the direction of the Rev. L. J. T. airwall. The glees "Tle ch ud-capt towers " (Sterena) end As it fell upon a day " (Lord Mornington) were given in , sparkling et. le and the part song for male voices " Full fathom live " (Charles Wood) received a beautiful interpietstion. Miss Dorothy Tmslove delighted the audience with '' Orpheus and his lute" (Sulliran) and " No longer mourn for roe " (Dr. Corp) ' which were treated in an anistic and expressive manner. Mr. Greenough sang' Under the greenwood tree ". (Dr. A roe) very ph a&amp;rNly.. Mr. Freak "tooth</p>
        <p>lay( d tlie accomPangnents u0u..1 lade. . . .</p>
        <p>Mr. W. 11. Wooticncs prop 'sod a vote of thanks to the Mayor, Profeesor El on, the Rev. L J. T. Darwall and his mu-ical friends, and the Rev. Canon Stevenson. He said Professor Elton's address would help tbe members of the mxiety in their study of the, evolks of the world's greatest writer and dramatist. It had been very suggestive and interesting and the memory of it would be one of the blight sr° s in tht ir thoughts of that evening's pr, ceedings. 'they were gr atly indebted to the Mayor for Lis astistsnee in the f rroation of the society. (Hear, hear ) They were inierd fortunate in lining secured the services of Canon Stevenson as their president. (Applause.) His long experience and well-known kindness would enable them to steer eh, ir ship successfulfy along her pathway. (Applause) The audiecce had thoroughly et Jared the renderirg of the Shafer spearian music by by Mr. Darwall's rarty. (Applause). Dr. LAW seconded ard the vote was heartily accorded.</p>
        <p>The MAI, n and the Pev J. T. DARWALL responded</p>
