      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>BOOK PICTURES AND PICTURE BOOKS</head>
        <p>BOOK PICTURES AND PICTURE BOOKS</p>
        <p>A lecture entitled " Book Pictures and Picture Books" was delivered in the Masonic Hall on Satur- day evening, this being the first of a series to be delivered during the winter tension, under the oust ices of the Ogden Trustees. A sum of money was left to the Minchest.r Corporation by Mr. Henry Og leo for the purpose of encouraging the love of natute and the study of art and the Warrington Museum Committee have availed themselves of the lectures arranged in accordance with his bequest. The lecturer on Saturday evening was Mr. Henry Cadness, and the chair was occupied by Alderman Bennett, the Chairman of the Museum Committee. The popularity these lectures attained last year was no doubt rre,aonsible for the large attendance on Saturday evening. The Chairman said that it was not encouraging to find a good book spoiled by illustrations which were not conceived or executed in the right spirit. With appropriate illustratio a the interest of a hick was greatly increased. One of the illustrators of Dickens was trained at the Warrington Art School while one of the chief characters in a book of the same author was a Warringtonian. The town had every reason to be proud of its high standard in the world of art and he hoped that position would be retained. (Applause) The lecturer slid in one form or another boots had ever helped in man's advancement, from remote times they had been one of the greatest factors in civilization. At whatever period they were produced they necessarily reflected the capacities of their authors and habits and customs of their times. It was impossible to imagine a world without be.oks. From the earliest times man lisel commanicated web his fellowmen by means of sounds and signs and symb le As one means of these expresrions, nature forms were -rudely chased on h ine or stone developing later into the most magnificent senlyure, from which lOU ce_ we were indebted for the greater part of our know ledge of prehistoric times. The very letters of our alphabet could he traced to the forms of natural objects which originally were used to symbolise sounds or ideas and later were simplified to facilitate rapidity of expression. Ideaography, or drawn ideas, give the first form of book pictures, not to explain the text, but to form the text itself. The objects thus depicted were rendered as the worker knows them to he and not as they would appear to the trained eye, but in all cases they gave a plain statement of facts no strongly emphasised that they had proved mach more useful in modern research than the most beautiful pictorial treatments would have been. For • greater convenience man at a very early age invented a portable means of con- veying Iris ideas in graphic form. Bones were incieed, tablets of wood were painted, plates of metal were scratched, and tablets of ivory were coated with wax A. series of leaves binged together with wire gave the forerunner of our modern leaf hook. The bark and the leaves of trees were also used and the leaves of the papyrus were so pasted across each other as to form a long scroll, while skins of animals were als prepared to give a smooth white ground on which pan and bush gave c dour pictures. Vellum skins • from the calf and parchment from the sheep were used ' in the Ith century, these being still used for the highest class of printing and far illuminated manu- scripts. About the Uth century, however, paper made of cotton pulp checked the use of parchment and in • the 13. h century linen paper had so established itse f I 98 to be quite common. Since then pulp paper has I teen in constant use but in recent years very smooth faced papers, enabling fine printing, bad so introduced an impermanent character that it was thought it . would have a destructive effect in the c urse of a , t century or so. Boobs were first produced in two ' forms, namely, a long scroll rolled on a rod and t rectangular leaves folded and bound together. In the: early treatment of books, not only the pictures but the I letters were executed by hand. This was followed by I the stamping of blocks, in which the wholepage was cot on the grain side of the wood, the picture being reversed so that it would be right when printed. Then came the arranging of separate types of letters to I form words. The type process had a great rival in copper plate engraving. The plate was engraved or ) etched with tool and acids to give sunken lines which I were filled with a transparent dark colour and the 6 surface being cleaned, so that when the paper was applied and pressed the ink in the lines was transferred , in varying depths of tones, according to the depths of - the etching. In the early part of last century t lithography or colour printing from stone, which bad an affinity for either grease or water, was developed. . This lithography was used chiefly for colour work and drawings. Photographic processes for the production I of book illustration had given a great impetus not . only in i line work but also n tone and colour. Three 7 colour printing was produced by means of three blocks r each representing the quantity of either red, yellow, t or blue, as the case might be, the three blocks being , painted over each ether giving a mixture of three colours. Referring to the subjects (.1 illustrations the lecturer said that it was inteiesting to see how much was being done for the benefit of the child in I this regret, in many cases the life of the child had r been so lighted up that the glow bad been retained throughout its life. Various illustrations were thrown on the screen . depicting the ideas of pre-historic communication and the gradual advance to the present advanced form of , colour printing. The lantern was manipulated lay Mr. , Jones, of Hill and Co., Bat terrnarket-street.</p>
      </div>
