      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>PREMIER AND MANHOOD SUFFRAGE</head>
        <p>PREMIER AND MANHOOD SUFFRAGE</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons Mr. Asquith (answering several questions on his forccast of ebctoral reform , said :—I am responsible, and the Government are responsible, only for whit I actually ssid. I repeated what I have said publicly on several occasions during the last two years that there is, in my opinion, urgent need for such a reform of our franchise law as will substitute for the present complicated and illogic ii network of suffrages a simple residential qualification. I added—and this was the only new thing in my Mate- o out—that the Government hopei to introduce proposals neat year. Legislation on the subject is overdue, and its urgency is increased by recent decisions which on purely technical grounds, have disfranchised, and ate disfranchising, thousands of i electors. It s obvious that I cannot at thls stage, as asked on several of these questions, anticipate details. In my opinion, any large reconstruction of slit franchise law must be followed, as a logical corollary, by an equitable redistribution of representation. , (Gapers! cheery.) . . .</p>
        <p>Mr. T. Ilealey (N.B. Cork, Nat.): Does the right hon. gentleman MORO to say that it will be effectively pared throngh the House ? . .</p>
        <p>Mr. Asquith : Time will show. Mr. !Jerold Smith (Warrington C.) : Would the right hon. gentleman my whether his recommendation of one man one vote means one vote one value. Mr. Arqiiith : I hope that in • rhetorical way approximately represents it. (Laughter). bir 11. KlMllet (Wandsworth, C.) : May I ask whether he will include in the bill the principle of proportional representation? Mr. Asquith : I cannot go into the details now, but no doubt that will be considered.</p>
        <p>Mr. Harold Smith naked the right hon. g,ntlemso whether he was prepared to gives week or more next session, as promised, for till discussion of the Conciliation Bill, in addition to the time available 1%, the discussion of female suffrage which would bn furnished by amendments adding enfranchisement of omen to the terms of the Manhood Suffrage Bill ? Mr. Asquith : The promise Melee with regard to the Conciliation Bill will, of coerce hold 'pod (MISS) the promoters waive its performince.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>NEW EDUCATION BILL</head>
        <p>NEW EDUCATION BILL</p>
        <p>Nonconformists Demand a Measure Next Session.</p>
        <p>Nonconformist members of the !louse of Commons at a meeting on Tuesday, under the presidency of Sir A. Spicer passed a resolution gratefully recognising the efforts the Government had mode to settle the educa- tion controversy, but at the same time expressing the conviction that the matter is of such vital importance that a Bill should be introduced next session for the purpose of removing grievances under which Non- conformists throughout the country have mutt-red so long. The resolution is to hc forwarded to the Prime Minister, with a rerpregt that a deputation may be received at an early date.</p>
      </div>
