      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>MAYOR'S SUNDAY.</head>
        <p>MAYOR'S SUNDAY.</p>
        <p>The Rev. E. C E. Clrleton on Church and State.</p>
        <p>The time-honoured custom of inaugurating the civic year by attendance at divine service was observed on Sunday when the Mayor Riad Corporation of Warrington paid an offici Ivist to the Parish Church. Unfortunately the weather war of the typical November kind—gloomy, damp and misty - and the number of townspeo,,le accompanying his worship to church was consequently not as large as urns'. The procession, which left the Town Hall shortly after ten o'clock, was headed by the band of the Territorials followed by a detach- ment of the battalion, under the command of Lieutenant Colonel J. D. Fairclongh. Then came members of the Church Lads' Brigade, St. John Ambulance Corps, Fire Brigade and police force. Preceded by the mace and sword bearers walked his Worship the Ma or (Councillor Dr. Joseph) who had on his right the Town Clerk (Mr J Lyon Whittle) and on his left his chaplain (Rev E C E Carleton). The members of the Council and officials present were : Aldermen Forshaw, Smet- hurst, Bolton, Pierpoint, Tinnion, Bennett and John Evans, and tkuncillors Birders; Jenkins Rogers. Ilawtlil rn, Dickenson, Toole, Halliwell, Cuuningbn, Lashford, Steel, Cr aik, Locker, Chantler, Sankey, Ashcroft, Wi'san and Henshall ; Messrs A T Hallaway (deputy town clerk), J Fairhurst (borough treasurer), W H Woodcock (borough collector), Dr. Hutt (assistant medical officer), W T Fl rod (inspector of r uisances), T Longdin (borough surveyor), A M Ker (assistant surveyor), It Young (c, rnetery registrar), C Madeley (curator and librarian), J Gray (water engineer), NV S Howard (cashier of gos deportment), and F V L Mathias (electric II engineer). The following justices were in attendance : —Messrs G Pilling, S Wright, W Richardson, R T Fairclough and W T Willi rms. They were accomponied by Mr F White (magistrates' clerk) and Mr E A White (deputy magistrates' clerk). In addition to members of the Town Council, the Education Committee was represented by Mr G Walker, and Mr. J. Moore Murray, (director). On arrival at the west door of the church, the pro- cession was met by the clergy, choir, and church- wardens. The hymn 'Onward, Christian soldiers " was sung as a processional to Sullivan's tune. The clergy taking part in the service were the Rector (Rev Canon Willis), the Rev Canon Stevenson, and the Revs E C E Carleton, A J Bratt, T Vickers and J M Buckmaster. The anthem "I was glad when they said unto me " (Elrep) was beautifully sung by the choir, the quartette being taken by Master Sankey, Messrs J H Draper, J B Poole and W Dennett, Mr. F. M. Darby, Mus. Bac., was at the organ. The Rev. E. C. E. CARLE rest preached from the words " Render unto Clear the things that are Cremes "(St. Matthew xxii. 21) and said he supposed the aim of the enemies of Jesus on this occasion was to get Flim to compromise Himself with the Roman authorities and no bring Himself under the suspicion of disloyalty to the Government. 'rho reply of our Lord was no simple and obvious, yet no complete and unanswerable, that Ilia adversaries were for the moment silenced. It was also a reply that served for the guidance of His people of all times—when the demands of the earthly Sovereign Appeared to clash or were incons'atent with the claims doe to God. Questions of this kind were continually wising in the Church. They arose in the early days of the Church's history, and questions of this kind were agitating the Church at the present moment. Jeans drew a distinction between what was due to the heavenly Sovereign and what was die to the earthly Sovereign, ant when the distinction was drawn and clearly recognised, it was found that both duties could be done without the clashing of one with the other. When the clashing came it was only because there had been some foul play, such as that of , which Daniel was the victim and of which the .early Christians were the victims under persecuting emperors There were two kingdoms—the one visible and the other invisible. G orl reigned over oat, earthly 1 Sovereigns over the other. Men male the Itws of the earthly kingdom,Ged the jeweler the heavenly kingdom, The one they found in the Statute Books of the different kingdoms of the world, the other in the Word , of God and in the rulings of the Church, based upon and deduced from that Word. In this land it bad been our happiness that the distinction between these two 1 things had been on the whole, faithfully kept and ' preserved. Our rulers had recognized the importance of</p>
        <p>SEPARATING TEMPORAL -AFFAIRS FROM THE SPIRITUAL</p>
        <p>which came specially under the cognizance of the Church, and which were better left uuder proper con- ditions to the charge of the chief Ministers, who were responsible for its well-being and government—Dever interfering as long al the Church kept within its own province. And if, through any evil influence, or mils- roneption of duty, their spiritual rulers and governors went beyond their province, the laws of the lend might very pruperly be appealed to, to restrain them. The ' separatenevs got the two jurisdictions was easily recognizable to those who would give it serious con- sideration. Parliament made the temporal laws, while the spiritual laws were made in Convocation—an institution older than the other. The temporal laws we called statute., the spiritual laws canons, and we had separate courts ant tribunals for the execution of those laws. The distinction of jurisdiction had come down to us from the earliest times. Notwithstanding all that, however, there had been dishing through carelessness acd inadvertence, end sometimes through motives that would not hear Investigation. On the one bead, men in the Church had</p>
        <p>SOUGHT To GRASP AT TEMPORAL POWER</p>
        <p>and on-ai-e-alseX-11;;1,--tle-itii-t;1;.I.;;;;;i6,;;;.wsught to use the Church for psliticsl ends. Wherever there had been a confounding of the civil aid spiritual juris- diction., harm to the Church and nation hsel el ways been the result and those who wished well to the nation had toilht always to keep them separate and distinct. The die. ioction was clearly suggested in the words of the Savi stir, " Itendrr unto Ce Sr the things that are C.esar's and unto God the things that ere God's." They must treat the Church as • Divine thing, leave it to the Divine care and trcst to the method God had appointed for its ssfety and security. A man must have a very iraderittate view indeed of the Church of God who thought it ens to he placed in the keeping of an assembly whose only qualiflca. lion for judging is spiritual matters wee ' that its members had been elected on • Lewis of household ittffiage-a very workable basis, no doubt, for a Government having to do with temporal matters which fell within the sphere of ordinary civil authorities-bat not fi r a body intended to represent the Kingdom of God on earth. They had to be continually going back in these days to first principles. They were in dr niter of forgetting even the simplest truthn and no gotng astray. The nation in days gone by sought in many ways to prevent the people forgetting the lessons of the peat. 1% hen Magna Charta was enacted the next anxiety was to see that it. provisions, which guaranteed the liberty of the subject, were kept in mind, and it was coafltxned over and again</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>00TWILL „:4 Its t?!!',.; fiAg 0r'....11111151 11-11tTI ( 4 4 C</head>
        <p>00TWILL „:4 Its t?!!',.; fiAg 0r'....11111151 11-11tTI ( 4 4 C</p>
        <p>NOTES BY " WRIST."</p>
        <p>The treasured home record has gone. Warrington failed to rise to the occasion on Saturday, and after a great game were obliged to bead the knee to the clever team loin Wigan. The result might easily have been different, for Warrington had two tries dis- allowed, and Wigan only won by 7 points to nil. Still, on the day's play they were the better team, and their success was largely • ne of brain over brawn ,I will explain what I mean.</p>
        <p>Warrington started off with rare confidence. The forwards heeled out well, and the backs combined with a precision that boded ill for the defence, of whatever calibre it might be. For twenty minutes, Wigan were outplayed in all departments, and if,the Wire- pullers had notched ten points in this period they would only have got their due. They were very near scoring more than once, and ill-luck bad perhaps something to do with the failure. But Wigan realised where and why they were getting beaten. The forwards were playing the orthodox game and losing the ball three times out of four. It was at this stage the secret cf Wigan's ultimate success was revealed. The men were wise enough to see the fault and remedy it. Tactics were changed. Instead of packing solidly, the scrummagers changed to loose formation with five men in the front row. No sooner was the bell in, than it was out—ou the Wigan side. They solved the problem of getting possession and the rest was plain sailing. Warrington continued on the same old lines—thrutching for possession—and were completely outgeneralled. That is why I say that brains beat brawn.</p>
        <p>-- Solar as the actual scoring went, Wigan did little considering the number of times the backs bad the ball. Their three-quarter play was no better than Warrington's, but the intelligent work of the front rank. and the superb individal efforts of Gleave at the base of the back, turned the scale. Gleave made one break-through a minute from the interval, which brought five points, and a goal was given to Leytham in the second half for a penalty against Tilley which should never have been awarded. Wigan bad only these chances of scoring and took them all.</p>
        <p>What about Warrington. Well, notwithstanding the outgeneralling of the pack, they should have won. They actually crosaed the Wigan line three times the defence was at anyrate beaten—and got not a single point. Once Bradshaw knocked-on from a crosskicic and was brought back after crossing behind the posts. That was a fair decision and a lucky escape for Wigan. The other two occasions leave room for the contention that the referee made a serious mistake. To take the last filet. Bradshaw and Way- well raced over at the corner and the touch flag was knocked down. But the Warrington men say, and many spectators suppott them, that it was Waywell who knocked the flag down, and that Bradshaw on the inside scored a fair try. It was so near a thing, and the men were in such a heap, that a mistake might be excused.</p>
        <p>But the other instance was of a more noticeable character and aroused hot contention at the time. 'oreover it was before Wigan had scored, when Warrington were going strong. and when the first point probably meant victory. In a melee near the Wigan line Leytham kicked over his own goal-line. The full back and tbe Warrington forwards raced for ,the ball, and got there simnlaneonsly. Renwick attempted to touch down, but Thomas threw himself full length on the ball and scrored a fair try. The referee thought Renwick had touched first, but I am told that after the match the Wigan full back admitted he never touched the ball.</p>
        <p>The decision was wrong, but it was one of those " near things " that any referee could be excused for missing. What followed aroused a lively debate on the stand as to the laws of the game. The referee ordered a " scrum " where Leytham kicked the ball— some fifteen yards out. The contention was that a " five yards scrum " should have followed the supposed touch.down, and at first thought it seems right. But rule 17 says "In all cases where Os bell 13 made dead in In-goal by a defending player after having last touched one of his own side in the field of play, or its player Lick, pass, knock, or carry the ball across his own goal line, the ball shall be brought back and a scrummage formed in the field of play at the spot whence it was touched, kicked, passed, knocked, or carried back."</p>
        <p>So the referee was right in his interpretation of the rule, but, he was wrong in thinking that Renwick had touched down. These things are all in the game, of course, but Warrington had the misfortune on Satur- day to suffer twice when a different decision might have altered the nhole course of the game, and iiossibly the ultimate result.</p>
        <p>Additional credit must be given to Wigan for winning the match, with substitutes in the ranks for Sharrock, Todd, and J. Thomas. But the substitutes were among the most notable players on the field and had a lot to do with the victory. Renwick was splendid at full back, but the three-quarters were nothing to boast of considering their reputation. On the home side Woodburn and Waywell were more prominent than Leytham and Miller, but they got little support from the centres Bradshaw has yet to play a good game against Wigan, and Dickenson was much too slow. The pace of the game, too, told on another member of the side whose years are more than he likes to remember Tilley. The full-back played his worst game this season, and long before the end he was practically run to a standstill. No one appreciates the clever Widnesian more than I do, but he cannot go on for ever, and so far he has played in every match. Who is to deputise Cr fill the vacancy when it comes?</p>
        <p>The League table up to Saturday M as follows : For. Against. Per- P. W. L. D. G. T. P. G. T. P. ent'ge Wigan Hudderaft'ldlo 8 1 1... 34 60 248... 12 15 69 85.00 Halifax lO 7 1 2... 20 30 130... 10 9 47 moo Hull K. it— 8 6 3 0... 23 17 97... 11 7 43 7500 Wakefield T. 9 6 2 1... 20 29 127... 13 11 59 72.22 Huuelet 9 6 2 1... 20 27 121... 15 9 57 72 22 Broughton 7 5 2 0... 14 11 61... 8 9 43 71.42 Oldham 10 6 9 1... 25 37 161... 21 28 126 65.00 Widnes lO 6 3 1... 10 14 71... 6 13 53 65.00 Warriogtoa 11 7 4 0... 12 23 93—'11 20 96 63 63 8 4 3 1... 22 22 110... 15 23 99 5623 Dewebury 10 5 4 1... 24 31 141... 18 26 114 55.60 St. Helene 8 4 4 0... 12 23 93-11 10 52 50410 Leigh 10 5 5 0... 26 31 145... 17 18 88 50 00 Rochdale 11.10 5 5 0... 19 23 107... 14 29 112 50.00 Salford lO 4 5 1... 11 M 91... 20 22 106 45110 York 9 4 0... 16 14 74... 27 25 129 4114 Hull ...... lO 4 1... 22 35 149... 20 22 106 4090 Barrow lO 3 1... 7 18 68— 24 26 126 3500 Coventry lO 3 1... 14 14 70... 19 42 164 35.00 Leeds 9 3 0... 11 20 82... 16 26 110 3333 Batley Swinton 10 3 - 0... 12 15 C 0... 22 27 125 30 00 Runcorn Ebbw Vale 10 0 2... 9 8 42... 25 38 164 2000 Bromley Bradford ll 1 0 0.,. 012 54... 35 51 900</p>
        <p>Since Saturday the Colonials have added another victory to their credit, but they were lucky to beat Leigh by 1:1 points to 12. The home team had the better of the game and missed a goal in front of the pow,. Ganley was great behind the " scrum" and for general cleverness equalled Mcliivat.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday Runcorn lost at Broughton by 3 goals and a try to 1 goal 2 tries—bard lines ! 'Davidson scored every point for the Rangers The same day, Hull got another beating at Hamlet, and if Warrington do not get a couple of points from the Boulevard to-day there will be 1100:18 disappointment. 'Hull are a weak tide at present.</p>
        <p>The Wirepullers have generally given a good acoount of themselves at Hull. Last year they drew there, with the score one try each, and as they have already beaten Hull at Wilderspool they should be full of confidence to-day.</p>
        <p>The selected team is: Full back, Tilley; three quarters, Waywell, ensworth, Bradshaw, Woodburn : half bi t!ks, lirookes, Creevey ; forwards, Thomas, Johnston (captain), Sbugars, Arnold, Cheaters, Bradshaw.</p>
        <p>the " A "team, though they were swamperl by 33 points to 2 at Wigan, gave a better showing than th</p>
        <p>score suggests. It was only in the forwards that they were badly beaten. The backs did well, and particularly Lloyd, Uneworth, Vernon and Povey, the latter giving a particularly smart isplay. By the way, Jordan, for the second week in succession, had a try given against him for obstruction. Jordan is a firm believer in letting no man pass him if he can help it—hence the slight difference between himself and certain referees. For all that, he is one of the beat all r, nod men who ever loaned a primrose and • blue jersey.</p>
        <p>To-day, the " A" team will be at home to Wigan Highfield, and the following strong aide has been selected : —Full back, Jordan ; three-quarters, Povey, Vernon, Carter and Clare; halfbacks, Lloyd, Daintith; forwards, Cooper, Naylor, Harmer, Critchley, Gibbs, (P.) Boardman lam glad to see Carter back again, and hope he will soon find the form which carried him into the first team. Boardman and Naylor will strengthen the pack,</p>
        <p>The transfer of Frawley to Hull does not look likely to materiality. bat there are more unlikely things than that the Colonials will have to bay him off for their own purposes. Injuries are weakening the Australasian resesves considerdbly, and the matter has already been discussed.</p>
        <p>To-day's matches are.: Batley v. Bramley. Bradford v. Halifsx. Brought n Rangers v. Widnes. Huddersfield v. Coventry. Ebbw Vale v. Dewsbury. Hull v. Warrington. Barrow v. Oldham. York v Hull Kingston Rovers. Hunslet v. Keighley. Wigan v. Leeds. 11,-chdale Hornets v. Leigh. Runcorn v. Svrintcn. Salford v. St. He'.ens. Wakefield Trinity v. Australia, Kick-off to-day 2.4).</p>
      </div>
