      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>Warrington's First Home Defeat.</head>
        <p>Warrington's First Home Defeat.</p>
        <p>At one time last Se' arday, the fog looked like pr. e- venting all play in the.big match with Wigan, but an hour before the kink off the mist was dispelled by the sun, and for the greater part of the game the light wee not had. IC3 doubt the weather affected the attend- ance, but there was nevertheless the biggest gate of the seaaon, th,ugh not no many as usual came from Wigan. Fully 8,000 people assembled, and the receipts were £lB2. The home team fielded as selected, but the Wiganers had substitutes for Sharron's, Tnomas, Tod 1, and Ramsdale. They came with a fine record of only one defeat, and as Warrington had not lost a point at horn) there wan a battle royal for supremacy. The teams were as follows : Warring ton.—Tilley, back ; Waywell, D ickeneon Bradshaw and Woodburn, threequarter-backs ; Brooks, and Creevey, half-backs ; Johnston, Thomas, Shugars. Arnold, Bradshaw and Chesters, forwards. Wigan.—Renwick. back ; Leytham, Jenkins, Price and Miller, tlyeequarter-backs ; Gleave and Prescott, half-backs ; Williams, Seeling, Winstanley, Silcook, Abel's and Green, forwards. Referee, Mr. R. Robinson, Bradford ; touch-judges, Messrs R II Moore (Oldham) and 0 Robinson (Runcorn). Johnston started the game for Werrington, and Renwick was early in difficulties, his clearing kick being charged down. Luckily for the visitors the full-back managed to recover and save on the line The Wigan forwards raised the siege and a speedy run by Seeling reunited in Dickanson being downed in the Warrington half. Warrington's front rank men scrummaged well and some smart handling by the backs kept the Wigan defence busy. Woodburn twice made fine runs down the touch-line, and Renwick was lucky to stop the second movement when Cheaters received the ball from Woodburn a yard from the line. Warrington were making 4reat efforts to ',mum the opening score, and at this stage Wigan were overplayed. Creevey got away on the right and then croaskicked cleverly to the opposite wing. Bradshaw dashed for the ball as Miller attempted to field and charging the oval down he gathered and raced over behind the posts amidst a roar of cheering. Unfortunately the Warrington man had knocked-on, and a floe effort was negatived by the referee. Fine play from both teams followed, and the crowd was kept on the tip-toe of excitement. Brooks, Creevey, and Bradshaw aOn gut going, but the centre was at fault, and Leytham cleared with a ponderous kick. Prescott stole away from a scrum in the home half sods short pant set Leytham going. The famous winger dribbled pant Tilley, but Brooks came up with a rush and made the ball dead se it passed the line. This was the narrowest escape Warrington had bad, and Waywell changed the venue with a smart run and punt on the left wing, further good work being done in the loose by Arnold, who dodged through and carried play to the Wigan quarters before being grassed. Leyttiam got into difficulties here and kicked Over the line. Renwick and Thomas both dived for the ball and the Warrington man appeared to have the touch, but the referee was against him, and the visitors escaped. Wigan rushed the game past the centre again, and a minute from the interval Glees° dodged through the Warrington backs and threw to Price, who took the ball on the run and scored a fine but unexpected try ender the posts. Les thorn easily added a goal, end the teams crossed over with Wigan leading by 5 points to The game was resumed with the locals still full of determination, though it was admitted that they had been unlucky. Dashing tactics upset the Wiganers, and Bradshaw rushed over at the corner after passing by Brookes and Creevey, but the flag was knocked down at the critical moment, and again Warrington were beaten by the luck. Brooks tried to rush through, but Wigan tackled well, and after live minutes anxiety , they took charge of the game. Fine punting , by Leytham and Prescott forced Warrington back, and an alteration in serum" tactics by the visitors gave them pessessics. almost continuously. Tilley was hard pressed, and once when tackled, the referee penalised him for not playing the ball—a decision which was challenged all round the ground—sad Leytham kicked a goal which was simply a gift. Wanington for some time were kept defending, but another rally saw Creevey try the cross kick once more, but Woodburn was adjudged off-side. The closing stages of the game were fought out in semi-darkness. Wigan had scheme when Leytham kicked with only Tilley to beat, and the custodian easily saved the eituation. The Warrington backs again got in a passing movement and were making excellent progress when Price intervened, but this player knocked on when he was just getting clear A run byGleave looked like adding to the score, but the superior pace of Woodburn tweed the situatioa. Result : Wigan two goals one try (seven points) ; Warrington nil.</p>
        <p>WIDNES BEAT SALFORD.</p>
        <p>Played at Widnes, before 2,000 spectators. Early on Widnes were penalised for obstruction, and Wesley kicked a One goal. Just before the interval Salford were penalised, and Taylor placed a goal. Lally injured his shoulder and bad to retire. Half-time : Widnes 1 goal (2 points), Salford 1 goal (2 points). Immediately on resuming Widnes attacked, and Jones dropped a goal. A dribbling movement ended in O'Gara scoring a second try,and a few minutes later this player retired, also through iujurles. John then broke away for Salford, and Caok scored an unimproved try. Just before the fininh good wor by Reid enabled Johnson to cross the line. Taylor failed at goal. Result— Widnes 2 goals 2 tries (10 p lints), Salford 1 goal 1 try (5 points). Teams and &amp;finials : Widnes—Jones, back ; Swinton, Taylor. Laws, and Reid, three-varter backs ; O'Gara and Lally, half backs ; (.0 Awe!, S Aspey, Lloyd, Johnson, Rigby, and Higgins, forwards. Salford—John, back ; Cook, Barnes, Lovelock, nod Wesley, three-quarter beck,; May and W Thomas, half backs ; Thom is, Ritchie, Rues, Pugsley, Thom and Bevan, forwards. Referee. Mr W Taylor, Hamlet. Touchsjudges, Messrs A Lee, Leigh, and tr, Crabtree, Rochdale.</p>
        <p>EBBW VALE v. ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>At Ebbw Vale, before a fair attendance. Both teams were at full strength. The home team had most of the game in the Reit half, and Davies kicked a penalty goal, no further score being registered to the interval. St. Helens improved in the seaond half, and the home line was in grave danger, but tligy set up a defence which was impenetrable. Result —Ebbw Vale 1 goal (2 points), St. Helens nil.</p>
