        <head>ST. VINCENT'S V. OUGHTRINGTOSI AMATEURS.</head>
        <p>ST. VINCENT'S V. OUGHTRINGTOSI AMATEURS.</p>
        <p>At Stamford Park, Altrineham, St. Vincent'e, one, goal ; Oughtrington Amateurs, nil.</p>
        <p>BROADHEATH V. LATCHFORD.</p>
        <p>These teams met on Saturday at Navigation-road, Broadhezth. Right from the start the Latchford (thwart:ls showed the better combination, and when they opened their account it was no more than they deserved. Aroused by this reverse, the Heathens infused more life and vigour into their play, but the Latchford defence held out, half-time arriving with the score : Latchford, one goal ; Broadheath, nil. In the second half play ruled even for a time. After good work by the home forwards they managed to equalize, only for Latchford again to take the lead. After this the game underwent a complete change, the Latchford defence falling unaccountably away, and the Heathens quickly added three more points, gaining a capital victory with the score: Broadheath, four goals; Latchford, two goals. INCTSFORD ST. CROSS T. LYNIM.</p>
        <p>At the Tower Grounds, Knutsford, Lymm played a capital game. At the commencement St. Cross want off In good style and were the feat to score through Taylor. Lymm soon equalized. Taken all through the game vas of a fairly even nature, hut Lymm proving the better adepts at scoring, ran oat winners by six goals to three. AIOBBERLEV V. SALE ALBION.</p>
        <p>At Mobber-lei;ii—iia-t.unfai",--itbe -lie-tne- imam winning a good game by four gosh to one. SECOND DIVISION, LATCHFORD R ECREATION v. ROBERTSON'S.</p>
        <p>Tirase local rivals met on the ground of the former and a capital game ended in a draw, both goals coming front penalties.</p>
        <p>OUGHTRINGTOS AMATEURS V. EOWLEY.</p>
        <p>At Bentley: Taken all through- the —ga-tne wen. fairly even, and although beaten the Amateurs were by no means disgraced. A couple of goals from the foot of Maclannie gave Howley the victory by two ,s,oals to nil</p>
        <p>LATCRFORD RESERVE V. WISIIP-lELD UNITED.</p>
        <p>Ai-Theiw-s-11-11;TT-h•-firs-t IMII;Fw-rell-e-cWtes—ted. but the United-proved the better marksmen and led et the interval by 3 goals to one. The second half wee entirely in favour of Wellfleld, who indulged in • deal of shooting practice, winning in hollow fashion by 14 goals to one. Hawthorn notched 7of the goals. PAL/GATE UNITED V. STOCKTON HEATH ST. THOMAS'S</p>
        <p>At Padgate. Padgette pressed their opponenta at the commencement, bat there was no score before half-time. In the second portion matters were reversed, the Sainta having the better of the game, but, like their opponents, they failed to And the net, the result, a goalless draw, being a fitting ending.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>1,168 1,092 Majority for Rylands' Recreation, 96.</head>
        <p>1,168 1,092 Majority for Rylands' Recreation, 96.</p>
        <p>THE LATE MR. CONGREVE'S SUCCESSOR.</p>
        <p>The directors of the Manchester Ship Canal Co., have appointed Mr. H. A Reed, MICE., of London, as chief engineer of the company in succession to the late Mr. Hubeit Congreve. The appointment is a a popular one, for although only about 45 years of age, Mr. Reed has had a notable career. .•." ' ' .</p>
        <p>—Perhaps his chief work was that in connection with the great naval works at Gibraltar, which took over 10 years to complete and cost something like six million pounds, and of which Mr. Reed was the chief engineer. This work, which the admitalty entrusted to him, included harbour and dockyard extensi one, wharfs for the coaling of men-of-wJr, warehouses, arsetala, and the erection of a number of cranes lifting 40 tons.</p>
        <p>He has also been engaged in erecting big works at Plymouth, and is well known in connection with works on the Great Central Railway. Mr. Reed will be especially valuable to the Ship Canal Company inasmuch as he was one of the engineers engaged in its constructimi. Mr. Reed takes up his new luties on January lat.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>HOW TO CURE RHEUMATISM.</head>
        <p>HOW TO CURE RHEUMATISM.</p>
        <p>Go to any Chemist or Drug-store, and ask for the following prescription One ounce cf Infusion of Gentian, one ounce Syrup of Rhubarb, one ounce Liquid Barkola Comp sand, and five ouncze of Syrup of Ginger. Let these be well shaken together and take a teaspoonful after each meal and one at bedtime. This simple recipe is the famous Barkola Prescrip- tion, which since it was first prepired by a well- known doctor a few years ago has done so much to relieve the pangs of Rheumatism, gout and all uric acid diseases. -Very many thousands of cases have been entirely cured by it, and its fame has extended all over the world. It is especially useful in severe cases of Inflammatory Itheumati,m, and cures when everything else fails.</p>
