      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>LOCAL PARLIAMENT.</head>
        <p>LOCAL PARLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>INSURANCE BILL APPROVED.</p>
        <p>The debate on the Insurance :Bill which was before the" House" at its previous sitting was concluded at the meeting of the Parliamentary Debating Society on Tuesday evening. 'Die resolution introduced by Councillor J. Hawthorn (the Home Secretary), was as follows: "That this house welcomes the introduction into the House of Commons of tte National Insurance Bill by the Chancellor of the Exchequer believing that this great scheme of National Insurance against invalidity and unemployment will lifts dead weight off the lives of an immense multitude of men and women in Great Britain and trusts that the measure will soon become the law of the land."</p>
        <p>Captain H. G. ROBERTS (Secretary of State for War), ia supporting the resolution said that the attempt that was being made by the Chancellor of the Exchequer to relieve the great distress the country suffered from during the period of trade depression in I outbreaks of sickneae, were matters which ought to have not only the support but the cordial approval of the whole country. The bulk of the work under the Bill war going to 'be performed by the Friendly Societies who would remain in the same position they occupied to-day. The officials, however, would be found in the General Post Office together with a supply of clerks. Voluntasy payment was better than compulsion, bat if it was left to the worker he would not provide for the periods of sickness and unemploy- ment and no it was necessary that compulsion should be brought to bear upon him. If the Bill became law as be was sure that it would—(opposition cries of" No ") --a man would always be sure of obtaining his les a week. The relief of the sick man from the thought that he would be unable to provide the necessary food and clothing for those dependeot upon him would tend to a more rapid return to breadth than would be the case under the conditions of the present day. (Applause.) Mr. Makestaf It) (C. Paisley) then rase to submit the following amendment That this House while it welcomes any scheme having for its object nati .nal insurance against sickness, invalidity, and unemploy- ment repine the conspicuous failure of his Majesty's present ministers adequately to deal with the question in the measure subrnitlei to the House, and trusts that as a result of the present diecunions in Parliament and the country, a sane, sensible and workable scheme may be developed, embodying the generally expressed sympathy of all parties towards such a desirable object." Mr. lA'srs•Liff-Jons (the Prime Minister) : That is no &amp;meta:meat but a direct negative to the resolution That should have been brought forward last week. The SPEAKER ruled the amendment inadmissable on the ground that it was a direct negative, but eventually the amendment was accepted as a resolution. Mr. MCDERm ID said that from the time the Bill was introduced it bad been a conspicuous failure ; every day it had been chopped ' until it Basin such a position as to be almost unrecog- , nisoble. A Government never had abetter opportunity of dealing with a great national question,as the support of the State was with them on the principle of the I measure. The Bill au first received with acclamma- tion on all sides of the House but upon being looked Into it was found that there had never been a Bill con- taining as many failures. (Laughter.) It did sot even attempt to deal with those in the most need—those over 65 years of age, with whom the liaL ility for sick- ' seas was more manifest. With regard to tha question of the Friendly Societies taking the matter over, and I speaking as one who had been a member of one of those organisations for a quarter of century, he would say that the Friendly Societies had been betrayed, but were now refusing to he murdered. (Applause.) If they did agree to be murdered, however, they would not commit suicide. (laughteir.) They were agreed that the result of this measure must inevitably be to "snuff " them out, if not immediately, by degrees. The day the Bill was passed they would cease to be a great asset of the country. The Bill would also destroy the voluntary institutions—the infirmaries sod hospitals. Something like £4,600,000 was annually being raised and expended for these it:Haiti:Hone and he made bold lousy tht.t that work could not be carried on by the Government with double the amount. The scheme in its effect was a direct additional taxation on the workers and industries without giving any relief in any other direction. Mr. PETER DEVONPOR't (C. Walt Salford) seconded the amendment and said that he thought the matter was going ahead at too fast a pace. Mr. M. HOWARTH (President of the Board of Trade) moved that the amendment Se rejected cu the ground that there had been no alternative scheme to the original resolution. He emphaticatly denied that the Friendly Societies and Trade Unions did not agree with the Bill, they had nothing but the highest praisa for it. He challenged the House to point out • previous Bill which had been so widely discussed and laid before the country for as long a period as this one. Mr. Lloyd George had taken the most infinite pains to meet every issue or interest that was concerned. lie thought the reason why the Opposition were wishing to hold up the Bill was because if it was allowed to get into work- ing order before the next election they knew that they would have to submit to a further term in the wilder- ness. He looked upon that Bill as the most important measure that bad yet been placed bef. r the House, ea it would lesson the amouot of misery to a greeter degree than any previous measure. Mr. W. WATSoN (Soc., East Birmingham) said that the Bill should he framed on a non-contributory basis. He thought that the Government would have been acting inS more sensible manner if it had been pro- posed to expend this money on the demolition of slum dwellings. Mr. G. DAVEV-SNIITif (president of the Board of Education) also opposed the amendment, and said that no idea had been expressed by the Opposition as to what aim they had in view. The most important factor In this country was the love of liberty displayed by every tingliahroan, and this Bill was going a step— in the right direction by providing a man with liberty and aiding him in the time of need. Mr. W. H. STANSFIELD (leader of the Opposition) said that although they were in favour of the prin- ciple of the Bill, they could have no alternative but to damn its details. But even Liberals themselves had disagreed with some of the details, as wu instanced by the objections of Sir Charles Macara. He maintained that the sectional charges :o the Hill were wrong in principle,and expressed himself in favour of • levelling up of the charges on an income tax basis. He sug- gested that the reason why the Radicals resented legitimate criticism of the details was because the whole matter was an electioneering dodge, and when the fallacies of the measure were exposed the shafts got between the joints of their armour and they did not like it. It was claimed that the Bill would improve and maintain the health and vitality of the nation. If that were so then there would be less sickness to provide for as the years passed, but the Bill proposed to eccumulate reserves and that meant unfairly taxing the present generation fur the beneli: of posterity. The real difficulty the country had to fight was the problem of casual labour but the Bill did not attempt to solve that difficulty. He was sceptical about the potential good of the Bill and urged that it should have a ..are extended and adequate discusston. There was, however too much haste on the part of the Governmeot to clear the path for Home Rule and other measures. (Laughter). _</p>
        <p>The PRIME MINISTLIZ (Mr. E. Wll3l:l64oDell)sppealed for support of the Bill on the ground that it was a great 'national scheme. It was surprising, when one studied the question, how few of the Acts pursed by the Imperial Parliament dealt with the nation as • whole. But this was justly called a national rcheme of horn, Moreover, it was not • charity scheme. (Applause). We hardly realised the tremendous scope of the Bill as yet, for it directly insured 15,000,000 people—in fact, the whole of the population under the , income tax limit came within its operations. The resolution proposed by the Opposition charecterised the Bill ass conspituous failure. flow could they soy that in face of favourable expressions of opinion from members of their own party ? Mr. F. E. Smith stated at Birmingham that he accepted the Bill without any reservation whatever, and no desire for party advantage would lead him to oppose it. Continuing, the Premier pointed out that the Bill bound the employer, the employee and the State together, and recognised the solidarity of the nation. For s )iue LIMB the friendly</p>
        <p>societies opposed the measure, but there was no ground for their fears. The only societies which would suffer were those which were not sound at the present lime. Surely if they were not sound and this Bill meant their disbanding, it would be no serious loss. On the other band be believed the friendly societies as a whole would benefit largely by the scheme. Mr. Lloyd George however, left them th, ir freedom of choice. Whilet the trade unions were objecting to the Bill they received an actuarial opinion that they woold benefit greatly by it. In the actuary's own words : It would mean a chstinct strengthaning of the financial position of the unions and an improvement in the security for the benefits not transferred to the Government section." That, in plain language, meant thot the trade unions would be greatly relieved on their friendly society side and would have mach more money available for their purely trade union work. The scheme was going to become law—(applause)—ba•suse it was in the hands of Mr. I,loyd George—the most humane states- man who had ever taken put in British politics. (Applause). Mr. T. P. O'Connor had p tinted out that no other man in the House of Commons could carry this Bill through, because Mr. Lloyd George Ind the courage that never flinched, tenacity that never loosed its grip, a patience that was never exhausted, a power of work that no labour appalled. (Applause.) Councillor J. Hawn:OßS (the Home Secretary) brought He debate to a close. He said if the Opposi- tion were so keen on this question of National Insurance, what had prevented them bringing forward such • measure during tha term of nearly twenty years they Were in office? The Bill WS not supposed to be perfect and the Chancellor of the Exchequer had stated that be was prepared to meet any legitimate grievance. It had ben forgotten, however, that the Bill provided a n ,tional help for the benefit of the poor of the country who were powerless to help themselves. He would appeal to them to throw asida all potty feeling and put the measure to practical test, as its provisions could be amended where desirable.</p>
        <p>The amendment wri-s—thien put to the Howie but was lost by 19 cotes to 22, after which the original resolu- tion was submitted awl carried without s dissentient.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>KEFORM BILL.</head>
        <p>KEFORM BILL.</p>
        <p>Next Tuesday evening the Prime Minister (Mr. E. Wynne Jone 3), will introduce a Reform Bill as follows : A Bill to establish Manhood Suffrage, to abolish Plural Voting, to fix one day on which Elections shall be held, to close all Licensed Houses on the Election Day and to arrange a system of Transferable Voting, and to provide for a Rodistribution of Parliamentary Beats throughout Great Britain and Ireland. Be it enacted, Clause 1. That all male persons of the age of 21 years and upwards shall be entitled to one vote subject to the conditions hereinafter provided. Clause 2. That it shall to necessary in order to exercise such vote that there shall have been three month's residence prior to any election in the con- tituency in which the vote is to be exercised. Clause 3. After a voter has been placed on the register he shall not be disqualified by reason of his removal from one constituency to another. Clause 4. That only one vote shall be given by any voter at a General Election, and that any voter guilty of voting more than once shall be liable to imprison- ment.</p>
        <p>Clause 5. That in the event of there being more than two Candidates for one Porliamentary seat each elector shall be allowed to indicate chick candidate he desires his vote to be transferred to if the candidate of Isla first choice is third or lower on the poll, and no candidate has a majority of the voles cant. Clause G. That all elections (excepting bye-elections) shall take place on one day between the hours of Sam. and 10 p.m. and all public-houses, club, &amp;c., licensed for the sale of intoxicating liquors shall be closed for the whole of that day. - . .</p>
        <p>Clause 7. That no person shall let, lend, levy, hire, or borrow any vehicle whatsoever for the purpose of con- veying voters to the place of poll. Clause 8. That a sufficient numf-er of Polling Stations shall be provided to ensure that so person shall be compelled to travel a greater distance than two miles to record his vote.</p>
        <p>Chose 9. That no canvassing shall be allowod on behalf of any Candidate other than by means of Public flfeetiogs and the Candidates election address. Such address shall be delivered through the medium of the Post Office.</p>
        <p>_ Clause 10. The Returning Oftluera chews viz.: The actual coat of holding an election, taking the poll, counting the votes and declaring the result shall be borne by the /state.</p>
        <p>Clause 11. That all expenses incurred by a candidate or his election agent or by any person, Institution, or Organisation, working in the interest or on benalf of arch candidate shall be computed from a date at least It months prior to the date of the election, such expenses shall be returned through the Returning Officer. And any candidate who spends either himself or ;by any organisation working on his behalf a cum exceeding .C 31.0 shall he unseated. Clause 12. 'flare shall be a redistribution of Parliamentary seats for England, Scotland, Wales and Ireland giving equal representation according to population over given areas. Such redistribution to be readjusted after each census. Clause 13. The provisions of this Bill shall come into force on January Ist, 1512.</p>
        <p>While a theatrical performance, entirled "An Ideal Thief," wan being given in the Town Hell, High Wycombe, the residence of the authorees, Mrs W Parry, balf smile distsnr, stets burglarowly ante redend money and other things stolen.</p>
