        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>SAWRDAY, NOVEMBER 18, 1911</p>
        <p>The decision arrived THE COAL at on Thursday by the CRISIS PAST. Miners' Federation of</p>
        <p>Great Britain should go far towards moderating the views of those who believe, against a great volume of evi- dence to the contrary, that trade union leaders are always in favour of strikes. Had the Federation not been anxious above every- thing for a settlement on absolutely peaceful terms, we should by this time have been spectators of another great industrial struggle. The representatives who have been in session at Westminster have had to resist a very strong movement in favour of a cessation of work, a movement too, which was not confined to a particular area like South Wales, where feeling has been running to extraordinary heights, but was spread through all the districts of the Federation. Fortunately, the men who do not want a strike if a settlement can possibly be obtained by other means, greatly outnumber the irreconcilables. As the voting of Thursday showed, there is a majority of about one-fifth of the [total number against an immediate ballot on the subject of a national stoppage. This result must be attributed in the main to wise and calm leadership. If the trade union officials had wanted a strike they could undoubtedly have obtained authority for it at once. The decision of Thursday is nominally nothing more than a postpone- ment, but it should really lead to a settle- ment, as by the soundest and most experienced men in the trade union movement it is expected to do. There is a similar suspension of hostilities in regard to the railway troubles. The opportunity for the peacemaker would be very great if either the railwaymen or the miners were disposed to tolerate interference or advice from the outside. Outside inter- ference, however,they will not endure. The real opportunity, therefore, lies with their employers —the railway companies and the mineowners. We must say for the railway companies that some of the most important of them are taking most admirable steps for averting a new crisis. They are voluntarily improving the wages and the conditions of service on their lines to an extent that cannot be considered otherwise than very generous. They have, in fact, acted in a spirit which would at once put an end to all anticipated trouble if they would give that recognition to the trade unions which has for almost the space of an average lifetime been conceded by the mineownere The difference between the mineowners and their men is one that is peculiarly open to the argument The decided negative that has so far been the answer to the men's demands is greatly capable of being improved on as a policy, and we venture to think that the country will expect the exhibition of a more conciliatory spirit now that the apprehended evil day has been put off almost indefinitely. There is one great coal district where, we may point out, the trouble is of a kind which is not affected by the decision arrived at on Thursday. In Northumberland and Durham there is an eight hours' difficulty. The miners of these two counties always protested against the Eight Hours' Act. They were willing that the measure should pass if the mines of Northumberland and Durham were exempted from its operation. There was good reason in this. No miner in Northumb?rland ana Durham worked more th In five hours a day. There were two shifts of five hours each. Since the Eight Hours' Act was passed the coalowners have insisted on three shifts in place of two. This has made a great and cruel domestic difficulty. The chances are that in most households in the pit villages there will be some members of the family working in each of the three shifts. The conseviuence, as may he guessed, will fall with "particularly heavy weight on the miner's wife, who must, under such con- ditions, be at work through most hours of the twenty-four. The miners are raising a protest on behalf of their wives, and it is one in which they may be relied on to persist. When all other questions affecting the coal- pits have been settled this will remain, unless, indeed the coalowners should come to the reasonable conclusion that the women must not be sacrificed to what are certainly not the necessary conditionompf an industry.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>200 ANINIALS KILLED AT SEA.</head>
        <p>200 ANINIALS KILLED AT SEA.</p>
        <p>The steamship Lismore arrived lIL Bristol from Cork after a terrible r x per ieece in tb• gale. Cettle, sheep, mid pig' to the number of 200 were killed &amp;alai the voyage.</p>
        <p>We had been content to "WHAT ABOUT THE let this matter drop,but BAND"? Captain Crosfield has rushed in at the last minute with an explanation. Now, explana- tions are proverbially dangerous, and after carefully weighing all that captain Crosfield has to say most of our readers will, we think, conclude that the Captain would have been better advised to remain silent. Ironical compliments on our ingenuity, and a con- fession of sadness "to see how we burk full and free discussion" do not amount to much, but they indicate a distortion of vision which has, we fear, descended upon Captain Crosfield since "experience opened his eyes." If any subject has had and still has, full and free discussion in our columns it is surely National Service; else we should undoubtedly have been spared the not infrequent and fervent protests of " Examiner" readers who prefer our space to be differently employed. This by the way. The Captain beats a resounding tatoo on the " thin crust which separates us from the volcano of war," and suggests that we are following the classical example of Nero when Imperial Rome was burning. Does it never occur to him that the admittedly fragile crust " is menaced more by the growth of armaments and the spread of militarism than by anything else? It is excellent that we should be prepared. Equally certain, too, is it that if we train up fighters they will want to fight. No ! the peace and protection propa- ganda of the N.S.L. does not appeal to us any more to-day than it did before our eyes were opened. There are better and higher ways of procuring peace on earth, and not even the martial strains of the Champion Band will render their appeal inaudible to the thinking majority of the people of England.</p>
        <p>Mr. Asquith met the THE PREMIER deputation of Women's AND WOMEN'S Suffrage Societies with SUFFRAGE. absolute candour and fairness yesterday. As head of the Government and of a Cabinet divided on the question he said he would not make himself responsible for the introduction of a measure which he did not conscientiously believe to be demanded in the interests of the country. But he was perfectly honest in informing the deputation that the Govern- ment left the Reform Bill (which is to go through next year) open to any amendments admitting women to the suffrage on the same terms as men. And he also gave the assur- ance that any such amendment, .which was carried in the House of Commons, would be considered an integral part of the Bill and defended in all its stages by the Government. We do not see that the suffragists could expect anything more—or hardly so much from a Premier whose personal convictions are at variance with their own. There is no mandate for adult suffrage—the sexes on an equality--but if they can secure a majority in the House the suffragists have a chance of coming into their own by the aid of the very instrument by which they say they have been betrayed. Seeing that for twenty years past successive Parliaments have given a majority to the second reading of the Women's Suffrage Bill, and that the suffragists claim to have a majority in the present House, the problem is anything but unsurmountable. How far are we, in fact, from the titanic change which will give every man and woman a vote on equal terms? The nearness of the great consum- mation gives one pause to think!</p>
        <p>We have previously THE BROTHERS pilloried some of the SMITH AND wild sayings of Mr. IRELAND. Harold Smith, M.P., in</p>
        <p>opposition t'o Home Rule, and the speeches of "F.E." indicate that the family feeling on the question is a family failing. Mr. F. E. Smith, was the principal speaker at the Conservative Colston banquet and he told his audience that "he thought he knew of at least two ways by which they could secure an appeal to the people on Home Rule, even under the present Constitution, and he could think of many ways of appeal- ing to the people outside the Constitution," Then why all this fuss about the Parliament Act and "single-Chamber Government " ? says the " Westminster Gazette." If Mr. 'Smith knows at least two ways of getting an appeal to the country under present conditions, both he and his colleagues have been talking a great deal of rubbish. Mr. Smith further told his audience: Things and acts which were indefensible under normal conditions might be deemed inevitable by determined men in revolutimary times, and, speak- ing with a deep sense of responsibility—lawyer as he was—he would a,sert that for those who were loyal in Ireland, who asked for nothing except that they should not be cast forth, there Iran no length from irhielt he would altrink in supporting the leader of the Irish Unionists Sir Edward Carson _ _ . • __</p>
        <p>Mr. Smith really Proves too much. If he knows two ways of peace of getting what he desires, the third alternative, of shouldering his pike and helping Sir Edward Carson to fill up the last ditch, savours more than a little of carrying his sword in his mouth, which, to be frank, is precisely the position in which we shall expect Mr. Smith's sword to remain.</p>
        <p>All who have any SHAKESPEARE strong regard for the SOCIETY, intellectual traditions of Warrington will rejoice that a Shakespeare Society has been added to our already rather formidable list of local institutions. There is room for the newcomer—in fact it has been a sort of reproach to the town that hitherto we have had no society established to honour the immortal bard by systematic study of his works. The inaugural meeting at the Town Hall on Tuesday promised well for the slimes s of the venture which is singularly fortunate in its officers and also in the fact that the ground has been well prepared for it by the painstaking work of Dr. Law and his colleagues 'in the " Macbeth " enter- prise. It now only remains to direct the keen and intelligent interest aroused along lines which will be most productive of really valuable results. Professor Elton, of Liverpool, and Canon Morley Stevenson, president of the society, placed before the members the true conception of the methods by which such a society can best fulfil its purpose. Pedantry and dry scholasticism are just as much to be guarded against as perfunctoriness or flippancy. The great aim should be profitable study on such lines as will ensure unflagging interest and genuine delight in the work. We would commend to the attention of the committee the pertinent criticisms and suggestions with regard to the programme for the session, which Mr. Alfred Perris contributes to the correspondence ,columns of this issue. Too much importance must not be attached to monthly meetings .of the orthodox type, with a treatise on some Shakespearian topic and a few general and complimentary speeches by way of comment. The most satisfactory and lasting results will be obtained by a series of groups meet- ing frequently for systematic and critical study and the interchange of views on knotty points. We concluded from what was said on Tuesday that the society has something a the kind in view, but we are inclined to</p>
        <p>agree with Mr. Perris that it would be advisable to hold anpther public in:iti:ing at which the scope of the society Id be defined in greater detail and candid expressions of opinion invited from intending members.</p>
        <p>Mr. Burns' regulations CoNsumrrioN making consumption a TO BE notifiable disease are a</p>
        <p>NOTIFIED.</p>
        <p>big _gain to the forces</p>
        <p>w-hich wage a never- ending battle against "the white plague." In future it will be the duty of every doctor or school medical inspector who comes across a case of pulmonary tuberculosis to report it to the local medical officer of health,l who will keep a register. The local authority will have power to provide sanatorium or other assistance. Dr. Newsholme, Medical Officer to the Local Government Board, estimates that the reduc- tion of the tuberculosis death-rate since 1880 represents a saving in 1909 of £5,000,000.0ncel the medical authorities can get on the track of the disease by notification, the saving of life and expense wia be increased to an extent impossible to estimate in figures. While the law is so far unsatisfactory in that it does not compel authorities to spend money in preventive measures, we may take it that willingness will not be wanting. Mr. Burns urges local authorities to use to the full their powers of preventing overcrowding and insanitary conditions, and it is satisfactory to know that the policy of the Warrington Health Authority in this direction, has, for some time been a forward one.</p>
        <p>Several correspondents MR. BONAR LAW'S have asked (says the "Two BAD "Morning Leader") for WINTERS." the reference to Mr. Law's "Two Bad Winters" speech. It was at the Hotel Cecil on 26th November, 1907, that Mr. Bonar Law, now the leader of the Opposition. said that everyone believed that we were on the eve of a new period of dumping "on a scale which has never been approached before. When that happens," continued Mr. Law, thea a ould be the opportuoity of Tariff Reformers. When cur workmen are going about the streets looking for work and unable to had it, and when ' they see the goads a bleb they would gladly make,' but are not permitted to make, coining from foreign countries which exclude our manufac- littera, and selling at their own doors, then it will take more thou any amount of theory to con- vince them that a system which had that result is, or can be, good for them or for the country. And what the Irish famine had done for Cobden's cause two bad winters would do fcr the cause of Fiscal Reform.</p>
        <p>The Irish debate on NOTHING ?—FOR Tuesday, while it THREEPENCE. interested and amused</p>
        <p>the House by the eloquence and the humour, conscious and unconscious, with which Mr. Healy and Mr. O'Brien gave expression to the bees in the bonnet that make them suspect and attack any legislation supported by the main body of Nationalist opinion, was significant, as it displayed from another angle the frame of mind which has, in the past few months, inspired a large section of the Tory Party to denounce the Insurance Bill as a fraud, and even to-day makes Lord Willoughby de Broke and men of his kidney, accuse the ' Government of "fiddling for servant-girls' , twopences." Mr. O'Brien, quite in the noble style of Lord Willoughby de Broke, accused the Government of taking threepence from Ireland and giving nothing. Was it nothing, asked Mr. Lloyd George, to give an Irish labourer 10s. a week for six months during sickness, and ss. a week if he broke down altogether? Was it nothing to a poor woman that she should get 30s. maternity benefit? Was it nothing to an Irishman that his stricken child could be sent to a sanatorium and get all the help that the best skill and science of the day can give?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>N9NS. ~,,,,</head>
        <p>N9NS. ~,,,,</p>
        <p>The new foot-bridge at Bridge Foot was opened on Thursday—without a banquet! * * "Hark the' Harold angels sing" a passer-by outside the Parr Hall last night was heard to remark. • *</p>
        <p>At Southwark Dioeesan Conference a lay- man caused much amusement by remarking "The laity, generally speaking, is 'give,' and the clergy is' take::</p>
        <p>On page 3 of this issue Mr. C. E. Parker discusses the elimination of industrial strife and the procuring of industrial efficiency, from a new viewpoint. His point is that "there is a great amount of latent intelli- gence amongst the workpeople themselves that our present system of industrial adjust- ment and remuneration does not call forth." The article merits the serious consideration of employers and workers in every trade and grade. _• * •</p>
        <p>Alderman Bennett h-as been regaling the "Lit and Phil" with an appreciative address lon Richard le Gallienne, and we are interested to note that he says of him: "he was born to be a poet, not a mere 'accountant." Would it be unkind to suggest to the Alderman that another poet, whom he knows even better, was born to be "a mere aclountant ?" All men hay.; their true metier, but not all are clearly aware what it is. • •</p>
        <p>Two of the " b'hoys'" who helped in their own unseemly way to get rid of Mr. Balfour from the leadership of the Unionist Party were the principal speakers at the Junior Conservative Association demonstration in the Parr Hall last evening. Their leader, Mr. Leo Mamie, was prevented a hearing at , the Conservative Conference at Leeds on Thursday, and we should not be surprised if now, seeing that the fall-in-and-follow-me brigade have become unpopular, they were to endeavour to repudiate their association with the "Die-hards." ••</p>
        <p>Mr. Balfour's seat in the House of Commons will, of course, be still on the front 1 Opposition bench. Mr. Gladstone has reeorded in a letter to Mrs. Gladstone a description of his first appearance in the House of Commons after his resignation of the Leadership of the Liberal Party : '1 came down to the House, and took my seat nearly in the same spot as last year, !finding Bright my neighbour, with which I was very well pleased. Granville and Hartington both preferred my continuing on the front bench to my going elsewhere. * * *</p>
        <p>Polling takes place on Tuesday in South Somerset, where Mr. Henry Vivian is the Liberal candidate. Both before and during the time he was Member for Birkenhead, Mr. Vivian addressed Liberal gatherings in Warrington, and invariably had such a good reception that we feel sure he has the best wishes of the Warrington progressives in his coming fight. A man of clear vision and great capacity, his absence from the House of Commons is a loss to that body.</p>
        <p>We wonder what Mr. Robert Pierpoint thinks of Mr. Balfour's " dismissal " from the , leadership of the Unionist Party. How like his own case it is I And what a peculiar satisfaction the present M.P., for Warrington must feel at being more or less concerned in both.</p>
        <p>' We notice that the hector still hop&amp; for "the right of entry" and denominational religious instruction in Council schools. His hope is assuredly vain, and at variance with the democratic tendency of the age in which we live. People nowadays, in increasing numbers, are content with religious teaching of an undenominational character in the day / schools while there is also a growing feeling in favour of secular instruction pure and simple. The clerical attitude is more consist- ent than commendable.</p>
        <p>* It is with interest that we notice that at the National Conservative Union Conference yesterday, a resolution was moved which suggested that Conservative members of Parliament should contribute the whole or part of their salary as M.P.'s to the fund for financing that hybrid production, the Con- servative working man candidate. With even greater interest we noted that the resolution was seconded by "Councillor Broadhurst, a blacksmith" (vide press.) This surely must be our old friend "Workman Jim "of Bank Quay fame, who is understood to have shaken the dust of the forge from his feet in favour of the pleasanter paths of paid politics. With what anguish must he have seen the resolution withdrawn!</p>
        <p>The Secretary of the Eighty Club has asked us to remind those of our readers who are on the committees of Political or Literary Societies of the work of the Eighty Club. The club consists of a number of gentlemen willing to assist the Liberal cause by speak- ing at local clubs, debating societies, or public meetings, as well as by lecturing on subjects of current political interest. No fee whatever is charged. In view of the forthcoming Home Rule Bill, the club is preparing to send speakers not only to large meetings, but also to ward meetings and to large village meetings. Letters should be directed to the Secretary of the Eighty Club, at 3, Hare Court, Temple, London.</p>
        <p>The resignation of Mr:Balfour brings to a close the hold on the leadership of the Con- servative Party which the Cecil family have possessed for thirty years. On the death of Lord Beaconsfield in 1881 the leadership passed to the man to whom he had sneeringly referred as a " master of gibes and !IMO and jeers." Lord Salisbury handed on the position to his nephew in 1902, and Mr. Balfour has led the party until to-day. IWe have been informed says the" Daily News " by a Tory journal that both Lord Hugh Cecil and Lord Robert Cecil will support Mr. Bonar Law, and it is certain that unless and until Mr. Balfour returns to fill his old place there will no longer be a Cecil at the head of Conservative affairs in this country. _ _</p>
        <p>• • Mr. Bonar Law's leadership has opened with a particularly uninspiring speech. One cannot help but sympathise with him. He has not Mr. Balfour's dialectical skill, he has no capacity for placing his proposals on a lofty plane of thought, and he has no proposals but those which have become musty with being in stock so long. All Mr. Law has to offer for the inspiration of the great Tory Party are Tariff Reform (of course), opposition to Home Rule, and the repairing of THE CONSTITUTION which has become his party's great concern since the Liberal Government ventured to bring it up-to-date. Not much, is it, to quench the thirst of politicians who have been out in the wilderness so long?</p>
        <p>The Anti-Socialist and the Tariff Reformer seem to be tarred with the same brush. At least that was the impression of the bulk of the audience which assembled to hear and criticise the Anti-Socialist lecture on Tuesday, notwithstanding the strenuous effort of Mr. Coleman to treat his subject from a non-party basis. It was when the questioners got to work that his armour was pierced, and the real colour detected under- neath. For sweated labour and under- cutting he advocated Tariff Reform as the remedy, and when asked for a definition of "The State," he replied with true Tory readiness that " John Redmond was the State." The Socialists are promising them- selves some rare fun when the second lecture of the series takes place.</p>
        <p>An American professor says that commentaries on Browning are mostly like foghorns—they proclaim the existence of the fog but do not disperse it This reminds the "Edinburgh Dispatch" of the story of Douglas Jerrold, who recovering from an illness, was terrified that he had lost his reason because he could not understand a word of " Sordello." Mrs. Carlyle read that poem through without, as she said, being able to make out whether Sordello was a' man, or a city or a book. Tennyson tackled it, but declared in blankness of spirit that there were only two lines he understood, and both were lies. They were the opening and the closing lines—" Who will may hear Sordello's story told," and " Who would has heard Sordello's story told." But it was an American editor who most neatly proclaimed the poet's obscurity. "Mr. Browning," he wrote, "has declined to furnish us with a poem in exchange for a thousand dollars. We find ourselves more than ever unable to understand Mr. Browning." * * *</p>
        <p>Thursday was the centenary of the birth of John Bright, and it may not be inappro- priate to quote from a little booklet edited by Cecil Wedmore, which gives a selection of some of the great statesman's utterances. Here is one: I believe (with my friend W. E. Forster) in the moral government of the world, that the moral law was not written for men alone in their ! individual character, but that it was written as well for nations, and for nations great as that of which we are citizens. If nations reject and I deride that law, there is a penalty which will inevitably- follow. . . . We have the moral law to guide us, and only so far as we walk by that guidance can we be permanently a nation, or our people a happy Eeople. "I do not think, he once said, "that examples taken from pagan sanguinary Rome are proper models for the imitation of a Christian country." * •</p>
        <p>One quotation is of -special interest at the moment: "There comes a time when the most zealous politicans may retire without blame." Here are some other brief sayings : The Sovereign may appoint a Minister, but royalty cannot make a statesman. Can the cats wisely and judiciously legislate for the mice ? The foundation of revolution has in every country been laid by those who preteaded to be especially Conservative. The Tory party is the turbulent party of this nation. Many people can understand the question of tales who cannot understand any other question. Another remark of Mr. Bright's wa3 : "Industry has no chance in the turmoil of a revolution."</p>
        <p>We know the name of the new Unionist leader, but we don't know, in England any- way, how to pronounce it, just as we always mis-pronounced the name of his predecessor. The only man who ever pronounced Mr. Balfour's name correctly in my hearing, writes a Scottish correspondent, was his rival, the late Sir Henry Campell-Bannerman who called it Balfoor. Now (says the" Man- chester Guardian ") we are talking of 80-nar Law, but the man from whom Mr. Bonar Law derives the first part of his name was Horatius Boner, the Scottish Presbyterian minister and hymn-writer, and in his own country " Bonar ' is pronounced Bonner. *4*</p>
        <p>The future of the Empire, not many years hence, is going to be decided not by men but by women.--Eari of Meath, to the British Girls' Patriotic League. England will never see industrial peace, to say nothing of national efficiency, so long as the present iunsatisfactory labour conditions last.—Sir Henry Norman, at Blackburn. Though legislation cannot do everthing it can remove a temptation and thus assist men to walk straight and keep sober.—Bishop of Liverpool, at Liverpool. Low conditions of housing mean low health, low thinking, low living and unem- ployment in after years.—Miss Cochrane, at , Exeter.</p>
        <p>LOCAL PARS.</p>
        <p>Museum Committee's Lecture.</p>
        <p>A leciure entitled " The Craftsman and his work " will he delivered by Mr. Henry Ogden in the Masonic Hall, Bold-street this (Saturday) evening. Wycliffe Church Sale of Work.</p>
        <p>X sale of work in aid of the Church Funds of Wycliffe Congregational Church will be held in the Memorial Hall on Thursday, the 39th inst. The opening ceremony will be performed by Mrs. A. H. Crogield. Women's Sufitage. • - • -- • •</p>
        <p>w- A public meet tog under the auspices of the National Union of Women's Suffrag, Societies is announced to be held in the Drill Ball at Frodsham on Wednesday, the 29th inst. The society is non-party and non- militant. The speakers will be Lady Frances Balfour and Miss Deakin, the chair t, be occupied by Miss Rathbone. Palace aad Hippodrome. • . .</p>
        <p>A capital programme is promised for next week. Frank Elliston and Co., described as "three and a fool "should be responsible for a good deal of hilarity as also should Adan, n 4 Ward, the eccentric comedians, both these items jointly holding the premier place on the bill. The other turnazill be :- 31teter James Garnett, the mar.velflous by !7loiphonist,; W. a. Raby, Domodion ; Yigoranto Trio, acrobats Ac ; Yorkshire Rustics, in their original sketch " Yokels in school"; Moran and Tingley, novelty upside-down dancers; and Cissie Thompson, comedienne. AwriinAcus STOVES, Ecomonical, clean, ch,erful Price from 97/6.—J. G. GARLAND, 51, to 55, Bridge at. Warrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Canonry for Former Warrington Curate. During a public luncheon after a church dedication at Grimsby, the Bishop of Lincoln offered a vacant prebendary stall at Lincoln Cathedral to the Rev. A. A. Markham, vicar of • Grimsby,—Canon Markham, who was taken by surprise, expressed his gratitude, Canon Markham was formerty curate at Warringtom Pariah Church, and had charge of St. Clement's Mission, where his gocd work gained him the affectionate regard of the congregation. " °SRAM " AND " TANTALCM " LAmes.—Agent. J. S GARLAND, 51, to 55, Bridge St., Warrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>_ . The Thomas Morris Charity. This Charity which was left by the late Mr. Thcmasl Morris, the former Manager of Bewsey Forge, was again distributed for the 15th time, at the office of Mr. Arthur Browne, Solicitor, in Sankey street on Friday week last. The trustees who are Canon Willis, Father Wihon, the Rev. John Yonge, and Mr. Arthur Browne, met a few days before when they found 216 applications made by respectable old women over 00 years of age, inhabitants of the Borough of Warrington, Lot in teceipt of parochial relief, but they could only select I 68 old women for blankets, and 23 old I women for the red flannel petticoats owing to both lots absorbing the annual income of the Charity which amounts to a little over !126 per alumni, although the remaining 120 applicants were almost all deserving eases, and strongly recommended by ministers of all denominations, or other leading men of the town. The petticoats were supplied by Mrs. Joseph Harrison, of the Charity Investigation Society. one Charity thereby helping another; and the blankets were supplied by Mr. Atkinson, draper, of Bridge-street, and Messrs. Pattison and Perkins, drapers, of Sankey-street. The Charity is conducted by Messrs. Arthur Browne and Co. and their office stall, free of cost, eacept7postages.</p>
        <p>- - - Co-operative Society's " At Home." Under the auspices of the Education Committee of the Warrington Equitable Industrial Co-operative Society Ltd., an " At Home" was held at the Empire Hall on Thursday evening. About 550 members attended and an excellent programme, consisting of a whist drive, concert and dance was gone through and thoroughly enjoyed. This "At Home "is part of the I programme prepared by the Education Committee of this Brciety who have an excellent object in view in providing through the winter months a series of concerts and the large attendance at the spacious hall is ample evidence that their efforts are being appreciated. About SO tables were requisitioned for the purpoie of the whist drive during the progress o which an excellent concert was provided by the follow- ing capable artists : Miss Sara Silcock, Messrs T Humphries, J Newnes (humorist), and Albert Trow (accompanist) At the close of the drive the prize winners were announced as follows —Ladies:: 1,</p>
        <p>Miss A Woods (four bottled cruet) • 2, Miss Creamer, (small finished timepiece) ; 3, Miss J B Brown, (pair of large guilded photo frames). Gentlemen : 1, Ur Rigby (easy wicker chair); 2, Mr Taylor (fern stand) ; 3, Mr C Hampson (Stropp's razor). Dancing was afterwards indulged in until the early hours of yesterday morning, to music supplied by ths Empire Orebeitra.</p>
        <p>" Personal Impressions of Richard Le Gallienne. This was the subject of a highly interesting paper read before the members of the Literary and Philo- sophical Society in the Masonic Hall, Bold-street, on Monday evening by Alderman Bennett. Ile described the beginning of his acquaintance with the poet,which ripened into a deep friendship. Le Glallienne was, he said, a singularly handsome fellow, with a face that reminded one of Shelley, refined and dreamy, perhaps a little sensuous, and framed in a poetic bead of hair. His voice was musical, his manner charming, and he felt instinctively at tbeir first meeting that they would like each other and would be god friends. The "memorable," deeply interesting and intellectual meetings of the poet with several Warringtonians— ladies and gentlemen—some living, some dead, were described, as well as the subsequent career of Mr. Le Gallienne, who is a native of Birkenhead. Le Gallienne, who was " born to be a poet, not a mere</p>
        <p>accountant," gave up his com;nerCial career and went to London as literary secretary to the great tragedian, Wilson Barrett. with a sort of veiled sug- gestion that he was ultimately to write his life. The poet's career after leaving Wilson Barrett was traced to the time when his photograph began to appear in all the literary papers. In 1802 he published his "English Poems." The edition was sold out upon the date of publication, and there were not wanting those who regarded Le Oallienne as the "brightest rising star in all the literary firmament," (Applause.) No literary name was then more frequently upon men's lips, and no writer had a wider or a more enthusiastic following among the really cultured net. The poet's subsequent career was traced, and the Alderman concluded with the observation : " I still love him and cherish lofty hopes for him. There is an honourable niche remaining for him in the temple of English petry if he will only do the best that in him lies." Applause.) —On the motion of Mr. A. J. Jolley, seconded by Mr. Dimmelow, and supported by Mr. Lofthouee,Adderman Bennett was warmly thanked for his paper. - The meeting was presided over by Mr. W. Silcock, president of the society.—The Hon. Secretary (Mr. Oswald Lofthouse) announced that the probable date of Miss Marie Corelli's visit to Warrington was Friday, February 2nd. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>Local Will. The will of the late Mr. Frederick George Taylor, solicitor, of Greenkiank, Manchester-road, Warrington, has been proved at i 1,1,2,54.1. I New Adult School.</p>
        <p>Au adult school lias been opened at Sankey Bridges and on Sunday last ths members listened to an address by Mr. Rupert Jones, on " Shakespeare." The subject was treated in a very able manner, and was appreciated. A short discussion followed. Mr. J. F. Awbery. -- • • - - -</p>
        <p>The condition or Mr. Awbery last Saturday gave rise to serious anxiety, inasmuch as a second specialist from Leeds bad to be called in on Sunday in consulta- tion with the specialist from Liverpool. Al ths time of going to press Mr. Awbery's condition was far from satisfactory.</p>
        <p>VACUUM CLEANERS free the home from duet. Price 2to 7 guineas. Try one on hire.—J. S. GARLAND, 51, In 55, Bridge st, Warrington.—Advt. Warrington Photographic Society. . .</p>
        <p>The cTemonstrattdc on Re-touching of Negatives arranged for Tuesday's weekly meeting by the Gevaert Co., was ably conducted by Mr. Bell, of Heywood. With skilful handling of diagrams, enlarged negative, and blackboard, the lecturer held the atten- tion of a large and appreciative audience for over an hour whilst he expounded the d.fferent strokes with the pencil, the handling of the knives, and all the minutiae which combine ta smooth and perfrct the crude work of the camera on our sitters. fie proved the excellence of the Tapers made by his firm by a great many examples of the highest class of porbaltare end picture-making both in P.0.P., bromides and platinum papers, one especial test being a b:omide enlargement toned with hot alum after being t don direct from the fixing bath. No harden- ing process had ben applied and yet not a blister appeared on the resulting print. The most interest- ing parts of the lecture were the bits which treated of homely apparatus devised by himself, quite inexpen- sive and so within the reach of anyone with a pair of hands with only one thumb each. He also showed us how saliva applied with the t Ingue has a special effect on photographic gelatine. He forgot, however, that we live in Warrington and see that name gelatine in its original stets passing through Winwick-street in carts, and so his recommendation is not likely to be adopte I. A hearty vote of thanks was accorded.</p>
        <p>CUTLERY, Rodgers celebrated knives and forks, Sm. —Agent, J. S. GARLAND, Ironmonger, Bridge at., Warrington.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>Warrington •Sociallst Society.</head>
        <p>Warrington •Sociallst Society.</p>
        <p>On Sunday last, under the auspices of the above society, Councill r Will Philbps, of Manchester, delivered an address entitled " The Development of Socialism in Great Britain." Taking as his starting point the pre-Rom in period, where everything within the Tribal Commune was enjoyed with corn, measure of equality : he outlined the development of the working class, from feudalism and Ail ienag3 on to copyholder or eteuant farmer ; front the hindicraft system of small production an i manufacture up to the present day capitalist method of production. He pointed out very clearly how the old Saxon free man was reduced to serfdom by the Normans under William, and how they were compelled to work so many darn upon the demensue of their Superior. Owing to the Black Death of 134i-40 the labourers were decreased and labour became seance. Taking advantage of this shortage of lab.eir the villeins rose under the It adership of Wat Tyler, Jack Straw and John Ball, and although the rising was ' stir:pressed, the abominable conditions of villeinage disappeared with the 14th Century. The speaker pointed out that the workers of that time, just as the workers of to-day were too easily satisfied with promises which rarely develops into any mutual advantage. Continuing, he pointed out how the common people hod been robbed of the land and the peasants driven off in countless multitudes to make room for sheep farming, which at that period was very profitable. These along with the thousands of retainers and men at arms which were turned adrift by the feudal barons at 1.11;f1 time, formed a new slave class. The whip with which the chattel slave was scourged was exchanged for the whip of want and hunger, by which the capitalist drives the workers in flocks to the factory gate. He urged the workers t use their political power judiciously, to obtain control of the legislative machine and win back that economic power upon which depended the future happiness of the people. FIRE GRATES. If you want • new grate our large and varied stook will interest you.—J. S. GARLAND Bridge St., Warrington.—Advt. Bakers at Dinner.</p>
        <p>The Warringmn branch of the Amalgamated Union of Operative Bakers and Confectioners, held their eighth annual dinner and smoking concert at Atkinson's Cafe, on Saturday evening. ALllilt 120 members and lady friends were present. The smoking concert was presided over by Alderman James Evans (chairman of the branch) who was supported by Mr Walter Rathbone 'distmict secre- tary), Councillor Steel (solicitor of the branch), Mr Lionel Fairclough and Mr J Junes (representing the millers), Mr John Greeley (district chairman),lfr W Creighton (branch chairman), Mr C F Eaton (branch secretary), Mr T Onitram (branch treasurer), and Mr W Barnes (district treasurer). The Chairman said they were living in days when competition was keen, when theme was a tendency to lessen labour, and when the cheapening of production was being brought into operation. In the larger bakeries—not in their onm town only --where machinery was used, the old-tashioned methods were set aside, and the opportunity of getting a fair remuneration fer labour was passing away. They always had the man who was looking for the cheapest labour. Personally he believed in paying and being paid. (Applause.) The toast of " The Warrington Branch " was proposed by Mr. W. Creighton and acknowledged by Mr. John Greeley. Mr. W. Rathbone submitted the health of "Millers' Yeast Merchants and kindred trades" Mr. Lionel Fairclough and Mr D J Kinnish (United Yeast Co Ltd) responded--" Our Guests" was proposed by Mr W Gregson and responded to by Councillor Steel who said public opinion was a great factor in labour questions and the people had the settling of disputes in their own hands. Other toasts were : ' The Ladies " proposed by Mr C F Eaton and acknowledged by Mr. F. Bancroft and " The Chairman "proposed by Councillor Steel. A musical programme was gone through by Messrs. R. Seariabrick,J Hannan, W. Eamhbone, W. Eibelley, A. Pemberton, and others. Mr. W. Barnes accompanied. Dancing was afterwards eng wed in.</p>
        <p>Many amusing stories were told of the late Mr. Joseph Pulitzer,the millionaire American newspaper owner. The imposing retinae— consisting of his physician, secretary, com- panion, groom, valet, masseur, and so on— with which he travelled once gave rise to a characteristic incident. " Who is that foreign prince they have at the— Hotel?" inquired a stranger one day. " Wall," responded the Yankee addressed, "I guess you mean that long-legged Hungarian that owns the" World." "He looks as if be did," was the stranger's comment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>TORY MEETING LAST NIGHT.</head>
        <p>TORY MEETING LAST NIGHT.</p>
        <p>MR. HAROLD SMITH'S BITTER ATTACK ON THE GOVERNMENT.</p>
        <p>RUDE REFERENCES TO MR CROSFIELD.</p>
        <p>Mr. HAROLD SMITIT, M.P., and Lord WmArs were the principal speakers at a public meeting held in he Parr Hall last evening under the auspices of the Junior Conservative Association. Mr. E. J. Hall presided over a large attendance. Mr. HAROLD Su rru, who moved a resolution express- ing deep regret at the resignation of Mr. Balfour and congratulating Mr. Bomar Law on succeeding to the leadership, was in his most vituperative mood and delivered himself of an attack on his opponents sliich for strength of language even Mr. Smith has never excelled.</p>
        <p>Be opened his speech with an expression of pious ,regret at Mr. Balfour's reaignation. Greafer brain and greater sincerity, be believed, was never found in any statesman within oar time. \Vele ming</p>
        <p>VTR A,'EsRION oh' MR IM.SAR LOW, he said that seldom in the annals of a party could it congratulate itself so warmly and sincerely on being led by a man whose lucidity of thought and wonder- ful grasp of all subjects —political and commercial— was 5) cert9i9 end 1.117;:i.g.0 as Riley torrid in M. Boner Law. lie was convinced that during the last ten years the Conservative party had never been more united, determined or more justly hopeful of coming into that kingdom which assuredly they would come hip before many months had passed. (Applause). Mr. Smini then embarked upon a tirade against the Government and its works. la the political history of the last fifty years there was</p>
        <p>NO SUAII EXAMPLE OF DUI.LICITY AND FRAUD as we had expvienced at the hands of the Liberal Government during tlyi last five or sin years. They had duped and defrauded the British electorate from the very mmient they won the great battle of 1906. The "People's Budget" Was heralded on every R tclical platform by mendacity, rancour and platform filth such as had never ben known in the history of peities. The Budget they were told, was to bring refreshing fruits to the mouths of the multitude and sunshine into the lives of the poor. Nearly half a mil:ion of money had been spent in collecting the land taxes to obtain 11200 for the Exchequer. That Ards equivalent to this : If any man in that room would spend £1 per day for 365 days, at the end of the year he might gather LI for himself. (Laughter.) " I don't want to be too hopeful or optimistic.' said Mr. Smith, " but I dare say the grandchildren of some of the youngest of my audience may see the day when every pound spent on the land taxes may bring half-a- sovereign to the exchequer. (laughter.) Was th Jr° ever such a mountain in such travail which produced such a MOUS) ? (Laughter and applause.) That is f rand number one."</p>
        <p>Fraud number two, continued Mr. Smith, was the House of I.m.ds cry. The Radicals went about</p>
        <p>lIATIZ ED by pointing oat the monstrosity of the hereditary system. He (Mr. Smith) had never defended the principle that because a min was the son of his father he should for that reason only have the right to legislate. 1:pon every hoarding appeared pictures of of toothless, dyspeptic old gantlemea with coronets. (Laughter.) 'Vet there tyss not to-day one less hereditary Peer in the House of Lords but there were Letup more. (Applause.) When they made that appeal, particularly to the unthinking and ignorant, the Government had not the slightest inten- tion of reducing the number of hereditary Peers—if they had, they had not a majority to reform the House of Lords. (Applause.) The Parliament Bill—" fraud No. 3 "—Mr. Smith characterised as a Bill to enable his Majesty's advisers to place upon the Statute Book any measure they liked without the inconvenience of having to consult the people. That was an outrage on the first principles of democracy. The various sections of the House of Commons were united not by agreement upon any great measure but only upon two things— one to defeat the Tory party, and:the other to enable each section to hang together to get Bills on the statute book Bills which would not pass if each member voted according to hi, consciences The political Nonconformists were willisg to betral their Nonconformist brethren in Ireland in order t ) obtain Mr. John Redmond's support of a Bill to rob English schools and English funds. The orders in the Ilouse of Commons now were "Clear the decks for Home Rule." The orders were not those of England's Prime Minister but of</p>
        <p>IT,ML nit Tat, tit —m R. Jam: e Eit Freedom of speech, the liberty of the House of Commons and of the people were being sacrificed on the alt kr of Home Rule, and important measures were being rushed through without adequate debate. "It was Home Rule " ,leelared Mr. Smith that finished Mr. Gladstone. It was Home Ruls that smashed Parnell, and it will be Home Rule which will finish Mr. Asquith and am eh Ids majority." (Applause). It was denianded that we should spend millions a year in order to hand Ireland over to the tyranny of those men who had for years past e‘ieered Englatde etetnies and been disloyal to our King and Crown. '' My persanal view "added Mr. Saint is that the</p>
        <p>110110 00% ERNME‘T To It is doomed because it lamed up of sections. none of which agrees with any other. flay after day and week after week one notices in the House of Commons a growing dissatisfaction on the part of one section with the other. We must Ite ready to take titer place whenever the opportunity occurs and I believe that oppsrtneity will come sooner than even the greatest optimist in this room helieveti.'' (Applause.) On the question of tariff reform Mr. Smith referred to a recent speech by Mr. A. 11. Crovfield in which he alluded to the way in which the English soap ' trade had held its own under Free Trade. Against that Mr. Smith made much of a recent speech by Mr. Barrett of Pears Ltd., justifying the erection by that firm of a factory in America on the ground of the unfair competition to which they were subjected by the Americans.</p>
        <p>11R 111)SFIE11,,</p>
        <p>in one of those nathetic attempts he vu making to win back the confidence of the people of Warrington in his lualificatione to represent them, said he could not poen,ibly. uaderetand why so many women should jnin the AVOIII,iI.I, Tariff Reform League. '' Of course." .i.I Mr Sitath. " no one has ever suggested that Mr. Crosfiebi is capable of understanding that laughter and it is because be is not capable of understand- it that be now has that in-relined leisure for golf which the electors of Warrington are not going to deprive him of. 'Laughter.) He has never attempted to understand it. Ile has looked at Free Trade leaflets, and Free Trade only, and has never endeavoured to look at the question i the light of a business questioo affecting the home and happiness of the working classes oi England (Applause).</p>
