      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>BOOTS ISki CHI EA 1 STS</head>
        <p>BOOTS ISki CHI EA 1 STS</p>
        <p>BUSINESS BY POST. MONEY IMMEDIATELY LENT FROM £lO TO £l,OOO.</p>
        <p>A Gentleman having a considerable amount of spare Capital is prepared to make Immediate Advances to Ladies, Clergymen, Gentlemen, Farmers, Shopkeepers, and any Respectable Householders at a moderate rate of Interest on Note of Hand alone. Business can be completed entirely by pnst if desired. GENUINE TRADE BILLS DiSCONTINUED. STRAIGHT- FORWARD DEAIANG AND STRICT PRIVACY GUARANTEED. NO PRELIMINARY FEES CHARGED. NO BILLS OF SALE TAKEN. Write, stating full particulars and requirements to</p>
        <p>- J. GRlFFlN,Nfinories Chambers,Minories,Birmingbam. N.B.—Don't pay Fees or Travelling Expenses, sod don't give a Bill of Sale before appiying to me.</p>
        <p>At the Borough Court on Tueeclty before Counoilbw 11. Roberta and other magistra•ea, Huth Miller, 7, Bartlett-street, Wavertree, Liverpool, was charged on command a ith collecting charitable contributions under false pretence.. Mr. W. B. Forshaw, solicitor, Warrington defended, ea.' prisoner pleaded not guilty' The Chief Constable outlined the facts which showed that the defendant came to Warrington a weak ago and represented herself as a collector on behalf of the Home for Destitute Children in Lower Breek-rosd, Tuebrook. She called upon two clerks at the Central Station, and obtained two small turns of money. Later she visited the police station and asked for an interview with the Chief Constable. Ile (Mr. Nicholls) saw the defendant, who made an application for the renewal of a subscription towards the home. Not being satisfied with her credentials, he refused to rensw the subscrip- tion, and subsequently elicted the information that she was not at preeent collecting for the home, but for her salary. He warned her not to continue her collection in Warrington, but later she was seen by a police officer to call upon two local shopkeepers. John George Thompson, secretary of the home, said defendant was engaged by him as a collector until two sears ago, when she was discharged. One of the reasons for the dismissal nag on account of her physical unfit- nem Ile requested her to return the official book, but she failed to do no. Defendant had no right, and the home did not allow anyone to collect for her salary. In reply to the chairman witness added that it was usual for their collectors to deduct their wages and expense' from the amount collected. The Chairman: A very bad plan. Mr. Forebear' for the defence, contended that Miss Miller had been suffering from neurasthenia, and did not realise what she had done. Iler mental condition accounted for her conduct, and negatived any intention to defraud. The Chairman, addressing Mr. Thompson, said the society was not altogether free from blame in allowing the collectors to deduct their salary and expenses from the contributions. It was a loose way of doing business,</p>
        <p>Mr. Thompson : It is the usual custom for societies In Liverpool. The Chief Constable said that on several occasions during the last four years the defendant had been cautioned by the police in Liverpool for collecting for bogus charities. From inquiries the Burnley police had ascertained that the defendant had been courting a retired slogger in Burnley, whom she met while on holiday, but the engagement was discontinued. She threatened to sue him for breach of promise, and the man gave her .e 5 as a settlement. Another threat was that she would commit suicide on Lis doorstep. The slogger was a man with fourteen children. On another occasion she inserted a notice of her death in a Liver- pool paper, and the slogger, on going to Liverpool, found her very much alive. (Laughter). Mr. Forshaw : That further supports my plea of her mental deficiency. The Chairman said the Bench were quite satisfied that it was very desirable that the young lady should not have an opportunity of continuing thin kind of conduct She would have to go to gaol for one month with hard labour. .</p>
        <p>—ii;;*defeodant was removed below, loudly protesting her innocence. CHURCH WALL DAMAGED BY BOYS. A WARNING.</p>
        <p>At a Juvenile Court held on Thursday, before Mr 3 Rymer Young and other magistrates, four boys named Samuel \Velmaley (12), 20, Cyril-street ; Alfred Howard (12), 19, Clegge-stteet I Richard T Wycherley (11), 19, Earle-street ; and Gilbert Armitage (II), 112, Orford. lane, were all charged with doing wilful damage to the wall of St. Benedict's Church on the 10th inst.—All the defendanta pleaded guilty. Mr C H Unsworth, who prosecuted, said that for a considerable time annoyance had been caused by boys of that neighbourhood, and many windows had been broken by them. On the date in question the defendants were observed to go down Earle-street each with a pole in hand as if imitating boy scouts. With the poles they knocked off the coping stones of the wall which surrounds this church for a distance of nearly 30 yards. Three stones fell on the inside of the wall and many of them were broken. The damage was estimated at EI. The police were informed and Detective Holland ascertained that the boys were con- cerned in the matter. This was a most serious matter and although they did not wish to press the case they hoped the Bench would so deal with it as to act as a warning that property was not to be destroyed in this manner.</p>
        <p>The Chairman of the Bench said that it was monstrous hen they thought of the damage that was being done in different parts of the town. Although they swished to take a view of leniency they had no alternative but to inflict a tine of les. including costa, each, and hoped that would prove a warning.</p>
        <p>Corner SANKEY St. and BRIDGE St. 126 BRIDGE St, Warrington.</p>
        <p>, i(in th ge - OURTS -1</p>
        <p>BOGUS COLLECTOR IN TROUBLE. A PLEA OF MENTAL DEFICIENCY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>Borough Police Court</head>
        <p>Borough Police Court</p>
        <p>SATI RDAY.—llefore" Mee 3t6 A J Jolley (in the chair) and T. Pemberton.</p>
        <p>A BRUTAL HUswalarri.—Paul Iletinesey, labourer, 27, Portland-street, was charged with committing an aggravated assault upna his wife on the previous night. He pleaded guilty.—Chief Inspector Williamson said the previous day the defendant went to breakfast at 8.30, in a bad temper, ard his wife was obliged to leave the house to escape bin violence. Later in the day she returned, and prepared his dinner, but was afraid to remain. In the evening, when his wife was at home, he struck her a violent blow oe the side of the face, and knocked her to the floor. While she lay there he took hold of her hair, and lifted her on the sofa and kickel her several times about the legs. She dropped tbebaby which she had in her arms, and fell on the floor. He again kicked her violently and Mel butted her in the face with his bead. Complainant had to be taken to the Inflrmary.—liennesey was sent to prison for three months.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.—Before the Mayor (in the chair). Aldermen Smethurst and James Evans, Messrs S Wright, H Thornton, W Roberts, W Furness and G Pilling. MISCELLANICOUS —Mere were no lees than twelve case, of drunkenness with disorderly behaviour beard at this court and also one case of begging and one case of peddling without a certificat•.</p>
        <p>6SDAY.—Before Councillors H Roherls sod C Jack.on Holmes, Messrs J Rymer Young and W Richards' a.</p>
        <p>Ex-SOLI/16R DISCHARGED.—James Goodwin, of no settled abode, wav charged on remand with stealing a pocket knife, the property of some person unknown. The Chief Constable said that the Cave was adjourned to see if the owner of the knife could be found and to ascertain if the prisoner was a deserter from the army. In the first instance their inquiries had proved fruitless. They had that morning received a letter to the effect that the prisoner had enlisted in the ermy under, false name but he had been discharged and they did wish to take any proceedings against him. The Bench discharged the prisoner. I TIIURSDAY.—Before Messrs. J. Rymer Yonng (in the chair) W Roberts, E Woods, W T Williams and J Upson.</p>
        <p>ALLEGED ATTEM PT AT SHOPRREAKING Riley, IS, Devonshire-street, Manchester, a seaman, was charged with unlawfully trying to enter the lock-up shop 1, Lower Wash-lane, occupied by Samuel Leth- bridge. The Chief Constable add about. quarter to twelve the prevous night a Mr. Taylor residing at 649, Knutaford-road heard a noise in the above premises which are situated at the rear of his house. Be after- wards beard a crash of glass. He informed the occupiers lof the shop and the prisoner when questioned stated that he was looking for somewhere to sleep. The putty had been removed from one of the windows and it is supposed that in endeavouring to remove the glass from the frame the prisoner dropped it. Later the same night Inspector Hodgkins observed the prisoner acting is a suspicious manner and not being able to , give a satisfactory account of his movements he wan taken to the Police Station. When charged he admitted trying to break into the shop.—The case was adjourned for saves days on the application of the Chief Constable, for further inquiries to be made. FRlDAY—Before the. Mayor (in the chair) Aldermen Smethurst, l'ierpoint and James Evans, Councillor Wilkinson. Messrs S Wright, W Furness, It Thornton and G Pilling. ARMY DESERTER CH ARC F.D.—Albert Rygor, was charged with being a deserter from the Lancashire Ft:milers at Bury. It was stated that the prisoner surrendered Monett' to P.C. Wood bnd stated that he bad been away from his regiment a week.—Pritioner wan handed over to the Military escort.</p>
        <p>WI FE DEsERTIoN.—John Clarke, 19, Ellesmere-street waa summoned L his wife Mary Ann Clarke for desertion and a separation order with 43. a week MU made against him, while a similar summons was brought against William Dickinson, 19, Ilrook-street by his wife Mary Jane Dickinson and was adjourned for 4 weeks to see if the parties could be brought togethe-.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <p>The Examiner Saturday November 18, 1911</p>
