      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>State and Religion.</head>
        <p>The State and Religion.</p>
        <p>Speaking at Crewe, on Sunday, the Dean of Man. cheater, litshop Welldon, said that be did not think the State was entitled or competent to choose out one religion and force it upon the people. The Church of England had too often displayed the spirit of exclusiye- ness, and this was false to the continuance of the National Church. He welcomed Nonconformists as brothers. The future force iof Christianity should be knitted together, and there should be lens ill-feeling, growling, and backbiting. They were wasting time by quarrelling amongst themselves. They must light shoulder to shoulder, and do all possible in accelerating the union of the several churches.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>Church Lads' Brigade.</head>
        <p>Church Lads' Brigade.</p>
        <p>I The Warrington Bittalion of the Church Lads I Brigade celebrated its twentieth anniversary on Sunday bye church parade and united aervice at Christ Church, Padgate. Colonel Appleton was in command. The battalion was led by the bugle band of St. Peal's company. The service was tsken by the RIV. G. W. Dickinson, vicar of Padgate, and Canon Willis read the lesion. The Rev. W. Bracecomp in the course of a powerful addresb, urged those preleut to live up to the ideals for which tha Brigade stood. lie spoke of the temptations of gambling, crooking, swearing and impurity V. which young persons were subjected, but begged them to remember their professions and quit themselves like men. There was an attendance lot 132, and the officers present included Lieutenant-Colonel Appleton, Major Brookes,aad Adj atant-Cap: in Sankey.</p>
        <p>Ifixture &amp;kw. ....... 4 Fifteenth 21t.. Mitue 3 rks. Twelfth 2 2-3 Fifteenth 2ft. Principal 4 •Principal 4 Flute 4 (Jambe 8 &amp;Pelona' R</p>
        <p>Stop Diap 8 Vox Angelic* S 1 Open Diap 8 Libßch Gedacht 8 2 Opon Diap 8 • Open Diap 8 ripntra (iambs 16 Double Open Dist, 16 ( 110 IR ORGAN. PEDAL ORGAN. Como de limmetto Bft. Flute 8 Gemahorn 4 Violincello 8 Suite Flute 1 %Neiman 16 DuMinna 8 Bourdon 16 Clarabella 8 Open Diap 16 Open Dlap 8 Couplera.—Great to pedal, @well to ped., choir to pad., aw to art, Sc to oh, sw octave, as nub-octare, unison. off, Tremulemt.</p>
        <p>Mechanical accesiones.—Four pistons to greet, four to swell, two to choir, three to pedsl, four composition pedals to great, four to swell, balanced swell peiaL</p>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society. There was a fair attendance, on Sunday, Mr. Himsworth presided. As it was Temperance Sunday, the addresses, lesson, and prayer were appropriate to the OCCASIOO. The Chairman spoke of the evil of intemperance which left its mark upon all its victims, high as well as low. Mr. James Shaw gave the address Upon" The evil of intemperance," referring to national, municipal, religious, and individual aspects. Tee £140,000,000 spent on the drink could be put to much better use. He urged all present to make a stand for saving the 60,00 drunkards, brightening homes, and giving the country and the individual a setter chance In life. Let their influence itpon the lives of others ho felt for good. Mr. C. Cotton read the lesson and Mr. Blanc led the prayer. Mr. W. H. Payton presided at the organ. One new member WAS admitted. The Secretory made reference to the retirement of the Rev. J. Yong°, president of the society, and wished him and his wife many happy 3 ears.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>Emmanuel Church. Annual Sale of Work.</head>
        <p>Emmanuel Church. Annual Sale of Work.</p>
        <p>The congregation of Emmanuel (Reformed) Church of England, Bold-street, have this week been engaged in their annual effort to raise funds by meaes of a sale of work, to assist the work of the church and Sunday school, which is maintained entirely on a voluntary basis. The sale, which was opened on Wednesday afternoon, and continued until last evening had, as usual, been most energetically prepared for by the ladies and there was a capital army of many varieties of goods. The following presided at the stallsNo.l stall : Mrs Jaye, Mrs Lees and friends. No. 2 stall : Mrs A Gandy, Mrs Atherton and friends. No. 3 stall: (Refreshment) Miss M Booth and friends. No. 4 stall : The Sunday School Teachers. No. 5 stall : Gentlemen's stall. N 0.6 stall : (Flower sal fruit) Mrs J Bibby ani friends. No stall • Wit N Gandy and friends. . . .</p>
        <p>Tea— ii;roli;:- The sale was opened on Wednesday afternoon by Councillor R. Elenahall.</p>
        <p>(curate-in-ohs rge) said that as I they looked back upon the past year they could thank- fully testify and praise God that He had been with I them and that financially speaking, they had nothing to I complain of in comparison with previous years. God I had supplied all their needs and he believed that so long I as they served him faithfully and preached Jesus Christ I and the cleansing in His shed blood as the only way to eternal life He would tuner leave or tot-mike them. It had been said that there was no need for Emmanuel Church in Warriogton. He begged to differ and to assert that as the days went by the need increased for the evangelical Protestant effort of a church like theirs. They claimed to be true and loyal C hurchmen. They loved the I Church of England and were never beard to speak I against that Church unit should be, if it wale conducted in a truly evangelical spirit. All thley protested against —and they believed they were doing God's work ins, doing—was the spread of ritualistic practices and the inculcation of sacerdotal doctrines into the hearts and minds of the young. Apart from that, they wished " God speed " to every good work, but above all they claimed to be true and loyal Protestants, trio children of the Raformation. He knew their friends connected with other denominations made the same claim but in a special sense they at Emmanuel Church felt they had been culled by God to do His cork in their p irticular TVAy. Their services wine precisely the same as those of the Church of England, with the exaeption that they ware just the same as they were in the dais of their forefather, when they could go and worship Clod indeed in spirit and in truth. Last year's sale of work, after paying expenses, realised £123 ss. and this year they hoped to improve upon it. They had a great deal to contend with at th present time ; never had we known there to be so much sicknesi in the district as there was to-day. One sad thing he would like to touch upon was that one of the oldest me obers of their church (Mr. Joseph Parsons), one of the original band who left St. James' Church, was lying on a bed of sickness and prayers were esked on his behalf. Councillor Newhall said he had had many years experience of church work. Ile remembered very well the secession from St. James' Church the beginning of the work at Einmaouelfend with some little interest he had followed its progress for thirty years. He was lost in admiration for the strenuous, persistent and unflagging efforts of a relatively small and not very wealthy body, in the erection sad sustenance of their church and minister year after year. He knew some- thing of the toil and ceaseless isiclifice that underlay their work. The impulse beneath it was one of service for Him Who, bring rich, for our sake hecame poor. Any work, though apparently obscure, was gilded with its own gold and filled with its own fragrance, if (km for His sake.</p>
        <p>Ou the motion of Mr W. Atherton (warden), seconded by Mr. John Bibby (tecretary and treasurer), a vote of thanks was panned to Councillor Henshall and also to the Rev. W. Cooper for their presence. The pre ceeds for the day amounted to £47. The opening ceremony on Thursday was performed by the children of the Sunday school. The proceedings were commenced by the singing of a chorus of " Welcome " by the whole of the scholars. Master Reggie Wainwright, the chairman, delivered his speech in the unique form of a charming little song, while the "opener," Master Albert Lees, also expressed himself in song about the doings of " a tiny Church " whose members ' for right herd work are heroes grand:—The ceremony was curried out without a hitch, and reflocted much credit upon Miss Nellie Gandy, who had the training of the cuildrea in hand. Miss S. Lister was the accompanist.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>Rev. H. Ogden on " Nation v. Brewer."</head>
        <p>Rev. H. Ogden on " Nation v. Brewer."</p>
        <p>Preaching at Golborne-street Baptist Church on Sunday evening the Roy. H. Ogden (pastor) delivered a notable addreu on " Nation and Brewer " taking as his text, Luke xvii., 1-2. Suppose Christ were to come to England," be said " His first victim would be the Church—which would learn through a sharp rebuke that it had dose the things it ought not to have done, and left undone the things it ought to have done. Christ would be dumb in the face of the colossal curse of these blinds. Would He have ratter mercy on the Trade? No ! Tile nation would be more to Him than the Trade, and so it intone I " The preacher proceeded to argue that England's curse was not her poverty_ great as was that evil—but the blight of intemperance, which was a thrice-armed giant in the way. Time and time again he had seen homes ruined by drink. In • district where men were brutal and, where women had become mates of the beast, the public-house was every mars', neighbour. The Trade was a menace to the nation, and under a recent law, meant to be pro- tective, children were nursed while their mothers were cursed. Justice Grantham once said that three-fourths of the crime would never come before him were it not for drink. At its lowest estimate, abstinence paid. The abstaieer as such, might have no ideals, might be content with very trivial life, but one thing was assured, his wife and children bad a chance of , happineaa in life. Trade, might come and trades might ' go, but the IVltioll goes on for ever. And when this Trade imperilled the life of the people, it most he ground to powder by the millstones of God. The nation must live !</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>St. Nary's, Buttermarket-street</head>
        <p>St. Nary's, Buttermarket-street</p>
        <p>Sunday the 2Gth ins', will be an important day in the history of St. Mary's (R.C.) Church, Buttermarket- street. Special services will be held to celebrate the re-openin:r of the organ after its re-construction „by Messrs. Gray and Davison, of London, and the , occasion is being looked forward to with much interest by the congregation worshipping at this beautiful church. Th 3 interior of the building has been cle reed and decorated by Mews 1 Richardson and Son, Market Gate.</p>
        <p>The special preacher at the service will be the Rey. Father Bode, O.F.M. Mr. Wa'ter Wallington will preeide at the orgal and g:ve a recital after the evening err vice. . .</p>
        <p>Tho org—an has been re-built ;n such a manner aa not to obscure the oindovrs Tubular pneumatic action has been applied to all departments. A new open diapason of la,ge Neale has been added to the great organ, and an cit. flute to the pedal. IFICATIOS.</p>
        <p>SPEC]</p>
        <p>Great Orpn.emill Organ. Clariou 4ft. Clarion aft. Trumpet 4 Corsopeao 3</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>FASHIONABLE WEDDING AT LYMM.</head>
        <p>FASHIONABLE WEDDING AT LYMM.</p>
        <p>An interesting marliage was e,leranized on Tuesday in the Lyinm Wesleyan Church between the Rev. E. Douglas Jackson BA, Wesleyan minister of Peterborough, and Miss Mary Hawkins Pawlyn a daughter of the Rev. J. Hawkins and Mrs. Pawlyn of The Hamlet, Lymm. The father of the bride and the Rev. A. John Hornabrook (ex-president of the Wesleyan Conference) were the officiating ministers. The bride looked charming in white satin dress beautifully embroidered tulle veil and wreath of orange blossom, her ornaments being a brooch the gift of the bridegroom and pearl pendant and chain the gift of her aunt. She carried a bouquet of white chrysanthemums and was attended by her youngest sister as her only bridesmaid. The bride's eldest brother Mr. J. Hawkins Pawlyn acted as best man. Mr. H. S. Brown presided at the organ. After the ceremony the guests adjourned to The Hamlet and later in the day the Rev. E. D. and Mrs. Jackson left for Ilkley. Many valuable presents were received, a notable one being a beautiful painting the gift of the Rev. John S. and Mrs. Pawlyn the uncle and aunt of the br:de, the painter being Miss Dora Pawlyn, an artist cousin.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>ANTI-SOCIALISM.</head>
        <p>ANTI-SOCIALISM.</p>
        <p>Is it Another Name for Toryism?</p>
        <p>LECTURER HECKLED.</p>
        <p>Judging by the attendance at the first of a course of three lectures promoted by the Anti-Socialist Union held in the NIOSODie Hall on Tuesday evening, War- rington as a whole is not alarmed at the growth of Socialism. the audience was very sparse and mainly comprised &amp; number of leading local Socialists who gave an attentive hearing to the lecturer and at the close fired off a number of quations. In the absence of Mr. C. A. Swanton, who was indisposed, the lecture was given by Mr. E. J. Coleman, who dealt with "Capital and its nature and function." The chair was occupied by Mr. J. Fulton-Smith. The LECTURIR, at the outset, said it was often pointed out by Socialist lecturers thnt under a Socialist regime the workers would be better off. He did not think they would, because if they worked fewer hours and received the same reward they were giving of their best ability and only rocAsiving according to their needs, which meant there would be less production and less distribution. The object of Socialism was to get rid of the capitalist and to retain the capital. Capital was that portion of wealth which was net snide to pro- duce further wealth and wealth was labour, manually, mentally, and intelligently, applied to national resources. The Socialists admitted that labour was entitled to its wage but did not admit that land was entitled to rent or that capital was entitled to interest. If Labour created the whole of the wealth why iu the nime of common sense did not the Socialists organise the vast army of unemployed and by capitalising a portion of the salary of the Socialist members of Parliament they would have a fine harvest. Capital could be divided into two classes—fixed capital which could not be removed and floating capital which was essential for the upkeep of fixed capital. The two chief agents of production were land and labour. If they wished to become a capitalist they must practice abstinence,thtift and self-denial. In conserprence of tha Socialist legislation during the hurt five years 750 millions of capital had gone abroad. In view of the general unrest is this country men would be fools to invest that at the present time. Dealing with the law of supply a demendon his opinion there was no surplus produation— supply created a demand. Syndicalism was being advocated up and down the country ; he contended that by syndicalism they were going to have &amp;universal strike which would result in diasster and labour would suffer for more than capital, which %mild be invested abroad. It was going at an alarming extent and men and women who bad been thrifty were following toe capital, and it followed that their productions entered into competition with the people in the country. The problem of to-day was either work or revolution. The Socialist saw that the working man desired work. The Right to Work Bill with a 30s. minimum weekly wage was impracticable. It meant that every municipality would be bound to had work for the workers and i.s n °dose- quence it would enter into competition with the people in the district. He maintained that the trouble in the industrial world had been brought about by tie agita- tion carried on by the Socialists. Something would. have to be done. Capital was entitled to its reward He had never said that Labour wan not entitled to its share. He believed in a fair day's work for a fair day's pay. The solution in bin opinion was the system of co-partnership which he thought would work admirably. The Socialists preached the gospel of despair whilst he advocated the doctrine of hope, that men and women should rely upon their own efforts and not on the State. (Applause). Questions having been invited the lecturer was asked whether he inferred that when a capitalist had been crushed it was due to a lack of brains, or because • more unscrupulous employer with sweated labour wm enabled to place cheaper goods on the market. Mr Coleman in reply said it was the duty of the capitalist to find other markets. He thought Teri if Reform would be able to cure it. (Laughter.) Another question was : If syndicalism meant s universal strike how could capital go abroad ? Mr. Coleman : Capital had been sent to South America to • large extant, and there was not much syndicalism there. Revolutionary governments' did not protect property. Syndicalism to be effective would have to go to all parts of the civilised world, and that in his opinion would be impossible, having regard to human nature. __</p>
        <p>Questioner; How do you recencile two of yoar statements - By practising thrift every worker might become a capitalist; (2) the men who practised thrift and abstinence were the men wha were going abroad.</p>
        <p>Mr. COLEM AN : I see nothing wrong with the state- ment. Tim capitalists who were going abroad were doing so in order to make a little more capital. The best labour always followed-capital. What is the difference between Nationalisation and Social on ? Mr. CoLF.M AN : The definition of the nal ionalisatinn of land meant that the nation or state or Government would be the landlord. Being tenants they would pay their rents to the State and be able to become capitalists. Under Socialism they would socialise the land and make it belong to everyone. Everything they produced would go into the coffers of the State. They could not become capitalists. By Mr. Neel e WILLIsms : Bearing in mind that the productivity of labour had increased from one to a thousand was there no suspicion in the mind of the lecturer that • surplus was going to some one other than the producer ? Mr. COLEMAN : When capital wm invested the Investor took the risks, awl therefore be was justly entitled to the surplus. He failed to see where there was any robbery of the worker. Is It not a fact capital will go where it can get the best return ? Of coarse it does.</p>
        <p>Judging from the remarks of the lecturer in regard to mttoicipal enterprise does be contend that all municipal enterprise should he judged only from a commercial standpoint Yes. A Tote of thaks t 3 the lecturer and the chairman brought the meeting to • clots.</p>
        <p>Mr. Richard Kerr, F.R.A.S., on Toes by, visited Dartmoor Prison, and delivered a lecture on " The microscopic wonders in nature" to 1,100 convicts. The lecturer was listened to throughout with the utmost attention.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>EGYPT AND THE HOLY LAND.</head>
        <p>EGYPT AND THE HOLY LAND.</p>
        <p>Locture by Rev. W. Bracecamy.</p>
        <p>The Vicar of St. Paul's (the Rev. W. Brag...camp commenced a aeries of lectures in the Girl'. School,) Rolleston-street, on Monday evening last.. It will be remembered that at the beginning of the year 63 visited wall members of his family Egypt and the Holy Land, and vim fortunats enough tu secure some beautiful slides, some 400 in all, lie took his audience with him on Monday evening in imagination as far as Cairo, and gave some interesting reminiscences of his journey. 'lle elides were very beautiful ; among those of special interest we may mention the Mosque of Sultan Humn built of stone taken from the pyramids of Gizeh. It is close to the Citadel, and is generally considered to be the grandest in Cairo. It was built by Masan, one of the younger sons of Sultan Nasir, and its construction occupied three years, A.D., 1350.1358. It is said when the building was finished the architect's hands were cut off to prevent his executing a similar work. Hasan's tomb is situated on the east side of the building. The slides of the Pyramids and the Sphinx were greatly admired. It WAS pointed out that old as Old Cairo is, she is but a child of to-day oomparel with the age of the l'yramids. In these stupendous structures we have the oldest, the highest, and most enduring monuments ever raised by the hand of man. For centuries, nry for thousands of years, their object was sbroude I in mystery. They are known to be tombs, tombs of Pharaonic kingsillwho lived and ruled in Memphis in the days of Abraham and Lot. They owe their origin to the ancient Egyptian doctrine of the immortality of the soul and beiief in a future resurrection. In accord- ance with thin doctrine it was considered necessary that the earthly tabernacle of the soul should he preserved. In order to remove the bodies of the dead from the influence of the Nile, they were buried in the dry rocky soil of the desert. Wealthy persons caused tomb chambers to be excavated for themselves, while the kings who wished to maintain their royal pre-eminence even in death, were especially anxious to ensure the durability and permanence of their tombs. Accordingly U soon as the king ascended the throne he began to build his tomb, sod Ibis work was contemporary with his life, the longer he lived the larger was his pyramid. It has often been remarked that no unfinished pyramids have ever been found. And the reason is that the building was commenced from the centre and not from the outside. A chamber sufficiently large to hold the sarcophagus which should contain the body was first built and layers of atones pyramidal in form were fitted round it. If eking died after a short reign his pyramid was consequently a small one. If he reigned like Cheops or Chephren for 50 years or more his tomb which bad been receiving its layers of atone the whole of that time, assumed gigantic proportions. The pyramid of Cheops is no vast that it covers 13,!, acres of land. Napoleon, when a prisoner at St. Helena, amused himself by calculating cubic con- tents, which he estimated at surly 7 million tons of stone, sufficient to build a wall 24 feet high and 1,000 miles long. On this particular pyramid 103,000 mu were employed for three months every year, and 10 years were consumed in making the road alone for the transportation of the stone. The step up the pyramid are in places 3 feet high, and though 283 in number, with practice it is said a pereon can emend in five minutes. Several slides of the Sphinx were shown on the screen and it was pointed out that the age of this monster is unknown. Some years ago it was generally believed to have been made daring the rule of the kings of the Middle Empire over Egypt, but when the stele which recorded the repairs made in the temple of the Sphinx by Thothmes IV. n c. 1406 came to light it became certain that this wonderful monu- ment was in existence under the ancient Empire. The height from the crown of the head to the floor between the paws is 70 feet, the body is 140 feet long and the paws protrude 50 feet more. It is named in the hiero- glyphics Horus in the horizon," that is to any the sun god, the type of all the kings, and between the paws was the altar where stcrifices were offered to this deity.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>The re-appearance of Florrie Forde, described as the greatest of all chorus singers, this week, ensures the success of the proAramtne. At the initial perform- ance she was accorded a hearty welcome which has been upheld tight through the week and the fact that she has established herself a warm favourite cannot Ibe doubted. Her songs, intermingled with witticisms, could be warranted to cure all sufferers from the "dumps" and other like afflictions. Among her latest successes may be mentioned" Meet me in Kelly land" and 'Has anyone seen my Soldier Boy," while she readily complies with the requests for the rendering of several of her original songs, the choruses of which are taken up with gusto by the audience. Otto and Olga present their comedy musical act which produces an abundance of mirth, while their rendering of the " Bohemian " on the conN3rtina earns warm approval. Les Fansettes in the two szenas " The I. 3M in Coos" and " The Palace of Terpsichore " are responsible for a high class display of step-dancing. The Ronr,ea are a smart pair of jugglers and equilibrists, the male artiste especially di,tinguishing himsalf ty a back- ward spring to the floor whilst sitting on a chair in mid-air. Fred Ward is a comedian who, if encored a dozen times would still be able to produce genuine hilarity. Some quaint humour it forthcoming from songs and patter of Tom Jones who is well described as a whimsical Welshm an. Bert Castle sings a couple of catchy songs The pictures call for special mention and the orchestra contribute a number of pleasing selections.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>CROSS-COUNTRY RUNNING.</head>
        <p>CROSS-COUNTRY RUNNING.</p>
        <p>Warrington A.C. visited West Cheshire Harriers on Saturday last. The racing divirion comprising six •isitors and 17 of Want Cheshire followed so eight mile trail over some good country. A count of five aside was agreed on and after about 50 minutes running C Voss the Northtrn Clumpion, came home about 60 yards in front of „I Parsonage who finished a far yards in front of A S.ddall. S.llO distanoe awry P Shadarel I ens struggling against the strength of the homs team and that he overhauled all except two was a very useful performance. T W.lson and 11 Rowlands, two novices completed the visitors quota by filling the 13th and 16th positions, the former displaying a grit which was very creditable and an almost certain forerunner of improved form. Result</p>
        <p>W ARRINGTON A.C. 'VEST CHESHIRE H. C Vose 1 A Hancock J Parsonage 2 T M Feral,. 5 A Siddall 3 A McMaster 7 F Shadwell 6 F Sea!' __ __ _ 8</p>
        <p>T Wilson ..</p>
        <p>_ . Under the circumstances an 8 point win for War- rington was rather a disappointment for West Cheshire who although rninus some of their beat mane— had hoped to do better over their own course. Rylands W.R.C. Harriers had a steady run and reserved their energies for this afternoon's conflict with the junior section of the premier club. Runcorn Tally Ho are moving in good style and rather surprised the St. Helens C.C.A. clubs inc com- bined run from the headquarters of St. Helens Parish Church. Dation beat all except C Holland, the Sacred Heart stalwart, with whom a splendid finish mu fought out. E Barnes (Sacred Heart), was followed by H Wilcox (Ravenhead). Result of team race :-1, Runcorn Tally Ho 51 points; 2, Ravenbead 70; 9, Sacred Heart. 73 St Helens Parish Church 153 ; Si. Helens Rees 180: Paisley Cross C.Y.M.S. 919. Runcorn visit Warrington inc few weeks time end if the local juniors intend to keep the honours at home they will have to mos.) a bit To-day's local fixture, Warrington A.C. (junior division) • Rylands, is from the new headquarters of the former, and ell intending participants ale requested to tarn up in time to start at 3 p.m. Probable competitors for the West Lancashire Novice , Team Race, a fortnight. hence, should note Slant two qualifying runs with the club they intend to represent are essential, therefore to-day and next Saturday Ire the only opportunities for those who bare confined their training to evening runs.</p>
        <p>Colos of Clerks. The monthly meeting of the Warrinom Branch of the National Union of Clerks was held at the Labt or Exchange on Tuesday. The annual balance .heet of the Cnion was read and showed a good increase and also an increase of over .4100 to the Management Fund doting the past 12 months. The report showed a net increase of membership of 1,21^ and also 25 new branches having been formed daring the past 12 months. The pint 12 months have shown marked improvement in securing recognition of the demands for a living wage for all adult clerks. The most notable victory was in conm.ction with the West Ham Tona Council. 1/wing I to the agitation, 23 per cent of thy staff received increases in salary ranging from 2s. to ISa. per wok. The principle of equal payment for equal work Las been carried cut, with the result that female clerks are receiving the same treatment as male clerks. The Secretary reported that SCCOT, ling to the November 'Clerk " the N. 1.7 C. enrolled 4'il new members for September and have formed four new branches, Brockley, Dublin, Manchester Ship Canal and Northampton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>RIGHT OF ENTRY IN COUNCIL SCHOOLS.</head>
        <p>RIGHT OF ENTRY IN COUNCIL SCHOOLS.</p>
        <p>The Rectors Hope.</p>
        <p>In celebration of the centenary of the National Society a special meeting in support of its objects was held on Thursday night in the Church House, Bridge. street, the principal speaker being Mr. W. H. Long, one of the society's representatives. The chair was occupied by the Sector (Canon Willis.) The Cu A I( MA N alluding to the great work accom- plished by the society said in their own Winwick Deanery during_ the last fifteen years the sum of £3,600 had been given to them by the National Society. Out of that sum £1,500 had been given to the • arious perishes and one half which bad been given to the Church school. lie felt they owed a deep debt of gratitude for such help. In the future it might be that new Church schools scold have to be built and others would have to be amintained and enlarged, Ile trusted they would gain the right of entry into the Council school,. No religious instruction of a denominational character was given in those elementary schools, but, the fact that undenominationel instruction was given showed that the principle of religious instruction was admitted. He did not see in the least why it should not be extended to the Secondary schools He earnestly hoped they would go farther than that and have not only in the Council Secondary schools, butts the Council Elementary schools, such religious instruction as the parents believed in and required for the children. Cdlloll MOR I.E V STEVENSON moved a resolution expressing thankfulness for the services the society had rendered to the cause of definite religious instruction, and recommending the urgent need for larger acces- si on to the fund, in order to secure the preservation of Church schools Ile said those who looked back upon the history of the Caurch education could only hays a feeling of deepest grati- tude for the unflagging and valuable help the a misty had rendered to the cause of Church elucni inn. Of all the valuable societies the Church possessed he did not know one which had done greater and better work for the cause and spread of true religion, and for the education of the children of our land in the principles which they as Church people held very dear, than the National Society. The Church of England training colleges were of the utmost importance at the present moment hemline they bad numbers of training colleges opened, which were not in connection with any religious body and in which to a large extent no religious instruction of any kind was given. That was a serious thing for the future. They were raising up teachers who were going forth unequipped to teach the truths of religion, without any help being given to them in the training colleges. In a few years time they would find a large number of teachers in their schools incompetent to give instruction in religion. Colleges in connection with religious bodies were at the present moment doing a work which was invaluable in the Moe of religious education. Such a soc;etv had the greatest possible demand upon their sympathy. Mr. B. S. TOWNROE seconded and said that during the past four years they had seen the Church attacked in many directions. They SIM forshadowed in the future, legislation which would make further attack' upon the Church.</p>
        <p>The resolution wee carried. A resolution calling for the settlement of the education question on the basis of the right of the parent to determine the religions character of the teaching to be given in the schools, was cnrried on the proposition of the Rev. E. C. E. CanttroN,seconded by the Rev. 11. PIGIC IVA L. My. LONG, in the COMO of Lis address, gave an interesting review of the history and work of the Soc:ety.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>PLAGUED WITH ITCHING PILES. Workmar.'s 12 Years Agony Ended by Zam-Buk.</head>
        <p>PLAGUED WITH ITCHING PILES. Workmar.'s 12 Years Agony Ended by Zam-Buk.</p>
        <p>Mr. William White is a happier man to-day than he has been for twelve long years, for Zam-Buk has completely rid him of itching p.iles that made his life miserable. Mr. White, who is a gas-meter tester, lives at 20, Derby-erect, Lower Broughton,</p>
        <p>Manchester, where he told his'retnarkable experiences to nleporter:</p>
        <p>" Its-must be twelve years &amp;nee the piles began to trouble me," said Mr. White. "At firat I didn't know what was wrong, but I was so plagued with the itching that I quickly had medical advice. The doctor's ointment., however, had no good effect at all; instead, I found the piles steadily grAting bigger and more painful, and often I could only walk with great difficulty. When I went to bed or got extra warm the piles itched worse than ever, and I made myself quite sore through rubbing and scratching them. At times I was S 3 bad that I couldn't go ti my work. For years I was never free for more than a few days from the torturing itch. "Just before last Christmas I had an extra severe attack of the piles which kept me in bed lying on my stomach. One pile was a tremendous size and bout me terribly. The doctor Viet" told me that nothing could cure me, and that I must let the complaint take its oon course. But I was in such torture that I felt I must try something. So, after reading how %am- Buk had cured another workman who had suffered for many a weaty year, I sent for a box of the balm. Never shall I forget the relief Zam-Buk brought me ! I had lealised nothing like it slime the piles started troubling me. Zam-Buk soothed and softened the growths and enabled me to get about in comfort. By lin-severing with this rich balm I gradually got rid of the torturing complaint—the big pile dying away in an astonishing manner. When at last I found myself free from pain and discomfort, I felt really delighted, but waited to see whether the complaint returned. But I hive never hail any further trcable, and I am convinced that Zarn-Bilk has coinp'efely cored me. "All my workmates know how I suffered, and they also know what a wonderful cure Zam-Buk has worked in my obstinate ease." Soother dressing can be so effective or so pleasant to use as Zam-Buk. The rich herbal ingredients of cm-Buk are unaffected by weather or age, and always show the same high degree of soothing and healing efficiency.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>DR CLIFFORD GRATEFUL</head>
        <p>DR CLIFFORD GRATEFUL</p>
        <p>For Promised Relief From Evils of Education Act of 1902.</p>
        <p>Tim Rev. Dr. Clifford, in a letter to the Press, says the greatest satisfaction will be given to the pirsi•e resisters of the country by the declaration of the Prime Minister In regard to the removal of their grievances during the life of the present Parliament. .• I have been In the North and Midlands within the last few days," he says, "and seen the impatience of Liberals and Radicals at the prolonged continuance of the evils of the Education Act of 1902. They have worked hard for the Parliament Bill In expectation that it would clear the road for the thorough realisation of sound and jiiit Liberal ideas in national education, and they have chafed note little at what appeared to be the bewildering silence of the Government on educe- , tion in the sketches they have supplied of the policy of the future.</p>
        <p>" The leader of the 11,1,bour pout!, Mr. lisfussy 11,facD,usald, has secured us this relief, sad we are grateful to him sad to the Premier."</p>
      </div>
