      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>UNIONIST PARTY.</head>
        <p>UNIONIST PARTY.</p>
        <p>Great surprise was caused by ti•e announcement that Mr. Balfour had resigned his leadership. For nearly twenty years he has been a dominant and a directing force on his side of the House, and his resignation is deplored by both parties. Mr Bonar Law, M. P. for Bootle, has been in the House only eleven years. He is a man of many qualities, an agile and resourceful debater, and it is expected that under his leadership a new fighting spirit will be infused into the Opposition.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>EARLESTOWN RIOTS.</head>
        <p>THE EARLESTOWN RIOTS.</p>
        <p>Smart Sentences on the Accused.</p>
        <p>At the Liverpool Moises on Saturday, Mr. Justice Avery delivered judgment in the case of Peter Al'Nlino, Richard Appleton, Frederick Merritt, and David ifulme, who on Friday evening were found guilty of rioting at Earleetown on August 19th. His Lordship, addressing the prisoners, said they might have been indicted on the evidence for an effrnce even more serious—that of doing things with intent to endanger the safety of persons travelling on the railway, and on the well-knoen principle thst people were taken to intend the natural consequences of their not, prisoners certainly would have been liable on the evidence to have been convicted of that offence for which the statute provided the puniehment of penal eervitude for life. In deciding what eentence to pass he wan naturally anxious to give full effect to the good characters which prisoners bad borne, but he stunt also bear in mind that the chief object of punishment was to deter others from offending in a like manner. He was satisfied that the attack on the signal-box was organised, and notwithstanding the advice which Mime was alleged to have given to the pickets, he could not help thinking that Hulme most have knoen in what way it was organised. The case of Halms was more serious than that of the other prisoners, and he must go to prison for twelve months. M'Alinn and Merritt seem sentenced to nine months' imprisonment, sod Appleton to eight months. PUBLIC INDIGNATION AT TIIE SENTENCES. At a meeting in Earlestown Town Hall on Monday evening, indignation was expreceed at the alleged severity of the sentences. Several hundred people attended. Speeches were made protesting against the decision of Lord Justice Avory, and it was stated that a tcwn'a protest meeting waa to be held within the next fortnight. Councillor Davies moved a reaolution expressing sympathy a ith the imprisoned men, and declaring that the sentence was altogether out of proportion to the offences, even if they were proved. Mr. Dixon (a member of the A.S.R.S.) said they were going to carry Holmes case to the Court of Appeal. It was decided unanimously to form a public sub- scription for the families of the men, and also to petition the Home Secretary against the sentencea passed on them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>WATCH YOUR CHILD'S CHEST.</head>
        <p>WATCH YOUR CHILD'S CHEST.</p>
        <p>Peps Banish Colds and Keep the Lungs</p>
        <p>Healthy.</p>
        <p>If mothers would only take care of their children's lungs and chests by treating in a sensible manner the coughs and colds to which children are so liable, there would be infinitely less suffering from lung 1 trouble in later life. Instead of coddling the youngsters , up with unnecessary wisps and clothing, and (toeing them with COMM] cough mixtures, cheap lozenges and concoctions loaded with opium, laudanum, sud other harmful drugs, mothers should keep a supply of the breatheable Peps talAts handy, and night and morning du, ing the damp and chilly weather give the little ones a tablet each to sock. In the case of infants and tiny tote, half a tablet even, crushed into a powder and placed on the tongue will prove very benefici,ll. - -</p>
        <p>The Peps treatment is not only unique, but the tablets are so entirely free from dangerous drugs that they can be given to the youngest and most delicate child with absolute safety. When Peps dissolve in the mouth the medicinal esserces stored up in each tablet are released in the form of fumes which the ' little ones breathe direct into the lunge, soothing and strengthening the delicate tissues in tho throat and chest, cleating the breathing tubes of all phlegm, and destroying the germs t f chest disease. Mixtures swallowed into Vie stomach cannot reach the longs, and that is where the seat of chest trouble is in both old and young. Peps will help and enccurage the children to breathe deeply by filling the lungs with pure and comforting pine-forest air. Give Peps to thechildten freely, and they still b 3 kept free from caighs, colds, sore throat whooping cough, croup, wheeziness, and bronchitis.. The slightest sign cf a cough or hoarseness in your child is a signal that Peps are needed.</p>
        <p>Dr. William Ilestma Hamer was on Timmlay appointed Medical Officer of Healtb, sod also School Medical Officer for the County of London at the commencing salary of £1,250,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>, II'ON CHAPTERS. CAPTAIN JOHN CLARE.</head>
        <p>, II'ON CHAPTERS. CAPTAIN JOHN CLARE.</p>
        <p>A PICTURESQUE WARRINGTONIAN.</p>
        <p>Fifty years ago the " Guardian " appeared with black bordered columns and announced in an eloquent eulogy that the town had lost its most picturesque personality. It was I perhaps no exaggeration on the part of the scribe to suggest that many eyes would be dimmed with tears as they read the news of the death of Captain John Clare, for in no small degree be enjoyed the affection and a Imiration—certainly the admiration—of his fellow-townsmen.</p>
        <p>His claims upon the attention of his town were many. The last survivor of the gallant band of volunteers who arose in 1798 to preserve Warrington from invasion, he lived through sixty three more years to tell of th 3 great deliverance. By fervent speeches he kept aglow the flame of loyalty to King and Country, and the "glorious constitution" was never omitted from the peroration. The clergy dinner would have been a blank without him and his annual speech thereat was reported and eagerly read. If a public function had been allowed to pass without a speech from the Captain the public would have laboured under a sense of very proper grievance and irreparable loss, for he and he alone could take them back to the great days wl en hel and his father shouldered the musket and struck fear to the heart of the Bonaparte.</p>
        <p>But his crowning service to the State was rendered at election times. From the hust- ings platform his massive figure and stentorian voice awed the crowd as he seconded the Tory candidate, and poured scorn like a very vitriol upon whiggish Con- stitution smashers.</p>
        <p>Warrington has had but one Captain John Clare and it is no wonder she wept at the parting. .._ . . .. .</p>
        <p>. Someday we may have a biography with a reprint of the speeches, and then we shall know the full awfulness of a sense of Empire and the gravity of becoming its chief defender.</p>
        <p>... That our readers may know the Captain in all his seriousness it may be a profitable thing to set forth a few facts and quotations, beginning with the opening passage of the obituary notice of 50 years ago.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>EULOGY.</head>
        <p>A EULOGY.</p>
        <p>"No more shall that fine old English gentleman with his ready nod, his hearty handshake and his bluff—how are you?— be seen driving through the streets of Warrington. No more shall the company at a clergy dinner, a mayor's banquet, or an election meeting, be roused to roars of laughter by his original wit, his apt allusions and his John Bull indomitable spirit. "The last of the Warrington Volunteers of 1798 is dead; and the Queen has lost a subject with as loyal a heart as ever beat; his widow as noble a partner as ever fell to the lot of woman; Warrington one of her worthiest sons, and the poor, the prime minister of charity in this distriq. "Mr. Clare had attained the age of 82 'by the blessing of God, and the not burning the candle at both ends,' as he used to phrase it. He was the oldest public man amongst us, being five years the senior of the venerable Mr. Blackburne of Hale who was his captain in 1803."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>JOHN CLARE AND THE FAMOUS BLUEBACKS</head>
        <p>JOHN CLARE AND THE FAMOUS BLUEBACKS</p>
        <p>It was in the memorable year 1798 that John Clare in company with his father came under the public gaze. He joined at that date the Warrington Loyal Volunteers xhich then numbered 190 men, and with the pardon- able pride of an old soldier often boasted, that father and son came into Warrington three days a week with their muskets on their shoulders, to take three hours drill time on the ground where Cockhedge factory I now stands. He used to tell also that Gilbert Greenall whose son afterwards became the member for the Borough and two clergymen carried the musket alongside of him and rejoiced equally in the title that followed them everywhere "Hark, boys, steady, and keep your powder dry." In the Volunteers he ever took a peculiar and pardonable pride. He rose step by step to the rank of Sergeant, and in the year 1803 when the men numbered 500 he attained the rank of Captain. WHEN ENGLAND LIVED IN DREAD OF</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>INVASION.</head>
        <p>INVASION.</p>
        <p>Right seriously-le-i-oa-the situation. It was felt that Napoleon, who was gripping every European capital must surely have Warrington in his eye. And there were many things less likely than that John Clare would have to settle matters</p>
        <p>with .him, and possibly do it alone.</p>
        <p>The situation was -carefully weighed, and the Captain seems to have decided that, with the Sankey Canal coming to her very door, Warrington would be most easily attacked by water. It would have gone badly for Napoleon had he made the attempt, for Captain Clare owned numerous barges on the Canal. Years afterwardsiihe declared in cryptic language that he would have packed " Bony " in a flat-bottomed canal boat "and sent him to where the moles see."</p>
        <p>The Captain never divulged the details of his scheme of defence and the place of ' Napoleon's banishment is given in words sufficiently vague. Yet everybody now agrees that Napoleon was supremely wise in not risking a battle on the Sankey Canal. But the whirligig of time brings surprises, and the _old captain at the close of _ the</p>
        <p>Crimean War found himself with the duty of proposing the health of the Emperor of France—a real Bonaparte. He frankly con- fessed that he had spent his days in cursing the name to extinction and never expected to live to drink health to it. But he did it— with a gulp.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>CAPTAIN IN POLITICS.</head>
        <p>THE CAPTAIN IN POLITICS.</p>
        <p>In religion ancl.politics.Captain_ Clare was</p>
        <p>a churchman to the backbone and a Tory of the oldest school. Says a local writer; "His • voice pronouncing 'Amen ' at the clergy dinner, or hear, hearing' a speaker at an election meeting was worth a score of other men's voices. It was like the sound of 1 many waters." He took his politics as he took everything else—very seriously. It was part of his religion, and he was a politician because he believed he was a christian. His last public act was to record his vote, though he had to be almost carried to the polling booth, and his end was hastened by an accident which occurred at the public nomination—in those days a noisy and turbulent public occasion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>ON THE PLATFORM.</head>
        <p>ON THE PLATFORM.</p>
        <p>Old Warringtomans might have welcomed elections, if only from the fact that it brought the Captain to the platform. In his eightieth year he was as zealous for his Queen and country as when taking up the rifle sixty years before, but the wrath that was then poured upon the name of Napoleon now flowed like molten lava upon the modern</p>
        <p>enemies of the Constitution, the Whigs. This is a mild extract from a speech delivered in April, 1859: "I have something to say to my opponents when I get them on the hustings. One of them alluded to me lately—l am not afraid of him, if I had him in a saw pit—one hand does there. (Laughter). I am charged—l know it means me—with using the cry "The Church, and King, and down with the Rump." Now that is a very good cry. I hope it will be put on my grrvestone. It was a cry before the speaker I allude to was born.—when I carried a musket sixty years ago. "Down with the Rump" means "Down with the enemies of old England." (Loud cheers).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>WOE TO INTERRUPTERS.</head>
        <p>WOE TO INTERRUPTERS.</p>
        <p>Only the very boldest of political inter- rupters dare interject anything when the Captain was on his feet. At the same election a well-known confectioner—Mr. Wright—interrupted'and this was the result. "Have you anything to say sir? (accom- panying the interrogation with threatening gesticulations which elicited roars of laughter). I am an old soldier, and won't be intimidated by any man under heaven. (Loud cheers and uproar). I understand that you are a toffy. merchant. (Roars of laughter and expectation.) With all deference I believe I am addressing the toffy merchant for the first time. Now I'll tell you a bit about toffy making. I hope yo</p>
        <p>CAPTAIN JOHN CLARE From an Old Print in the Reference Library.</p>
        <p>put plenty of treacle in it my boy. (Immense laughter). In my neighbourhood a youngster once tumbled out of an apple tree, and the folks thought he had knocked his neck out. But after a while his mother, whimpering and crying, said to him "Oh Tommy, do speak one sweet word to me," and the boy said "Treacle, mother." (Roars of laughter). Now sir if you make your toffy with plenty of treacle in it send me a pound to-morrow. It delights me to see a real toffy man."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>TIIE CAPTAIN'S NIGHT CAP.</head>
        <p>TIIE CAPTAIN'S NIGHT CAP.</p>
        <p>Though the Whigs tried very hard they never quite vanquished the gallant Captain. On one occasion when the public nomination took place he was put up as usual to second the Tory candidate. The Whigs organized a noisy interruption and the Captain tried hard to get on with his speech. But he had come prepared for emergencies, and at a moment when an ordinary man would have given in he reached down into his pocket and proceeeed to unfold a cotton night cap. "1 expected being kept a bit late" he said," and so I brought my night cap. But if it's after midnight, I'll make my speech,"</p>
        <p>There was no resisting the night cap and the speech was interrupted by nothing but laughter after that.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>VERSATILE AND VARIEGATED.</head>
        <p>VERSATILE AND VARIEGATED.</p>
        <p>In business he was in turns a cotton spinner, a glass manufacturer, coal agent, ropemaker, and a Sankey flat-owner on an extensive scale. The latter department of his many activities gave him the text for many a happy hit whilst speaking and gave a nautical flavour to his language that often mixed curiously with the language of the Loyal Volunteers.</p>
        <p>Every public event in Watrington brought a speech from Captain Clare, and that fact alone made public events very welcome. We may quote in illustration his speech at the [Opening of the Warrington Market on October 18th. 1856.</p>
        <p>Mr. Mayor and gentlemen, I do not exactly know why I am called upon to respond to the" Town and Trade of Warrington. "(the Mayor, As senior tradesman') My worthy friend Alderman Peter Rylands has told us much of tramps—all tramps begin on ten toes. (Laughter). I was born in this town two months short of 78 3 ears ago, and have seen it very low but never without loyalty. It always stood forth for our glorious con- stitution. (Cheers).</p>
        <p>, It was never second in the Kingdom for that. Look at the Irish Rebellion. We had men who stood forth—but you'll say, " Poor simpleton, he's praising himself." (Laughter.) Never mind; let those who can, speak against him. (Cheers.) In this town of Warrington there were more loyal soldiers for the army and sailors for the navy than in any other town—aye, any of double, the population. In the last war, or I must now call it the war before the last, 500 Volunteers were asked for and 1,300 came forward. (Loud cheers.) I tell you that, though half of you were not born then, and the man who tells you had a Captain's commission then, serv- ing his country, and he is ready to serve it again. (Loud applause.) Our worthy Member of Parliament knows that his father and my father and myself were soldiers in 1798, yet none of them had ',gone out of the country to serve except my young friend Henry Lyon. He is a chip off the old block. (Cheers.) He is a bit of an amphibean—a bit of both land and water. (Laughter.) His father and I were soldiers fifty years ago. (Cheers.) There is not much to speak of in Warrington, but I can name the Grammar School, the Blue Coat School, the Clergy Training School and many others. The town of Warrington is, I trust under Heaven, without the blessing of which nothing can prosper, beginning a new era. (Hear, hear.) It has often pained my heart to see people drenched to the skin, and taking fevers for want of a covered market. I have often wished to see a covered market, but I am not one of the Corporation. (Laughter). You would not have such a nondescript as I am. (Laughter). By the facilities now given by railways and commerce some of my neighbours now go to St. Helens market, although within a hop, skip and jump of Warrington. But they need not do so now, for we can have our eggs new laid and our butter fresh. (Laughter). I thank the Corporation for the way they have finished their labours, you must excuse me for spinning such a long yarn. God bless you all. (Loud cheers long continued which seemed to sensibly affect the brave old captain, who, straight as a ramrod, at 78</p>
        <p>delivered his remarks with a juvenility and spirit which will long be remembered).</p>
        <p>AN EARLY INCIDENT RECALLED.</p>
        <p>The Captain always caught the public imagination. When a young man he paid a visit to London and went to see Madame Saqui, the female Blondin of the day. I.tri this evening the performance was delajed owing to the discovery that the rope upon which she was to balance was defected and needed splicing. The attendants struggled with the task and the audience waited in patience. Not so John Clare. He was made of another kind of stuff and had not serled on the Sankey Canal for nothing. With the cry "Clear away you land lubbers and let me show you how to splice a rope," he leaped into the arena, swung them aside and quickly roads the splicing to the accompaniment of roars of applause from the crowded house. Such was Captain Clare. There was swagger in every movement, but it was unconscious, and the humour was unconscious too.</p>
        <p>Oocasionally he flashed with conscious fun, but not often. We quote one instance.</p>
        <p>SKEWERED.'</p>
        <p>In the year 1804 when on duty with the volunt4e s at C esters a young inn Paul Greenwood, of the company, came to the Captain breathless. " Captain " he said "I beard a man cursing the King. Ought I to have run him thro "No, air" said Captain Clare "You should only have s?cured (skewered) him."</p>
        <p>Not that the gallant Captain would have cared a feather if the traitor had perished for his treason, for he used to boast that if a single corner of his own heart proved disloyal to his Sovereign he would himself take a knife and slice that corner off. _ _</p>
        <p>The Empire has had to manage for fifty long years without him, and until lately the "glorious constitution held together. It would be unkind to wish him back in this realm of strife; but what wouldn't it be worth to hear him call down plagues upon that fellow Asquith! And what an asset to his party at the two last General Elections! A. M.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>FROM ' LABOUR'S STANDPOINT.</head>
        <p>FROM ' LABOUR'S STANDPOINT.</p>
        <p>By Philip Snowden, M P. WHAT A DEMOCRATIC REFORM BILL SHOULD DO. The isnnouncement made laat week by the Prime Minister that it is the intention of the Government to bring forward an electoral reform Bill next year has excited great interest. It is not proposed in this article to discuss the promise from the point of view of its effect on the woman suffrage agitation, which is perhaps the most important and interesting of the consequences which will follow from the intention to extend and , simplify the male franchise. That matter is so complicated that it could only be discussed as a separate issue raised by the Prime Minister's announcement. All that need be said here just now is that it will be the most shameful betrayal ever perpetrated by the British Parliament if it should pass a Bill for the extension of the male franchise without dealing at the same time with the claims of the women for the Parliamentary vote. In what follows it is assumed that women must be treated as citizens, and must have all the political rights which men enjoy. NO STRONG DEMAND. Not in this generation has there been any agitation for the extension of the male franchise. Labour and other Congresses have passed pious resolutions about electoral reform, but there has been neither soul nor spirit in the demand. fhe explanation of the absence of any strongly expressed desire by men for an extension of the franchise is that there is no sense of injustice felt by the minority of adult men who may be for the moment without votes. They may be with- out to-day, but they may have votes to-morrow. But in spite of that it is rather extraordinary that the Labour Party has not made stronger demands for electoral reform than they have done, because they as a party undoubtedly suffer more than any other party from the shortcomings and peculiarities of our present franchise laws. THE PEOPLE'S CHARTER. The Labour Party has repeatedly put its ideas of what a Reform Bill should be into the form of a Resolution passed at its annual Conferences. In view of the coming Government Bill it may be interesting to set forth what the Labour Party regard as needed electoral changes. At its Confer- ence last January the Labour Party carried a resolution declaring that a Reform Bill to be satisfactory must enact (1) adult suffrage, that is, a vote for every adult man and woman on equal terms; (2) the shortest possible qualifying period for registration; (3) the payment of returning officers'expenses; (4) all elections on one day and one man one vote only ; (5) strengthening of the Corrupt Practices Act; (6) the prevention of the election of a member by a minority vote. These constitute what we may call the modern People's Charter, Unless each and all of these proposals are embodied in the promised Reform Bill it will be unsatis- factory and will leave the political machine incomplete for the proper discharge of its work, which should be to get a perfect representation of the Will of the People. ONE SIMPLE FRANCHISE. It is understood that the promised Bill will sweep away every one of the existing qualifications for the Parliamentary vote. All are to go. In future there will be no property vote, no lodger vote, no latchkey vote, no university vote, no household vote, and no plural vote. There is to be one simple franchise, namely, that every man over a certain age (twenty-five it is said), who has resided in a place for six months shall have his name placed on the voters list. That is alright as far as it goes. Instead of having some hundreds of Acts of Parliament dealing with the vote there will be one only, and instead of a score of different ways of qualify- ing there will be one way only. It is certain that there will be some restriction, as for instance if a man be a lunatic (that is a certified lunatic) or an 'lien. or if he has been convicted of some corrupt practice, he will be excluded. The heartless disqualification of paupers must surely disappear. AN AGREED REFORM. In regard to the length of time that should be fixed as the qualifying period there is a difference of opinion among election experts. Some of them argue that it should be as short as possible—say a month, while others argue that it ought to be not less than six months. It is said that if a person could qualify in a month as a voter in a particular constituency there would be wholesale transfers of votes just before a General Election in order to adjust the strength of a party. It is said that the Government intend to fix a period at six months. The payment of returning officers' expenses will no doubt be included in the promised Bill. It cannot very well be left out. All parties support this reform,</p>
        <p>and if there is going to be a great increase in the electoral roll it will make the cost of elections much greater, and therefore the injustice of making the candidates pay the official expenses would be s,ggravated.</p>
      </div>
