      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>DOUBTFUL POINT.</head>
        <p>A DOUBTFUL POINT.</p>
        <p>- -• It is hardly likely that the Bill will provide for all elections taking plase on the same day, but there will be no real reason against the change when plural voting has been abolished. The resolution of the Labour Conference says nothing about closing public houses on election days, but when that question was last before the House of Commons it received a measure of Unionist support. The case for a stricter Corrupt Practices Act has been strengthened by the adoption of Payment of Members. It was bad enough when M.P's. used their own money to gain favour with the constituencies, but it becomes intolerable when they devote the money they have received from the public funds to such a purpoEs. It ought to be made an offence under the Corrupt Practices Act for any person in a member's constituency to ask for a subscrip- tion or donation; because it is difficult for the member to resist and such subscriptions are really an indirect form of political corruption.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>AN IMPORTANT MATTER.</head>
        <p>AN IMPORTANT MATTER.</p>
        <p>- - ---• The last reform mentioned in the Labour Party Resolution is one of great importsnce, and until something is done in the way indicated, the Labour Party, or any third party, will always be at an electoral disad- vantage. The matter is the adoption of some plan by which a candidate cannot be elected unless he polls a clear majority of the votes or is the choice of a majority of the voters. In three by-elections during the past few weeks candidates have been sent to Parlia- ment who do not represent the majority; in one case the successful candidate received very little more than one-third of the votes cast. So long as the Liberals can manage to win in three-cornered fights we are not likely to hear much from them about the injustice of minority representation, but if the inter- vention of a Labour candidate invariably resulted in the return of the Unionist candi- date we should soon have something done to prevent that.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>AN ALTERNATIVE VOTE.</head>
        <p>AN ALTERNATIVE VOTE.</p>
        <p>- - • --- There has been a considerable gain of support in recent years for the practice of the alternative vote, which obviates some of the disadvantages of the Second Ballot. The Alternative Vote is a very complicated thing to explain, but not difficult to practice when it is understood. Its adoption in this country has been strongly recommended by a Royal Commission which sat a year or two ago, and the system is in operation in some of the Australian States. By this system it is impossible for any candidate to be returned if there is a majority of voters opposed to him. A REDISTRIBUTION.</p>
        <p>There are a few other reforms which ought to be embodied in the new Reform Act. It goes without saying that such a measure as the Prime Minister has foreshadowed will necessitate a Redistribution of Seats. There have been great changes in the location of the population in the last twenty-five years, and the present inequalities in regard to the number of electors are absurd and unjust. We have some constituencies with from thirty to fifty thousand electors, and others with about a couple of thousand. The Unionists are quite right in insisting, as they have done, that one man one vote must be accom- panied by a reform which will, as far as possible, give each vote the same voting value.</p>
        <p>It is a very extraordinary thing that the country has tolerated our present complex, unfair, and costly electoral system for so long. Nothing better illustrates the con- servatism and patience of the British people. A people who have quietly submitted to such a franchise system, for a quarter of a century without any attempt to reform it are not made of the stuff that need keep timid people awake at nights hearkening for the booming of revolutionary guns,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>AFFLICTED CHILDREN</head>
        <p>AFFLICTED CHILDREN</p>
        <p>4,500 Foiled Yearly in Cheshire Schools. At the Cheshire Education Committee, at Crewe, on Monday, Profesmr Dormer brought forward a report by the County Medical Officer (Dr. Young), on the Children's Csre Committee. Dr. Young said that soul° 21,000 children were medically examined in Cheshire schools every year, and of that number some 4,500 were : round to need medical or surgical treatment, or I hygienic advice or cupervision. I Many of these cases re-eived adequate treatment immediately the necessity for it was pointed out, hot its considerable number of casee—opproximately 000 to 2,000 a year—it was found that for one moon or another—poverty, neglect, indifference, ignorance, and so forth—the children did not receive proper treatment, or the treatment, if applied, was not maintained for such time or in such manner an was necessary to restore the afflicted child to the minim of the physically Or, The Medical Officer showed how Care Committees could co-operate with school medical officers, the tend:Ara, and nurse.. He was empowered to take tbe necessary steps to carry out the Children's Act when- ever occasion might arise.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>LIVERPOOL FIRE.</head>
        <p>A LIVERPOOL FIRE.</p>
        <p>The corn mills of Mersrs. Bernie a-n-d. Co., Liverpool were on Tuesday destroled by • Ire which raged for five boors. The street is narrow, and the building of *seen stories N bounded by large oil works mod paint works, while close by Is congested house property. The fire w•• fortunately confined to Messrs. Bemin's mills.</p>
        <p>[ALL RIGHT 11119ZRVID.] (Copyrighted in the United Stoles of Anterie by Arthur W. M(frehmont.)</p>
      </div>
