      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>SIR GREGORY'S SILENCE</head>
        <p>SIR GREGORY'S SILENCE</p>
        <p>ET ARTHUR W. MARCHMONT, Author of The Man Who Was Dead,- 'By Right</p>
        <p>d Sword," &amp;c.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>SYNOPSIS.</head>
        <p>SYNOPSIS.</p>
        <p>IT is the corning of age of Blamer Trevellock, only son of Sir Gregory Trevellock, and the rejoicings are general A wife has already been chosen for laint—a wealthy widow Mrs. Catilllchl but when the goests have departed Soltnei Infornw his parents that he can ntarry no one save Helen Paster, a poor girl who earns her own living. This de velopment decbles Sir Gregory to delay no longer the die closing of a secret. At his death, while Bulmer suweede to the title, the elitisten pans to his eldest child, a madcap daughter of his first wife, named Lotta. His changed for tunes make no difference to Bulmere intentions toward, Helen Paxton, and the parents are petsuadeil to Invite het to Trevellock Chase. Sir Gregory forthwit h puts Bulmeee position plainly to Helen. and in the course of the Inter view she lets fall certain facts ist respect to her parentage which stagger him. Unconseiously she ;et urns to that one black page of his pastand lie in convinced that, though the girl herself knows it not, she is in reality the right ful,iwnel of Trevellock Cilllso. When she hands him papers it latch ;move this, he is face to fore with a great temptation Hole, disgrace, end beggary N% ill be Ids lot if those paper. are published to the world. Helen leaven him, and when she returns he tells her t hat he has acridentally burnt the papers. As a matter of fact he has lackol the etrength to perform that shameful deed, and has locked them in his desk.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Caulfield, thwarted In ber lore for Holm,. sets about evolving schemes to put him from Helen, and so skilfully puts the cue before Sir Gregory that he agrees As the ,future possessor. of Trevellock Chose, Lotta and her affianced husband, Gerald Grainger, make theinaelves unless hat objectionable. The latter Introduces to the boom duper Bostock, Member of the State Legislatute of Colorado," In reality a plausible acoundrel, who ha, learnt something of Sir Gregory'a pest, and does not hesi• tate to me his knowledge no a source of blackmail. Ilia first most is to obtain an invitation to atty at the</p>
        <p>Here he meets Mrs. Caulfield, who recognises in him a notorious American. whom the Denver pollee would he glad to lay hold of. She decides to employ Bostock, and. cunningly uuscrupulous, he scsin proves a useful tool. Ile first buys over to their side Mary Drescot, who share, ien's humble fiat. Meantime Buinier is offered a lucra- tive post in Canada, and oilmen he visits Ica sweetheart with the news suggests that they should be secretly mar- ried and go out together. Helen demurs, and oi hen Bulmer leaves her she mates the mistake of confiding in Mary Preseot</p>
        <p>This erstwhile companion Immediately Informs Boatoek, and, iii coinpany with Mrs. Caulfield, they decide on a new clause being added to Bill mer's foreign agreement—he must go unmarried. Meantime Bitstock is pitying 3e another card, In successfully poisoning Letts) Trevollock's mind against her fiancee, in the hope of win )) i)) g the girl (awl incidentally the estates) for himself. Pritoentiona assail Helen Penton. engagement after engagement being mysteriously cancelled, and all efforts to trace the remaid. proving unavailable. Jr. rioter not to worry Buttner she Rays nothing of this, however, and then on a day's river trip they talk for the first time of their young liver. lier real 1181. is Powell, anti her lather's elinstian name Gregory. Btalmer's father was previously Gregory Powell : Then for the first time &amp;suspicion is borne upon her, there have been two Gregory Powell's In Montano at the same time P" she asks hermit. "If</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>CHAPTER IX.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER IX.</p>
        <p>" THIRI MAT II Ali IMPLANATION."</p>
        <p>Despite her natural buoyancy of mind and high courage, Helen could not shake off the depression caused by Bulmer's reference to his father's former name, and the fact that be had been in Montana in the old days. A hundred times a day she told herself her fears must be groundless. It was impossible that a man like Sir Gregory Trevellock, high in the esteem of all who knew him, a type of an English gentleman, and regarded as the soul of honour, could be the same Gregory Powell who had married her mother only to desert her, and whose life had been an unbroken career of evil, cowardice and darkness. She recalled all the stories she had heard of her father, eager to find reasons to lull her fear; and when she pictured Sir Gregory, soave, courteous, kindly-mannered gentle- man that he was, as the doer of them, all her instincts rebelled against the possibility which so distressed her.</p>
        <p>But against that there was that strange in- terview at the Chase, when she had told him her real name. Was his explanation —a sudden heart attack—the real meaning of his collapse? She recalled that it had come just at the instant when she had placed the mar- riage certificate and her father's portrait in his hands. The plea of illness might have been false; or the illness itself might easily have been induced by the shock of the dis- covery. His agitation, confusion, and abso- lute loss of self-command were all likely enough to have been caused by some over- whelming shock. There was the destruction of the certifi- cate and the photograph, too. Had that been no more than an accident? If not, was it not just such an net as that other Gregory Powell would have committed to destroy the proofs against him? Could they have been de- stroyed in the way he had said/ She made an experiment to test this. Tak- ing a sheet of paper and a photograph, she let a lighted match fall upon them as they lay flat on the table. The result astonished her. The match flickered out slowly, burning a hole in the paper only, and consuming only that part of it where there was air beneath. To make sure, she lit a second and a third; but only with a similar result. He had deceived her, and hod burnt ths papers intentionally. Why? Was he really her father? Could he be so consummate a hypocrite under the gilded mask of his titled respectability? If so, how right had been her mother's judgment of him! How utterly base and despicable a man he was! She must find out the truth, let the cost be what it might. But she could do nothing un- til Bulmer had goi.e. And at the thought of him, her agony of mind was almost intoler- able. If this horrible thing were true, they were brother and sister, and must never meet again!</p>
        <p>'She saw her course plainly, and faced it with the courage of a martyr. Bulmer mould never know the truth about his father. She would make some excuse for not seeing him again before his departure for America; and then, she would face Sir Gregory and drag the truth from him. And if it were what she dreaded, she would go away and hide herself somewhere, and take the secret with her. _</p>
        <p>In all the days of her anguish, Mary Pes- cott endeavoured to learn the cause of her trouble; but Helen told her nothing except that her marriage with Bulmer had been postponed until after his return from America.</p>
        <p>Then a letter from Bulmer announced that the date of his leaving had been fixed, nod that he was coming up on the day before to say good-bye. The reply to the letter gave her 'both pain and infinite difficulty. Cote scioue of the barrier now between them, she could not bring herself to write on the old affectionate terms of open-hearted confi- dence. A dozen letters were written and torn up, until at last she contented herself with a bare, plain statement that she had to go away on business, and that she must bid him good-bye in that letter. Then, ill as she could now spare the money, she hurried down to Liverpool and waited there until the day when his steamer started, and watched among the little crowd of loiterers on the wharf until she saw him go on board.</p>
        <p>On her journey back to London the planned her next atep ; and began to realise the difficulties. Sir Gregory would probably deny everything; and she had no proof now, and no means of obtaining any. Moreover, ehe was hampered by the want of money; and her work had fallen off to ouch an extent that alio had fears for the future. Yet one thing was clear in her mind—she would not go to Sir Gregory aa a beggar, and would starve before she would take A penny piece fromn him.</p>
        <p>For thres days she tramped round Lrulon in search of work, only to meet with friliite everywhere. And then, all unexpectedly, &amp;sine a letter offering her an engagement as rypist. The relief brought team to her eyes, the first she had shed in all her troubles.</p>
        <p>It was from Mr. Harold Garwood, a soli- citor, who said he had heard of her through a firm he mentioned, for whom the had done work; and he wee willing to pay a salary of three pound, a week, if she could accept that.</p>
        <p>•—• She showed the letter to her friend, and</p>
        <p>\fail PeseoU opened her eyes wide In stonishment and was loud in her expressions pleasure. "I shall go off now with • ,etter heart to my engagement, Men," ahe aid. "I was getting so afraid that things could be all wrong with you." "I meant to leave London, dear, if nothing had turned up. I should have sold the fur- niture here and gone to some other town." "Where?" asked Mary, full of curiosity, as usual. . .</p>
        <p>"I don't know. Anywhere to escape the persecution which I am sure is at the bottom ,f my loss of work. And if I am hunted ont if this place, supposing I get ic, of course, I Mall go." "You'll let me know where you are. Don't let me lose sight of the orly friend I have left in the world."</p>
        <p>......_. "Of course I shall write to you; but you 'taven't given me the address of the family ;on ,re going to." "I will before I go. Be sure of that." While Helen was discusaing the prospects if her new engagement with a feeling of ;itch profound relief, Jasper Bostock wan loseted with the young solicitor, Harold ;arwood. After talking about the business shich he had put into the lawyer's hands, he aid casually: "You wrote to that girl, I .uppo '' sr" -Yea. I expect her to call to-morrow- norning. But you know I really haven't sough work to keep her busy." "Don't worry about that. Give her any- hing to do you like. Find some old deeds Ind let her make half a doses copies of them. kny old thing will do. All that my friend viehes is that she shall get the three pounds wenk through you and believe she is earn- lig it. My friend's deAre is to help her vithout her knowing she is being helped. he's been a bit out of it lately, and is too -mud to take help if offered openly. We hall stand for all you give her as salary." to she presentable; all right I mean for ,ffice purposes? And straight? Or shall 1. nave to watch her?" "I don't know anything about her char- %der. She can't do much harm here, I .uppoae, in any cane; and of course you can mty on it that if there should be any mon- 'reying around, your client will see you hrough. I suppose you keep everything of any value in your safe?" "Of course I do; but I don't mean any- hing of that sort," said airwood with a 'ight laugh. "It looks a good safe, anyway," said Bos- ock. "It the contents only matched it, our clients would take you for a rich man." "I'm rather proud of it, to tell the truth. it was my father's; and it's about the only lhing those thieves, Burman and True- weather, didn't steal, when they collared my father's practice under the pretence of allow- ing me to finish my articles with them." "I know something about safes," said Boa. :ea, getting up and going to it. He said no more than the truth; but he did not tell .he lawyer how he had acquired his know- 'edge, nor the use he had often made of it. "It s a bit old-fashioned." Mr. Garsood opened it, and while he was ipeaking Bostock looked at his watch. "Hello, it's later than I thought. See if your clerk has finished that document, will you? I must be off." Utterly unsuspicious of his visitor's motive, the young lawyer hurried into the outer Ace; and the instant his bock was turned, Bostock took a lump of wax from his pocket Ind took an impression of the safe key. He was an adept at the work, and did it both rapidly and well; and had resumed his seat before Garwood returned, saying that the document was not quite finished. "Guess your new typist will do things a bit quicker, Garwood," he said, with a lighl, easy laugh. "And by the way, there's one warning I ought to give you about her. She's a pretty girl, I'm told; but don't fall in love with her." . .</p>
        <p>"There's no fear of that, Mr. Simpson this was the name under which Bostock passed to him—"Thfi fact is I'm engaged to be married." "Well, that's safer than if you were al- ready married," replied Bostoek. "And see that she's decently treated by that clerk of yours, too. My friend would make trouble if anything went the wrong way in that respect. And get her a separate desk with some drawers and so on in it. Girls like that sort of thing; and you can put the charge in the bill of costs."</p>
        <p>"You may be sure I'll do all I can," agreed the lawyer, thinking highly of the care and thought with which all the arrangements were being made. "I shall look on her as a protegee of Madame, my new client. I'll make things comfortable for her." "And don't worry her with any questions about herself, and don't listen to her if she wants to talk. And, by the way, give her a couple of hours for her dinner instead of only one. And as for the rest, all you have to do is to keep her busy, and make her believe she is earning her money. Is that fellow through with that paper yet? If he isn't I can't wait any longer." They went through together into the outer office, and soon afterwards Bostock took his departure. "He's a nice, pleasant, innocent, honest, young fool, that," he muttered to himself as he left. "Just the sort to make a thing of the kind dead easy. Ile hasn't a peep of suspicion. That's the best of using an honest man—when you can find him. Pretty Helen will think she has fallen on velvet; and the long dinner-time will enable me to make my visits to the office without her ever seeing me. Glad I thought of that touch." He was right in his forecast of Helen's opinion of her new position. She liked her sew employer from the moment of their first meeting. Her instinct told her that he was • straight man; and he, on his side, was more than favourably impressed by her. He was young enough to be self-conscious, and stumbled a little over the task of ex- plaining how he had heard of her. But she was in no mood to be critical; and only too glad to accept the offer he made. He was nervous also when he detailed her duties, and showed her a tiny cupboard .of a room which would be furnished for her sole use.</p>
        <p>Hq was induced to take the latter step for personal reasons. She was so much better looking than he had expected that he thought it more discreet she should not be too mud] in evidence when people came to the office. There was always a lot of chaff on the sub- ject of pretty typewriters; and it was just as well, on the whole, he thought, that the girl he was going to marry should not see Helen. For a day or two, he was not quite at his ease with her and was a good deal bothered to think how to keep her employed. He adopted Bostock's hint at first, and gave her some old deeds to copy, enjoining great care in the work. And when that work was com- pleted and he had grown more accustomed to her presence, and having abundant time on his hands, he .began to dictate legal docu- ments of all sorts, drafts of conveyances, pleadings in imaginary cases, and at last articles, the matter for which he got up care- fully overnight. He was perfectly frank with her and told her he was doing it all as practice for his profession; and ileJen recognising his sin- cerity, began on her side to take an interest in the work and in his progress. Sympathy grew quickly between them, and before the week was out she thought she understood the position and that she had been engaged rather for this sort of practice than for the actual work of the office, which she soon per ceived was very scanty. When the salary dav came he put three sovereigns in an envelope addressed to her and placed it on her desk, and this little act gratified her. Before she left the office she went to his room. " I should like to ask you a question, Mr. Garwood, if you please.' "Certainly. What in it!' He was half afraid that in some way he had failed to con- ceal the curious circumstances which had brought her to the office. "I have now been with you a week and you have seen my work. Does it satisfy you com- pletely," "Absolutely. Absolutely," he assented, relieved to find he had been mistaken. "You have been of (treat assistance to me."</p>
        <p>“'I hank you,” she replied, with. a MU iP. "it means so much to me," she added, as if in explanation. Now that she was once more independent, she resolved to write to Sir Gregory. The in- terval and the week's work at the office had to some extent deadened the shock of the fear which Bulmer's words had roused; but her first impulse had hardened into a set purpose. It would be several weeks before Bulmer re- turned; and if her fears were realised she would use the time to hide herself from him. Probably she could do this while still retain- ing her position at Mr. Garwood's erns*. London was • big place in which to hide, but she must he prepared need even to leave there, and for this purpose she must save every possible shilling in order to have funds for flight. She wrote a short, very carefully worded letter to Sir Gregory, saying merely that cer- tain things had come to her knowledge which made it essential that she should see him, and that under the circumstances it would be best for him to come to London, but that if he could not she would go down to the Chase. ''lt is absolutely necessary that we meet, however, as there must be an explanation," she concluded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>CHAPTER X. TR! ROBBERY.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER X. TR! ROBBERY.</p>
        <p>The fact that Helen had written to Sir Gregory was duly reported to Bostock, and, after an interview with Mary Pescott, in which she had described to him the effects of Helen's last outing with Buliner, her obvious listress and agitation, and the curious cir- cumstance that she had hurried out of Lon- don apparently to avoid saying good-bye to him, Bostock went down to the Chase.</p>
        <p>The facts set him thinking. Up till then he had had no suspicion of the identity of Helen Paxton with Sir Gregory's child by the wife he had deserted. It did not cross his mind even then; but the facts were so strange that he could not resist the conclusion that there must be some connection between the two which it would pay him to discover. He searched his memory for the name of Paxton, hut he had never known the name of the girl Gregory Powell had married. As a matter of fact, his memory of the man was of the rguest, and he had been in- tensely surprised at the effect which his knowledge had had upon Sir Gregory. He had set it down to the latter's fear of Jake Stevens revenge; but he now recalled that his power had really come after his mention of the fact of Sir Gregory's first marriage. - . .</p>
        <p>Mrs. Caulfield had told- him also of the change in Sir Gregory after Helen's visit to the Chase, and in the light of all he had learnt since it was not a difficult inference that in some way she had some sort of hold :ivt.r him. Bostoek was thus fumbling on the verge of the truth. . _ .</p>
        <p>"—Why should the girl avoid Buttner?" was the question he kept- putting to himself. "bhe e-as going to marry him; everything had been .ill but fixed; what can have happened there- fore that day on the river to cause such a ;hange in her? They didn't quarrel: this little devil Pescott is certain about that. l'here's only one explanation, therefore. She must have found out something which frightened her. What was it? And why does she write to Sir Gregory the moment her lover's back is turned? I'd give something to know what was in the letter.''</p>
        <p>He had thus three objects in the present visit to the Chase. To report progress to Mrs. Caulfield and obtain from her the jewels of which he had before spoken; to play another card or two with Lotta in his own in- terest and against his friend Grainger; and to find out the truth about Helen's relations with Sir Gregory. _ _</p>
        <p>The first wcas an easy matter. Mrs. Caul- field gave him some jewels and asked no questions. She did not wish to know went use he was going to make of them. She was still resolved to part the lovers, if possible, hut preferred to know as little as possible of the means by which it was to be (lone. With Lotta he acted very discreetly. She (lid not rea:ly care much about Grainger, he saw ; she did not care about anyone except herself, indeed; but she was as jealous r.s a narrow-minded woman could be. He knew that Grainger's affair with the girl Lucille Field was enough in itself to embitter Lotta against him, and as he had been stupid enough to Flop in London and neglect her, it Was easy for Hemlock to make matters worse by insinuating that Lucille Field was the attraction.</p>
        <p>But he did not say one word openly against his friend. It was altogether too painful a matter for him, he declared. He was astonished and inexpressibly shocked at Grainger's conduct, and while he had every wish to do all in his power for Lotta, and had the deepest sympathy for her in her trouble, he asked her to excuse him from doing any more in the affair.</p>
        <p>In high spirits at his success with Lofts, Bostock went to Sir Gregory to achieve his third object—to discover the secret about Helen—but in this he failed completely. To keep that secret from all the world had be- come the old man's one set purpose, and as soon as Bostock referred to Helen he was on his guard. The result was that, instead of Bostock learning anything, Sir Gregory ascertained how little he knew.</p>
        <p>"My son is to marry Miss Paxton—that is why I am interested in her. Ido not approve the match for monetary reasons, and if it were in my power to prevent it I would do so. Tbst is why I am glad he has gone to America. He may tire of her, or meet some one else; but if his happiness is set on marry- ing Miss Paxton, neither his mother nor I vk ill interfere."</p>
        <p>"But there is some mystery about her," de- clared Bostock.</p>
        <p>"If so, and it Ili anything discreditable, I ought to know it in my son's interest. If you know anything, tell met" "You are only trying to put me off the scent," was the reply, somewhat angrily given. "Why should she rush away from London to avoid him?"</p>
        <p>"It you can find out anything which amounts to a reason why the marriage should not take place, I will listen to it. But it must be a solid reason."</p>
        <p>"You yourself know that there Is one," re- torted Itostock. sharply. _</p>
        <p>"If I did I should act upon it, Mr. Boo- tock."</p>
        <p>"I wish to be your friend in the matter, Sir Gregory." "That is impossible," replied Sir Gregory, with something like a shudder of repugnance. "Do you think you are well advised to hold me at arm's length in this way?" Sir Gregory paused, and pressed his hand to his head. "You greatly misunderstand the position, Mr. Bostock," he replied slowly. "There is a threat beneath those words, and I know that you believe you have me com- pletely in your power. I have given you some reason for that belief by submitting to your demands. But the hold you have over me is little more than a shadow. Be content with it, and with what it yields you. If you attempt to turn the screw too hard, it will only break in your hands. You have suc- ceeded in making me one of the most wretched men on earth. Should you render me desperate your pocket will suffer. Men like you generally understand that argument. And now leave me, if you please." Bostoek went out of the room, telling him- self that this change in Sir Gregory was die to Helen's action; but be could not fled out anything. He remained several days at the Chase, and saw the old man more than once, but without result.</p>
        <p>Yet be returned to London more firmly convinced than before that there was a secret somewhere. If his other plans had made it possible, he would have spoken to Helen herself; and he began to see how he could attempt that, after he had carried out his first scheme in regard to her. This occupied more time than he had cal- culated upon. On the day after his return to London he went to Mr. Garwood's office. at the time he knew Helen would be away at dinner. "I've another bit of business for you, Garwood. My friend—he never men- tioned Mrs. Caulfield by name, always refer- ring to her as "my friend," or "Madame"— "wants you to try and ascertain precisely the value and history of these jewels. Three of them are said to have been the property of Marie Antoinette; and she's very anxious, like Americana in such things, you'll say, to know if that's the truth t: he doesn't care</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>MOTHER</head>
