      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>Th. 2,6 beide contains three Ca,. 114 MLICh As the 1111 size. AlO u tiLlet 2/9„</head>
        <p>Th. 2,6 beide contains three Ca,. 114 MLICh As the 1111 size. AlO u tiLlet 2/9„</p>
        <p>what it costs if she can only get the 'real his. tory of the gems. The four rings are Portu- guese, she thinks; she was told so, at any rate, and they're said to be genuine antiques. The seals are Stuart things, pawned by some of that illustrions lot when they were in trouble in France. There are twelve of the things in all, you see. Give me a receipt for them, and do the best you can. She is not in any hurry, but do the thing as EOM as you can. It'll please her." 'file lawyer wrote the receipt, promising to do what he could, but remarking that he warn not much hand at such matters; and the jewels were put in the safe. Bostock then began to talk about the legal business, and presently a client called, and the solicitor went out to speak to him in the clerk's office. This gave Bostock. the chance he was waiting for, to test the duplicate key of the safe which he had made from the wax impression. It would not open the safe, so he blackened it with a preparation he had brought with him, and inserting it skilfully into the lock, he ascertained where the fault lay. When the lawyer returned the discussion about the business was resumed, and pre- sently 13ontock spoke of Ilelen. Garwood was high in his praise of her, and showed his visitor the arrangements he had made for her, the little room just off his own which he had furnished and the desk he had pro- vided. Boatock expressed his satisfaction with the arrangements, and then left. He had told Mary Pescott to meet him that afternoon, and the news she brought startled him considerably. "Mr. Trevellock is on his way back," she announced. "Helen had a cable from him this morning. He will be here in about a week."</p>
        <p>"What was the effect of the news upon her?" he asked anxiously. "She was very excited, and I think very upset. She sent a telegram on her way to the station. I watched her into the office. But I don't know to whom." - --</p>
        <p>Hostock guessed that it must be to Sir Gregory. He thought a moment and then said " You will leave Miss Paxton to- morrow; but without saying anything to her of your intention. I'll go back with you to IVest Kensington now." He hailed a hansom, and on the way ques- tioned her very closely about Helen's move- ments during the past few days. He dis- missed the cab before they reached the little fiat, walked the remainder of the distance, went in with her, and got her to show him which was Helen's room. He stayed only a few minutes, and before he left he gave her a sum of money, and promised to keep her supplied. He found a wire at his rooms from Mrs. Caulfield confirming the news that Bulmer was returning, a cable having tense from him from New York to the effect that he was sailing at once. Ile called again the next day at Garwood'. office, and made an opportunity of testing the alteration he had made to the safe key. "Have you done anything about those gems yet?" he asked the lawyer. "Not yet; bu. I have a man coming to- morrow afternoon to ace them. Has an expert in such things, and will tell me in a moment all about them."</p>
        <p>"Good," said Bostock, lightly. "And how's the fair typist?" "I was going to ask you. Is she in any trouble? She was very much agitated all yesterday, and again to-day. So different from before."</p>
        <p>"I don't know anything about her, my dear fellow. But I shouldn't take any notice if I were you. Let her go her own way. Won.en are like that at times."</p>
        <p>"I thought perhaps she had Borne love trouble, ' said Garwood; but Bostock only laughed and threw his hands up and went off. _</p>
        <p>"Fits in very sweetly," he murmured to himself. "Just the right sort of prepara- tion for to-morrow's business." . .</p>
        <p>On the following day, having watched the lawyer leave the office to go to lunch, Bostock went in and declared he would wait in the private office. The young clerk gave him the morning paper and left him alone. In a moment he had opened the este .and taken out three of the rings which he had confided to Garwood's care, and relocked the safe. One of them he took into Helen's little room and rolling it up in a piece of paper. thrust it away into the far corner of one of the drawers.</p>
        <p>Ten minutes later he went through to the clerk and declared that he could not wait for Mr. Garwood, but would call in the course of the afternoon. -</p>
        <p>On leaving the office he went by train to West Kensington, and let himself into the little flat with a key which he had taken from Mary Prescott. helen's trunk was in her room; and he chose that for the purpose of his visit.. It was locked; but the key was only • common one, and he had no difficulty in opening it with one of the little skeleton keys he carried with him. Ile rolled the other two rings he had taken from the safe in one of her handkerchiefs, and pushed them into one corner, and wrapped up the key of the sale and put that in another. "Pretty strong evidence, that,- was his very satisfied comment an, having relockeo the boa, be closed the door of the flat behind him and went off to hin club to lunch and await the developments. • About tour o'clock he was wanted at the</p>
        <p>vatephonc. Mr. taarwood had called him up to announce the fact that the rings were miss- ing. liostock was appropriately sceptical. "They can't be. I came to your office to-day about them, too. My friend wants three of them h, a hurry. You'd better look again." "I've hunted everywhere. I tell you they're gone." its mighty serious. Here, hold on, clear your people off home and I'll come down to go into the thing with you privately." He waited half an hour to make sure that Helen would have left; and then drove down to the office. He found the young lawyer in a condition of intense excitement, and let him talk himself out of breath.</p>
        <p>"You've got a thief here, man. That's the English of it. Now, which is it, the clerk or the •Orll It's one of them." .</p>
        <p>"It'r, impossible. Impossible. I'd answer for young Destwood absolutely." "Well, there'll be the devil to pay if you don't find the rings. Madame won't stand for it, and, of course, she'll take her business away. Let's look in the boy's desk. That's why I wanted you to clear both of them out of the way." They made a thorough search together, and of course found nothing. "Then it must be the girl. I wonder what Madame will say to this?" "I can't believe it for a moment," declared Garwood.</p>
        <p>-"Wei,- turn out her desk and see." He left the lawyer to do this. He made rather perfunctory search, and, somewhat to Boa- twit's dismay, did not find the ring. "I was sure of her," said Garwood gladly. "It's • mystery." .—• . .</p>
        <p>"Rings 'are 'not wings, man. They can't Ily," said Rostock irritably; and he began to pull the drawers right out and upset the con- tents on the desk, telling Garwood to look through everything again. 'f he discovery came this time. " " •"</p>
        <p>A• short whistle, and an exclamation of con- summate surprise from Garwood announced it. And he unfolded the ring from the paper and held it up. "What did I tell you?" asked Bostoek, with a shrug. "She's the thief; and we're up against a pretty stiff proposition in dealing with her."</p>
        <p>Garwood took the ring back into his private office and at staring at it with s look of pro- found trouble. "I'd have given fifty pounds rather than have found this," he declared, with a heavy sigh. The proof was inoontestible. Bostock watched him and smiled grimly. . _</p>
        <p>(To be continued.)</p>
        <p>C a g Gowan, on the Ba!morel estate, every year when the Court has bon in Scotland, and it Is Mr. Alias Matikenxie, son of the Iste Sir Allan MacKeesle, of Glenmulck, whom Miss Knollyeis to marry. She is a , very tall girl, with dark eyes and her full Christian names are Alexend a Lunettes Xlii tbeth, the second ' bring m deepi from the first letters of the names of the ' King's three sisters Louise, Victoria and Maud. New Fabrics.—A list of the DOA materials thi• year would be a very short one, for it would consist of less then half a dozen items. First in beauty end extrava- gant price are the metal and velvet brocade., whioo are E intern in magnificence, sumptuous and fniVastie, glowing with precious metals, which combining with the bloom of rich velvet nod the eherta of satin, make a materiel that is fairy-like in its loveliness and richness. Next must be mentioned the various donble-faoed materials, drsigned for everyday weer, which are most substantial looking. It is many years since the nuitinas have been no servictable. They are thick, and very closely woven, usually Leo-faced, or two coloured. It will be quite safe to predict that very few one colour thron ;hunt costumes —with which we have been no long familiar will be worn this winter. Lastly there are the woollen velvets. The legions of msterialx that have had a vogue in prwrieu I sesame, end are still , seen, are made up, or rather used differently. Black velvet for instance was used last wiwer &amp;Imola entirely for walking costumes. Tots season it is replacing to • certain extent, the transparent materials, such as chiffon, ninon and voiles, for afternoon and evening dresses, but it will lose none of its popularit y as a materiel for tailor-made costumes on this account, whilst for the new shot velvets, a warm welcome in assured. The vogue for lace may in Rome meneure account for the return to favour of velvet and velveteen for evening wear. Taffeta is much lewd in conjunction with velvet, and alone for afternoon dresses, whilst it is still in evidence for blouaes. About Sleeves.—Sleeves are a mush discussed question this year. In those shown in the Psris houses, the fulness at the (dhow characteristic of the bell-sleeve, is often simulated by ruffled under. 'fever, or by a variation of the bell shape. Everywhere, it IS admitted that a change of styles will come, and when it does, it will begin with the sleeve. For th• present no radical change can be attempted, since the !mutant faelcion ho s taken too strong a hold on popular taste, but when the new r6;ime makes its appearance, it will be heralded by a new style of sleeve, to ehich the other parts of dress will accommodate themselves. The kircona shape still leadn, but it is riot without a rival, as was the case last year. Many sleeves are separate from the bodice, and some are sewed into the armhole in the orthodox way, others are eased io, or have a little drapery which is suggestive of the penitent sleeve. Others again, instead of being sewn into the armhole of the bodice, are attached to the liniog only the outer sleeve being piped, to cover the NOM The separate sleeve ions a iole lung and close-fitting, or it hes some fulness at the elbow, and is slashed at the hand, the opening keing trimmed with buttons and button holes and a frill of lace. The kimoua sleeve this season is generally cut in one with the yoke or with the upper part of the bodice. Coats are still cut straight with no current the waist, and close fitting round the hips. They may be marls perfectly plain, with a plain sleeve like a man's if 60 preferred. Among the new models are coats not exceeding :line. ia length. . . . . . .</p>
        <p>A Useful Suit—Toe knitted coat will take on a long lease of life in the guise it has been presented this winter, his now possible to buys suit oonsisting of knitted coat, cap and tweed skirt to match perfectly. Nothing more useful or sensible for winter wear in town or country could be devised, moreover the styli unite middle aged and young alike. The phineet of tweeds should be chosrn for the 6UCCMS of the suit depends upon the exact matching of the cost and skirt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>WOKEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOKEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>India and the Royal Visit.—All reports from India I Agree in stating that the departure of the Kiog and Queen for Delhi, Ins been regarded with the greatest possible interest, and that their approaching visit to ' the Indian Empire is anticipated an the most important event of the la. half century. Of course only a very small proportion of the 300 millions of the population of India, ban expect to see their Erripero -, but an attempt ham wisely been made to secure the r 'presenta- tion of distant parts of the country, sod when these representatives return to their lowan and villages, they sill have wondrous tales to tell of the splendours of the great city which had been enlarged for the occasion to an area of thirty square mile.. The Gorgeous Ea...—. Royal weather " hers not conspicuously favoured the functions of this great year, and it was raining and blowing when the Medina, with their Majesties on board, left Portsmouth nit Saturday. Such disagreeable conditions are to be expected is November, and they remind returning Anglo-Indians that there are, at any rate, some compensations for spending their lives tinder " the burning Eastern sun." 'the voyage to the East, soon takes one into the warm sunlit seam, and after arriving at Bombay on December 2,tbeir Majesties should experience the most delightful weather during the whole of their six weeks stay In lodis At that time of year the poet's "gorgeous East" with its blaze of colour, and esure splendid, is seen stirs beet. The climate of Northern India is then almost everything toe could with for, and compares with our own dismal winter conditions, very much the same as day in to the night. Christmas Is always a gay time with Anglo-Indians, notwithstanding that its festivities are shorne of same of its associations in the old country, and vest home shall miss this year the customary Yuletide announcement of the arrival of the baron of beef and the boar's head, for the Royal table on Cbristm. Day. Society Matisca —A story has got al out that the I Queen is to be the guest at a Hindu ladies' dinner- party in India, at which everyone situ on the floor, and eats with their lingers. Nothing could be snore improbable than that her Al•jesty would think of doing anything of the kind, bet there are not a few of her people 14 to roam to be getting madder every day, and some of them are trying to start a freak fashien in London, of sitting crost- legged on the floor, and eating their food with their fingers. One would have to he loosely clothed, and muttering from Moans paralysis of the brain, to do that with comfort, but there are always some people ready to go to any length, in mskiag themselves ridiculous. An effort is being made to ID tto Society dinner hour earlier and for that there is • g ion deal to be said. When Jane Ainten's Fanny Price went to her first dinner party, the htor was at five, but it has come to be so absurdly late that, in the ordinary way, there is no time left to make much farther use of the evening. Coort Fayouritest—Fullowiog the marriage this week of Mira Blanche Lamella!, Maid of Honour to Queen Alexandra, to Mr. George Lloyd, ef,P., cosies the ennotincement of the engagement of Miss Lou•ino Koollys, who ha• been • good deal with the Royal Family from her earliest childhood. Born in Iftedl, she la the only d oghter of Lord and Lady Knollys, and has always lived with her parents in their apartments is St. J•mea Palace aed Windsor Castle. Since the death of Que. Victoria, Lord sod Lady Kaolly• have occupied</p>
      </div>
