      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>Examiner Saturday November 18 1911</head>
        <p>The Examiner Saturday November 18 1911</p>
        <p>If DISTRICT tipris E</p>
        <p>Fearnhead.</p>
        <p>- UISLt'Ir INDEPENDENT METHODIST CH I: RC ir.—A social was held in connection with the above place of worship on Saturday evening last. Mr. Peter Lythgoe occupied the chair end spoke of the progress the church had made in the last few years. A good number of persons were present and a very enjoyable evening was giant. Games Ac, were indulged in and selections were given on the pathophone by Mho Bate, About 8.30 the company partook of supper which had been provided by the ladies. The proceeds are for the reduction of the debt.</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath. HILL CLIFFE AND STcCICION HEATH UNITED BAND OF HocE.—On Monday evening this society held its sewed entertainment of the session in Hill Cli fie Schoolroom. The Rev. George Curtis, president of the societT, occupied the chair and gave an address on " Habits," which was listened to very attentively. A good programme had been provided by the members and friends, recitations being given by Gladys Pemberton, Lucy Gerrard, Genie Chile, Agnes Mips, James Parker, Mi,s E Turner, Miss NI Kilk'and and , Miss Amy Little wood. Mr J J Worrell caused much I amusement with a recitation in the Lancashire dialect. Songs were well rendered by Dolly Cain,Miss M Minefield and Miss Mellor, of Warrington, end pianoforte solos were given by Ethel Houghton and Francis Philps. A well executed piano and mandoline duet was contributed by Eva Alcock and Sophie Cross. The dialogues "Six I ttle nurses," by members of Bold-street Band of Hope and "rho right kind of music," by Elsie Whitfield, Ethel I longhton and Doily Cain were much enjoyed. A vote of thanks to all who contributed to the evening's enjoyment was given on the proposition of Mr. G. Whitfield, secretary. Master Sydney Pemberton, Miss Ethel Houghton and Mr Harry Mellor acted as accompanists.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="0" unit="words">
