      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>Croft.</head>
        <p>Croft.</p>
        <p>MEMORIAL TO THE LATE MRS. WADESON.—A meet- ing was held io the vestry at Christ Church, on Sttur- day, under the presidency of the Rector (Rev. H. I. Wadeson) to consider plans and estimates for the pro- posed alterations in the chancel t f the church, and an application for a faculty. The faculty sought for is to enable the Rector to erect a memorial rerodos beneath the east window, and to panel in oak the remainder of the east well, the cost to he defrayed by the Rector. The faculty is also to enable the parishioners et Croft to panel in oak the north and south walls of the chancel and to replace the present commut ion rails with others more suitable, the cost to be defrayed by donations. The Rector presided. It was decided that the 'resent communion rAls be replaced by brass ones of a chaste design, the estimate for these being £3O ; also that the estimates for Oaanelling the north and south wells for £49 be accepted, making a totel of .C 79 rs the parishioners' portion of the alterations. The Rector was requested to obtain faculty to uncover two windows (at piesent bricked up) in the north and south walls of the chancel. This will not cnly admit more light ; but Mrs. P. Ingham has undertaken to provide suitable inscriptions on her own behalf at a maximum cost of £25 in memory of her dear parents.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>Padgate.</head>
        <p>Padgate.</p>
        <p>RAILWAY CHANGES.—The many friends of our obliging, courteous and genial stationmaster, Mr. F. Newton, will receive with great regret the news that he ban to sever bin connection with Padgata. It in highly satisfactory to learn of his appointment to the more important position of stationmaster at Garston. Alter a varied experience of work in several places for the C.L.C. he came to Padgate about four osars ago. Few men, we think, are able to maintain kindness so unvary- ing and disposition so cheerful as Mr. Newton, whose promotion at who have come into contact with him will hail with the greatest sotisfaction. Mr. Edwards, of West Derby, has arrived to take up the position vacated by Mr. Newton,</p>
        <p>• PARISH COUNCIL. A special meeting of the Padgate Parish Council IVES held in the Church of England school on Monday. Mr. R. Denham (chairman), presided the other Connelllore present being Dr Bennett, Messrs di Clad)ley, J Houghton, W S Howard, Wr Potter, Walker and F Wright (clerk). A letter was read from the clerk of the Rural District Council, respecting the suggested flagging of the foot- paths of the township. The Clerk was directed to reply,stating that a resolu- tion on the matter had been passed in March, 1910, at a pariah meeting and that the Council in asking for the work to proceeded with was simply carrying out the decision of that meetirg. The Clerk was also instructed to ask tbt County Surveyor to extend the footpath to meet the rt meats of two now semi-detached houses in Manche..ter- road. After considerable discussion it wag resolved— " That the Clerk write to the County Surveyor with respect to an obstruction under Spittal Bridge, Padding- ton, the farmers having already at considerable expense, cleared out the brook. This obstruct ion prevents the full flow of the water and the Council would be glad it this could be removed." THE SEWAGE SCHEME.</p>
        <p>In view of the reported decision of the Rural District Council to proceed with a sewage scheme for the town- ship, was resolved that the Clerk, upon obtaining con- firmation of the matter, ask that the Surveyor be allowed to attend a meeting of the Parish Couocil, so that the scbems may be thoroughly discussed and understood by the members.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>WESLEY GUILD RALLY.</head>
        <p>WESLEY GUILD RALLY.</p>
        <p>The members of tho Wesley Guilds of the Warrington Wesleyan Circuit have had a busy week end and Padgate hes been the centre of activity. The Rev. W. B. Fitzgerald, founder and secretary of the Wesley Guild, was present at a meeting of Guild worksra held on Saturday evening in the schoolroom end gave a very helpful and instructive addresa, bearing chiefly on the best and most modern methods of Guild administration. On Sunday the Rev. NV, B. Fitzgerald occupied tee pulpit at both morning and evening services. It being: Temperance Sunday the rev. gentleman emphasized the urgent need of Temperance reform in this country, remarking that we were a very long way behind our colonies and the U.S.A. ahe morning sermon wan based upon the words, " This is the end. . . . . Fear God and keep his commandments," Eccl. xii , 13, RN. At the evening service the preacher took for his text the words 'What think ye of Cadet," Matt. %sib, 4 . The choir rendered the anthem "Call to remembrance" (V. Novello), Mr. Levi Richardson being heard to advantage as tenor soloist. After this service a short devetiensi reeling was held, several local Guild caisalbers taking part. On Tuesday evening a grand united rally of the Guilds of the Warrington Circuit wis held. Mr Frank Harris presided, and was supported ot the platform by ths Revs NV B Fitzgerald, NV A Becket, E Morgan, H Willismson, R B lintobloson, and also by Dr Bennett said Messrs William Healey and NV IS Phaeton, the president, vice-president, and secretary respectively of the Circuit Guild Council. After the Rev W A Becket bad offered prayer, the secretaries of the fallowing Guilds each gave a short report of work accomplished and progress made. Bold- street, Bevesey-rced, Sankey Bridges, Latchford, Padgate, Miters and Stockton Heath. The report of Peuketh Guild was reed on thetr behalf by Mr Phaeton In response to the Roll Call by the Rev. E. Morgan, the members of each Guild stood and sang suitable verses, the well chosen verse and plucky efforts of the few Moore friends calling forth spontaneous applause. Later in the evening this incident drew from Bev. W. B. Fitzgerald an amusing story to this effect. For • circuit guild rally, a certain country guild had arranged toning, as a response to the Roll Call, averse of' Dare to be a Daniel." Through an accident en route to the conveyance in which they were travelling, the members were prevented from attending in time to answer the Roll. One member however, not having accompanied his confrere's, was present in time, and when 'bin Guild was called upon he solemnly sang " solos "the appointed verse, to the huge merriment of the audience. The address of Rev. W. B. Fitzgerald was eloquent suggestive, practical, and finely relieved by a fund of humonrous stories and illustral ions. Having very recently returned from the Ecuminioal Conference in Toronto, the rev, gentleman spoke on " Yonth and Enterprise in the New World." After a brief reference to Dr. Nansens claim that the original disco ee America were Norsemen, the speaker went on to comment on the political value of the C.P.R., and if its importance as a bond of union from East to West. From this he drew the lesson that the churches and particalarls the Methodist churches, needed to be more united. "Union by communication" as 300 miles were concocted by the C.P.R. was the necessity of the churches of to-day. He reported that an effort Is to hems&amp; (by the simultaneous publination, in all parts of the world, of( a aeries of articles, by eminent men) to drew the young people of the churches more closely together. Speaking of the Ecomincal Con- ference Mr. Fitzgerald mid that it represented 32,000,000 Methodists living under twelve different governments. Referring to Canada he said that the enthusiasm and activity of the young people, both in church and all other matters had greatly impressed him. In Canada enterprise was in the climate and character of the people was contagion" Those who did not intend to work were not wanted there and no " Weary Willies" need apply. On the preposition of the Rev. R. C. Hutchinson, seconded by Rev. H. Williamson, an omnibus vote of thanks was carried unanimously with applause. The Chairman and Mr. Fitzgerald replied. The large new schoolroom was well filled, about 450 people ht lug present. The local Guild bas this seesion an excellent syllabus next week's meeting being taken up by Mr. Medley Taylor's lantern lecture, "The incessant sterfare in nature."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>Culcheth.</head>
        <p>Culcheth.</p>
        <p>NEWCIFIURCH }TOOL—A teaparty and dance in aid of the Melanesian mission, wee held in the schoolroom on Saturday evening last. The arrangements for the evening had been successfully organised by the mem- bers of the young ladies' Bible class. After tea dancing was indulged in. the accompanist being Miss Marsh end Mr J Orme. The evening throughout was a huge success, the schoolroom being packed, to that • good sum was realised for a worthy object.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>Hatton.</head>
        <p>Hatton.</p>
        <p>FARM Flog—About half-past nine on Tuesday morn- ing tbe was seen leaning from a building belonging to Mr. James Pair, Hatton Hall. The Warrington Fire Brigade was telephoned for. In the meantime a goodly number of the villagers assembled in the vicinity of the fireand rendered excellent service with pails of water by keeping the fire from spreading to the adjoin- ing buildings in which was valuable a trick, corn, &amp;c. About ten o'clock the Warrington Fire Brigade arrived on the scene and Mr. Turner end his colleagues soon had the fire well under control. About ten to twelve tons of newly threshed oat straw was destroyed. The men succeeced in getting some carts and implements out and also a valuable bull which sum stalled just beneath the ft e. The roof was completely gutted. As yet there is no clue to the origin of the fire, which is said to be covered by insurance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>Daresbury.</head>
        <p>Daresbury.</p>
        <p>PETTY SESSIONS. TUESDAY.—Before Messrs William Long (in the chair), A H Talbot, Roger C Parr, CI Dakin and W Fletcher. DANGEROUS TRESPASSING.—EIizebeth Parker, 19, Bark Dolmans-lane, Warrington, and Margaret Camp- bell, 16, Dolmans-lane, were summoned for trespassing on the railway at Walton Old Junction end exposing themselves to danger or risk of danger on September 26th. Detectives Clarke and Goode stated that defendants were walking about the sidings picking up coal. A floe of 10s. and costs was inflicted in each case. OBSTRUCTI ON BY A MOTOR CA R.—El9l3llt Samuel Abraham, Stanley House, Lancashire Hill, Stockport. was summoned for leaving a motor car as as to obstruct the highway at Grappenhall. Defendant, who did not aprear, was represented by Mr. Sidebottom. It was alleged that the car was left stranded on the highway with no one in charge of it, and it was moved into a aide road. Mr. Sidebottom said the defendant was driving the car from Liverpool to Stockport and on arriving at Grappenhall the car broke down. Defendant asked someone to look after it while Jae went to Warriogton and telephoned to his employ /Pa, who told him to have it towed into Warrington to be repaired. Defendant was ordeted to pay the coats.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>Lymm.</head>
        <p>Lymm.</p>
        <p>CONG RIO ATIONAL CHRISTIAN S envies BAND.—A good attendance of membem were present at this meeting on Monday evening last. A stirring address was given by Mr. Stephen Ackerley, of Lymm, on "The Unseen Powers of the world," and was listened to with great interest and greatly approved by all. A solo was was rendered by Mr. Alfred Males" In the secret of His presence." Mr. Samuel Moss presided.</p>
        <p>CONGREGATIONAL CH CRCII.—The missionary FETViCEE were held in this church on Sunday last, when the Rev. J. Richardson, who is a returned missionary, occupied the pulpit. He took for his disconrse in the morning the command of Christ to his disciples before He left the earth Go ye therefore and teach all nations." In the afternoon Mr. Richardson gave an address in the Sunday school, his subject being How thoy teach the heathens in Madagascar to love their children." He was listened to with great interest by the children. During the afternoon the missionary boxes were opened, a sum of over £6 being realised. At the evening service a good congregation sons present, Mr. Richardson giving some of his experiences in Madagsscar which were very interesting. The services all passed off in a most successful manner, and the collections were most sat is- factory. They were all devoted to the Loudon Missionary Society. THE LATE MR. WILLIAM Dovnts.—The funeral took place on Monday at Higher-lane Baptist Chapel, of the late Mr. William Davies, Hill Croft, Bollington, for many years a member of the Backlow Guardians and the Education Sub-committee at Lymm. The service was conducted by the Rev. Mr. Lennox of Manchester, and there was a large gathering of sympathising relatives and friends at the graveside. The chief mourners were : Miss Davies, Mrs Davies. Mn J Davies, Mr Sam Gould, Mrs Hndswell, Mrs Norman Hudswell, Miss Lucy Davies, Miss Mildred Davies, Mr 3 Niel&amp; Mr James Gould, Mr William Gould, Mr W W Baker, Mr and Mrs Jackson, Miss Hannah Vast, Nurse Sprott, Mr and Mrs GI Clarke and Mr tindswell. The Rev. Mr. Lennox reviewed the life of deceased and spoke feelingly of his work, portion- larly in connection with the children and the lligher- lane chapel.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>PETTY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>The Lynne Petty Sessions were held on Wednesday before Mr. Alfred Watkin (in the chair) and Major J. E.G. Groves. .6 THE LAW MUST BE OBETED."—Walter Fitter, Hood-lane, Sankey, was summoned for being the owner of a locomotive that did not carry lights that could be temporarily shut off. A constable stated that he saw • locomotive of which the defendant was the owner being driven by a man named Fletcher en the highway. It Lad two old swir ging lamps in the front which could not he ehaded. He questioned the driver who stated that those were the only kind of lamps he had ever carried.—Superintendent Sutherland said that the worst thing about the lamps was that they showed a bright light and any vehicle approaching from the rear at • quick speed would not be able to distinguish which way the locomotive was proceeding. The Clerk : Of course, this act came late operation before the time of motor oars. The idea was that the lights would be able to be shaded, or temporarily shut off on the approach of a horse-dritett veh,cle. The defendant mid the lights cat ried at the prezent time 'we much superior to those thst were used when the Act was psssed. They had adopted the awing lamps because they gave a better light and did not dazzle anything approaching. During his experience in the business extending over 30 years he had never previously been cautioned about the using of dose lamps. It was the custom of all threshing machines at the present time to carry this class of lamp.—The Bench ordered the defendant to pay the costs (49. 6d.) A LAZY HUSBAND RECEIVES HIS DESERTS.—Lnoy Brazendale, Park View, Statham, summoned her husband, Edward Brazendate, 1, Moston-street, Lymm, for neglecting to provide maintenance for her, thereby causing her to leave him, and asking for a separation order.—The complainant said that they had been married 13 years. During the last eight weeks the defendant had only given her 16s. He was formerly worked with to threshing machine but he had given it up through drink, and would not work. Three weeks aro the defendant signed the pledge and promisod to reform but a week later he came home drunk. Com- plainant had been compelled to work and keep the home whilst the defendant would not work and went getting drunk. She had previously left him on two occasional but had gone back on his promising to reform.—The Benda re::red and upon their return the Chairman said that with regard to the defendant's previous bad record they had been compelled to grant a separation order with 63. 644. a week, the costs were remitted. A YOUTH'S ESCAPADE—Herr! Taylor. &amp;whereon. Lymm,. farm labourer, was charged with furiously riding • bicycle in New-road Lymm on the 301 h October. A constable stated that he saw the defendant at 6.35 p.m. on the day in question riding a bicycle at 18 miles an hoar. His head was almost down on the handle bars and he narrowly missed knocking a woman down. Defendant was called upon to stop but did not take any notice. The constable was able to identify him ash. palmed one of the street lamps.—A fine of 2.. 6d. was imposed. MI,CELLASEOUS.—For riding a bicycle without a light en October 20th, Herbert Talbot, Etuslogreen, Lymm, a farm labourer, was fined 2s 6.1, while for a similar offence a MOD against Harold Keenan, 14, Baxter-street Warrington, was dismissed upon his statement that the top ot the lamp was broken off through it falling to the ground. For being drunk and acting in a disorderly manner in Lymm villlge on the 4th inst. Iwo Wilson, Rushgreen, Lymm, a labourer, was fined 55.. while for being dram's on Octocer 21st, Willi am Cadman. Beech wood-lane, Lymm, a labourer, was fined in a like amount. WARRINGTON MAN ix TRW. I r.E.—At especial sitting of the Lymm Petty Sessions on Saturday, before the Hon. J. Cross and Captain Groves, John Jeffreys, of Warrington, who is engaged as a porter at Heatley Station, ass charged with an assault upon to girl named Hilda Wood. Mr. Steel of Warrington, appeared for the defendant and said that it was one of those inex- plainable CaSell with which magistrates had occasionally to deal. The defendant was only 20 years of age, and was the main support of his mother and brothers and sisters. lie was a young man of previous good char- ncter, and be asked the magistrates to meet the case with a floe. and not to read the defendant to prison.— The magistrates retired, and on their return the Chair- man stated that they had given every consideration to the circumstances urged by Mr. Steel, and had deter- mined to inflict a fine of 40s. including costs.</p>
        <p>-- pVrOMEN'S RIGHTS.—WHY pay enormous price., when for the sum of 30. we can make you a seven gorel Tailor-made skirt from our famous Homespun Costume Cloths is Black, Navy, Brown, Wine, Grey and Green to your own measurements; satisfaction guaranteed. FREE to every purchaser we present a handsome :12 Carat Gold Cased Necklet and Cameo Pendant for a limited time only. Send P.O. also length, waist and hip measures to HAIGH &amp; Co., Manufacturers, 4, Thornton rd., Bradford, Yorks.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>ENJOY LIFE'S BLESSINGS. The Experience of Thousands of Working</head>
        <p>ENJOY LIFE'S BLESSINGS. The Experience of Thousands of Working</p>
        <p>Men and Women.</p>
        <p>An Extraordinary Food Beverage.</p>
        <p>No food beverage at present on the market contains no much that in nourishing to brain and body as DR. TIBBLES' VI-COCOS. Manufactured on a scientific basis, and approved by the highest medical authori- ties, it contnins not only Cocoa, but three other ingredients (Kola, Malt and Hort), all of which have remarkable nutritive and health-giving properties, and which, in combination, form • most irresistible foe to 1 dyspepsia and kindred ailments. 'thousands of working people are to day enjoying health to a degree which they never experienced before taking to VI-COCOA, and lair file contains thousands cf letters testifying to the benefits derived from its use. Try VI COCOA to-day, and prove for yourself the extraordinary health-giving properties of this popular food-beverage. Give Vi-Cocra a trial. Sever mind what you've been drinking at breakfast or slipper in the past. wait drinking Vi. Cocoa to-dry— you'll feel the benefit of it at once. Do not ask your grocer ford Cocoa "—ask for</p>
