      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>arael*</head>
        <p>arael*</p>
        <p>—lt makes all the difference. Every grocer sells Vi-Cocos in Gd. packets and 9d. and Is. 6d. tins.</p>
        <p>Great Sankey. WRIST Du I VE.—About SO ladies and gentlemen joint d together on Friday evening at the Great Sankey Sunday schools to have a game of %hist, arranged by Messrs F Davies, .1 Woodward and E Dumbill. Mesdames G Clarke, F Davies, J Ray, jun., and W Woods were in charge of the refreshments. The room, as usual, was very nicely decorated. Prizes were very kindly given by the Rev J It Jones and the Misses Tinsley. The prize-winners were :—Ladies : 1, Miss Webster; 2, Miss Elsie Woods ; consolation prize, Mrs T Cook. entlemen : 1, Mr .1 Ray ; 2, Mrs Brocklehurst (play- ing as gentleman); consolation, Mr E Dumbill.—Miss Tinsley handed the prizes to the respective winners. These pleassnt evenings are for the good object of renovating the Infants school at Penketh. Meta CLASS SOCIAL.—Ia connection with the St. Mary's Church Bible Class a social was held on Saturday is the Sunday schools, and provided a very enjoyable and successful evening. After tea, a concert was held and Rev J It Jones presided. The Sankey Choral Society opened with" Comrades' song of hope." Miss Elsie Clarke gave " When the heart is young." Mr E Darlington gave a humorous song entitled "Paper Bag Cookery." A quartette party of the Penketh prize brass band gave three selections in very pleasing style. The concert over, games and dancing were taken up. Two whist drives wire held, first game, winners ; ladies : 1, Miss Knowles. Gentlemen : IMr H Ray. Consolation prizes: \ 1 re S Leigh and Ms Webb; Second garne,ladies : 1, Miss Jackson. Gentlemen : 1, Mr J Ray, junior. Consolation prizes: Miss Baines and Mr R Ray. Supper was served between nine and ten o'clock under the charge of Mesdames G Clarke and Davies, assisted by the committee. The Rev .1 It Jones gave a short address. The prizes were very generously given by Mr E G Medley, the Misses Jones and Gorse. The prize winners received their prizes from the hands of the Vicar. Miss Foster and Mrs F Davies were the accompanists for the dancing and singing respectfully. linketh.</p>
        <p>LABOUR PARTY.—The other evening a potato pie supper was held in t'-e Infants schoolroom, Penketh, arranged by the Entertaining Committee of the Building Fund Scheme, and great credit was due to them for such a successful evening. The schoolroom was far too small to enable all to attend and was crowded by 8.0 p.m. when supper was served. After- wards there was some speech making and then games and dancing were indulged in to a late hour. Councillor Forshaw of St. Helens, the Laboorparty agent, wits the principle speaker. He said the Labour party exiated to bring more leisure, treasure and pleasure,into the homes of the toiling masses. The people who should enjoy the good things of this life were the men and women who by their labour produced the wealth which made the good things of life possible for anyona The object of the Labour party was to make it es hard as possible for anyone to live a life of idleness out of the labour of others, and to make it as easy as possible for all those who were willing to Wore to get work under conditions which would enable Halm to live a full and happy human life. They must remember that the masses were not poor because they were poor producers of wealth, but because much of the tasalth that they pro- duced was taken away from them in the shape of dividends, ground rents, mining royalties and other such ways. Take the railways as an example, last year 47 millions was paid in dividend while something like 100,000 railway workers received wages varying from 15s to £1 per week. The problem which faced all workers was how to get more of the wealth which was produced into the pockets of those who produced it and only a workers' party could solve that problem. They wanted all workers to unite together to send their own representatives under their own control to all governing bodies. Representatives ash) would use their voice and vote to win for the toilers the wealth which their labour produced. He was glad that they were very much alive in Penketh and urged them to go on with the good work of extension and organisation so that when the next election arrived they would return Mr. Seddon to the House of Commons to fight on behalf of the mwses of the people for those better conditions of life and labour which they are justly entitled to. The ladies were heartily thanked for their services and all those who had worked to bring the entertainment to a success. Mr. G. Faulkner said they were indebted to Mr. Fershaw for coming that night and speaking to them, and said the local Labour party congratulated him on his recant vietory in Hardshaw Ward, St. Helens, in the municipal election.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>TECHNICAL SCHOOL PRIZE DISTRIBUTION. BISHOP WELLDON ON OUR COMMERCIAL SUPREMACY. The Dean of Manchester, the Right Rev. Bishop Welido°, D.D., visited St. Helens Town Hall on Tues- day night, and distributed the prizes gained by the students of the Municipal Technical School and the Evening Continuation Schools. Colonel W. W. Pilkington, D.L.,chairman of the Education Committee, presided. Bishop Welldon said that the country was subject now, and would be more subject in the future, to an industrial and commercial competition such as it had Dever known in the long centuries of its eventful history. There was not only tha competition of European nations, there was the far more formidable of the East. So far as he could judge, there was only one thing which could maintain the supremacy of British commerce—the good faith, the honeet sterling quality of goods sent out—the inviolable uprightness of British merchants. That was the asset which- was most important to our country, and he prayed that not one of them would ever, whatever his lot might be in the future, do alything to tarnish or impair tha credit of British good faith. The time had gone by when the citizens could afford to be drunkards, gamblers or wastrels. The Empire and ' the nation needed them all to be God-fearing men and women. The age of democracy was coming in with a full tide. He felt no dread of democracy ; for his own part was atri inevitable, it. 0 Whether they remained welcomedab oeal itf oor, DILL ministry of religion to lift the thoughts of the masses of the people above the poor personal interests of the present world, and so to dienipline and cultivate by every means which it was possible to employ, that they in their generation might hand down to the generation which was to come after them a country not debased, not degraded, but even stronger and higher and better than it had been in the past. (Applause). THE REAL EMPIRE. MR. WILL CROOKS, M.P., AT ST. HELENS. A largely attended meeting was held on Monday night at St. Helens Tows Hall in connection with the St. Helens and District Banclof Hope and Temperance Union, at the conclusion of a special ten de a' mission. Mr. J. B. Leach, J.P. (president), occupied the chair, and the speaker was Mr. Will Crooks, M.P. Mr. Crooks mid that teetotalers had now become respectable, but he remembered the time when they were not allowed to amociate with respectable people, who regarded them as the long-faced, watery crowd, who were alwals miserable. Ile welcomed the great change that had come over the habits of the people. In the Corpo-aticn of London there were now twenty aldermen and couneillors who were teetotalers, and were not ashamed of It, whish was the most important thing of all. Last gear they bad a real live mayor who gave a temperance reception at the Mansion House. They constantly beard a greet deal about the Empire, and saw large maps of it ; but the empire he was concerned about was the happiness, contentment, and sobriety of the toilers. The foundation of his empire was the work- men's homes. Some day Borneo°e would have the courage to get up and any, " Enough of you sod your Empire ; remember the people." He was out to take his share in the work of attending to the real empire of the people —the home. They cried for Protectioo for their Empire, but they wanted protection for the homes and the home life of the people. That was much more important than a big Navy. The care of the children was of the first importance to the nation, and the children from the home of the drunken father and mother were handicapped as they sever ought to he. The remedy for that was active, persevering, prayerful work—work which he hoped would be taken up most heartily is St. Helens by all rections. The address was followed with great interest by the audience, who hugely enjoyed the many racy pampa.</p>
        <p>A vote of timnlce was {visaed on the motion of Dr. J. J. Buchan, medical officer, neconded by the Rev. J. Gwilym MAYOR'S SUNDAY. - -</p>
        <p>The Mayor and Corporation of St. Helens attended service on Sunday morning at the Parish Church, accompanied by detachments of police and Territorials. I The procession started from the Town Hall headed by ! the baud of the sth South Lancashire Regiment, and a section of the borough police force. Detachments of the Lancashire Hussars, Imperial Yeomanry, the 2nd Lancashire Royal Engineers, sdh South Lancashire and 2nd Lancashire Field Ambulance, R.A.M.C., were on parade. The Mayor (Alderman Beecham) was I accompanied bv the Town-clerk (Mr. W. H. Andrews) I and Alderman Foote (deputy Mayor). The service at the church wee conducted by the Rev' Albert lining. (vicar), Revs. C. M. Cbavasse, and F. T. Page. The vicar preached from the words, " Come unto me and I will give you rest," He said those who presided in the council-chamber did not merely hold a social position, but were really discharging a highly religious duty. They not only represented the temporal interests of the people, but they were, or , should be, the guardians of their moral welfare. If that was recognised then there was no room for despondency. Yet there was a tendency to despair of our town and city life. That was an unwarranted pessimism. No doubt even in their own town a very dark picture could be drawn, in spite of the marvellous advanoe of the last decade or eo. The miserable conditions under which many of the poor live ; the Saturday night orgies, which are a disgrace to civilisa- tion; and the widespread neglect of Sunday observance, itself a menace to any community. Those, ani other things which might well be mentioned, were depressing enough, yet none could doubt but that there was steady improvement. They could only realise the great advance which had been made in recent times when they flung the searchlight back over three or four generations. What they wanted was more goodwill. They must begin by giving others the credit for good intentions, then try to realise the other man's point of view. There must also be a recognition of spiritull things.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>MORALITY OF WIDNES. VICAR'S GRAVE STATEMENT.</p>
        <p>The Mayor of Widnes (Councillor F. Neill) presided over a special meeting of the Police Court and Prison Gate Mission, at the Widnes Town Hull, on Monday evening. He was supported by Mr. Stuart Deacon (Liverpool Stipendiary), Alderman Owens Councillor T. Smith, and the Rev. H. Tibbins (vicar), Septimus Jones, B. W. Pheysey (secretary), and Mr. J. Fleming (missionary). Mr. Deacon said he admired the work of the Police Court and Prison Gate Mission, and was always glad to assist them at any meeting calling for support to the funds. He could think of no work more essentially Christ-like and divine than that which strove to hold out its hand tv a mvn and woman who had sinned against society, who had made himself or herself in greater or less degree an outlaw and an outcast, and to whom help at such a time most be of the greatest of all possible kindnesses, and to whom at any time a kindly word from a brother or sister was of the greatest possible use and assistance. Referring to the recent strike in Liverpool,Mr.Deacon said that one of its aspects was that it had opened the eyes of the people, who bad not thought much about it to the conditions ender vhich the people lived. Local authorities were doing their test to cope with the evil, and the Liverpool Corporation were the pioneers in the direction of improving the houses of the working people of Liverpool. The Rev. Stephen Jones (vicar of St. lVfary's, West Bank), moving a vote of thanks to the Mayor for pre- siding and to Mr. Deacon, said that if the meeting was not going to be unreal he was folly persuaded that, among other things, something ought to be done with regard to the posters on the walls in the town. He also referred to the number of unhappy marriages that took place in the town, and alleged that 50 per cent, of the marriages that took place in his own church were forced cases.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN LIBERALISM.</p>
        <p>LADY RYLES ON INTERNATION L CRIMINALS,</p>
        <p>Lady Bylet, wife of the member for Salford, and president of the National Women's Liberal Association was the chief speaker at a Liberal meeting at Runcorn on Tuesday evening. She said she welcomed manhood suffrage as the realisation of an ideal she had hoped for all her life. The next bill could not meet the fate of Mr. Harcourt's bill of four years ago, for the House of Lords had gone. Labour was now king in this country, and they bad to take care that lebour got justice. Hitherto it had not, for the rich man had often forty or fifty votes to the poor man's one. The chaos of electoral anomalies, exasperating and costly, were to be swept away, and she recalled the words of Gladstone, "Liberty alone tits men for liberty." The Insurance Bill, the Speaker said, would be a worthy successor to the Wotkmen's Compensation Act, under the provisions of which ik2,7C0,000 had been paid as compensa'ion in 1910. There were anxious times abroad and the spectacle had been witnessed of one nation stealing the territory of another without any provocation whatever. We in this country wire supposed to have some difficulty with Germany, but we had no quarrel with a people to whom we owed much. Men who talked of war between two such nations were international criminals. The people must make Governments that would not dare to go to war, but would choo e a more equitable way of settling disputes. (Applause.) The Chairman (Mr. Roscoe Brunner, of Messrs. Brunner, Mood, and Co.), eaid he hoped the difficulties between masters and men now existing throughout the country would be settled without the necessity of stalker. Everyone must admit that labour did sot get Its fair share of the profits, and every employer ef labour must recognise that a larger share must be paid out in the future. The* ff rot of a strike was felt, first' by women and children, and he knew districts in Liver- pool where childrrn tad died by the score because they were deprived of the cecessities of life at the time of the strike. He hoped the railway and mining trouble would be settled in the proper way—by the raising of he wages of the men employed in these industries.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON CALEDONIAN ASSOCIATION. Successful Scottish Concert at Co-operative Hall. A Scottish concert, under the auspices of the War- rington Caledonian Association, was given in the Co-operative Hall, Cairo-street, on Thursday even- ing last. and was a pronounced success, the large hall being crowded to its fullest capacity, by an audience which was from first to last thoroughly appreciative and enthusiastic. There had been specially engaged for the occasion the Manchester Scottish Choir, with their trainer and conductor,Mr. T. Somerville Burton. The following well known and popular Scottish part songs were rendered by the choir, viz :—" The Standard on the Braes 'o Mar," " Annie Laurie," "A Hundred Pipers", '• Macgregors' Gathering " and " The Deil's awn," in addition to a Scottish Vccal Fantaaia with which they opened the second part of the programme. The choir sang with great precision, and showed at once every evidence of careful training. The fire and spirit thrown into such items as " A Hundred Pipers" and " Macgregors ' Gathering " were thrilling at times, and the fortissimos were rendered in perfect tune, which is not always the case when declamatory passages are given. Their pianossimos in the lighter and more psthetic pieces were quite a feature of their performlnce, and altogether their talented trainer and conductor, Mr. Somerville Burton has every reason to be proud of his choir. The programme opened with bagpipe selections by Messrs Robbie and Roy, pipers and dancers, who apiieared in full Highland dress, and during the evening gave smart exhibitions of dancing the Highland fling and sword dance. Perhaps the feature of the evening was the singing of the champion boy soprano, Master Wiifred V Perry, whose first contribu- tion was " Afton Water." In the second half he was listened to with almost breathless silence as he feel- ingly sang Burns' fine lyric "The Lea Rig." In response to a vociferous encore he gave "The Lark has left his watery nest." Master Perry, who has been trained by Mr. T. Somerville Burton, was first prize winner at the Blackpool, Southport, Preston. Lytham, Buxton, etc, musical festivals, and, as stated in the concert bills, was "A treat in himself." The other vocalists were Miss Isa Imrie, who contributed the stirring Jacobite song, "Sound the Pibroch," and in the second ball " Jessie's Dream," is which she was heard to great advantage, and heartily applauded. Another "rousing Jacobite air, " Wha'll be King but Charlie " was finely rendered by Mr. L. M. Sinclair. Mr. James L.Nich, who should have contributed Burns' " Bonnie Wee Thing " as his first number, was pre- vented from being present, and his place was taken by a Manchester tenor who sang " I hear you calling me," and later gave the old Scottish favourite "Mary o Argyle." The humorous part of the evening's entertainment was in the able hands of Mr. Nat Stuart, who was ix every way a success,being enthusiastically received and deservedly encored. His first two slogs "Sony Mackintosh" and " Tobermory," with arrow partying patter,keit the audience in roars if laughter.. The concluding item on the programme was the trio " Willie brewed a pack o' remit,' which was rendered , in an inimitable manner by three of the male singers. Before dispersing" Auld Lang Syne " was sung. The Caledonian Association are to be congratulated on the success which has atteu,led their efforts in promoting such an excellent concert, and great credit is due to the hard working committee who spared no pairs to make the function the unylaiiiied success it was.</p>
      </div>
