      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <p>The Liberal Party and National Service. Sir,—Would you kindly spare me a brief space in your valuable columns in which to consider the possibility of Compulsory Military Training becoming a plank in the Liberal party's prcgramme in the near future ? Up to the present day no party, either secretly or publicly, has advocated National Service, and therefore it has been left to a ncn-party organiza- tion— the National Service League—to place this questien before the electorate. Last August, how' ever, the position changed. The Liberal Government, who had been told by their expert advisers to sleep quietly in -their beds, discovered that we were dozing on the edge of a European volcano. Fortunately our Naval strength had been maintained and therefore our ships were moblilised and ready. Mr. Lloyd- George and the Cabinet also showed in the plainest possible language that • they had awakened. Thanks to their firmness and our Navy's readiness, the danger was averted, and only to-day are we mere men in the street learning the inner meauing of recent Liberal speeches The danger however has not passed. When the next crisis comes our Navy may not be near our coasts or our Regular Amy at Aldershot, as licth Navy and Army have other duties to perform in tha one-fifth of the world for which we Britishers are responsible. Then will come the day of trial for the Territorial Army, which is splendidly organized, served with devotion by officers and men, but hampered by the defects inherent in the voluntary system, which has never provided either the numbers or the training required. Will the present Government who certainly do not lack courage— introduce some form of compulsion in order to fill the diminishing ranks and give more continuous training to our citizen soldiers? Straws show the way the wind is blowing and there have been several significant symptoms of a change in the political breezes. Many of the older Liberals, men animated by the traditions of Adam Smith, J. S. Mill, W. E. t gatistone, Lord Rosebery, Sir Henry Campbell Bannerman, all of whom supported National Service. have declared that compulsoty training wculd be a just and liberal measure, besides bestowing physical and moral advantages. A former member of the National Service League—Colonel Seely—Las been appointed Under Secretary of State for War. Lord Haldane Las flirted aith the subject. The corollary of the manhood suffrage suggested by the Prime Minister is manhood service, for equal rights imply equal duties. The " Westminster Gazette" itself, has gone so far as to point out the benefits of ccmpulsory cadet training on Austi alien lines. The example of the leaders is permeating down to the rank and file and Liberal Clubs this Pinter are inviting speakers to explain our proposals, and lifslong Radicals are join- ing the National Service League.—Yours, Ac. B. S. TOWNROE. 10. Winmarleiah-street, Warrington, November 15th, MA..</p>
        <p>- - „ t ' - 4 II trers 11 Lg, to the 14 EDITOR. _I - !</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>National Service League iind</head>
        <p>The National Service League iind</p>
        <p>the Band.</p>
        <p>you slllow me to orlupliment you cn the ingenuity you display in your attempts to minimise the undoubted success of the National Service League gathering in the Parr Hall. I know, sir, that you are against the proposals of the League tut that does not worry me because before experience opened my eyes I was just as much against National Service as you are. What does, however. rather Fadden cne into see how you burk full and frank discussion cf the League's aims and objects Instead of a bald frontal attack you take cover under silence except in so far as to accuse the league of methods worthy of a general election. Such a policy, if I may soy so, is unworthy of the high line generally maintained by your paper. Such a statement, too, as the one in which you assert that 00 per cent, of those who came to the meeting went there simply to hear the band, is one which I feel sure on calmer reflection will appear as a mere rhetorical flourish. For if you really meant 00 per cent, or anything approaching that figure it would prove that you were quite ignorant of the steady growth of public opinion in favour of a citizen army on democratic lines; and that you were unaware of the growirg band of men and women who are work- ing with the greatest zeal and patriotism and with no small measure of success to arouse the country from its state if lethargy on matters of national defence. The recent march of events is driving home the logic of our arguments. The Bosnia business, the Tripoli coup-de-main, the Morocco entanglement have all helped to show how thin is tle crust which separates us from the volcano of war and therefore how neces- sary to make that crust as hard and strong as possible. Well kept as the inner history of the .Agadir incident has been, the fact that we were only. a few weeks</p>
        <p>back within a few hours of war is gradually getting known. In the midst of the really great dangers</p>
        <p>which lie around us the hue and c'ry that ;oar correspondent raises over the omission of the part played by the band is really puerile : Nero fiddling while Rome was burning. As to your reference to the " Nation-in-Arms," I am delighted to learn that you read that valuable journal, but may I remark that in reportii,g on those League meetings which happen to be helped by means of music it is unusual to refer to such and smith a band as having played, for the very good reason that space is limited and accounts have to be cut down to a minimum.</p>
        <p>Finally our worst enemies admit that the meeting was a success. We on our side readily admit that the band and the choir conteibuted towards that</p>
        <p>success. But the success that does matter, that is of real consequence and which does not concern the band or the choir, is that many who were formerly opposed to us have now, as a result of that meeting, become followers of Lord Roberts.—Yours GEORGE R. CROSFIELD. Warrington, November 15th, 1911.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>Swedenborgians.</head>
        <p>The Swedenborgians.</p>
        <p>S;-,—flaving examined all the numerous and scattered passages in which Swedenborg refers to the state in the hie hereafter of Louis XIV., George 11., David and Paul, I find the facts are as follows :</p>
        <p>1. Louia-XIV. He nowhere states that he met Louis XIV. 'in the heavenly kingdoms." He says it was granted him to speak with Louis, that whilst he was in the world he worshipped the Lord, read the Word and acknowled the Pope only as head of the Church, in consequence of which he had great dignity in the spiritual world.</p>
        <p>2. George 11. He speaks of seeing in heaven, and relates that he said that in the world he had told his Bishops that the Lord ought to be approached, and also that charity, and not faith, was primary. These two references occur in Swedenborg's works published by him in his own lifetime. The references to the state in the life hereafter of David and Paul all occur in the " Spiritual Diary," a work containing a private record of Swedenborg's experiences in the spiritual world when enjoying open vision, a work which he left in MS, and which there seems no reason to suppose he intended publishing, or thought would meet the public eye, at any rate in its present form. In these works Swedenborg certainty speaks of David and Paul as being infilled with the love of self and the love of dominion and being met by him in the com- pany of evil spirits. In none of the four cases, so far as I can ascertain, does he declare their final state. This, of course, may be inferred from what is said, with the reservation that as regards David and Paul there seems to he an element of uncertainty as to whether the spirit he refers to was that of David or some impersonating spirit. But, however that may be, Swedenborg does not actually state their final condition, and the greater number of his referecces were written before the Last Judgment took place in 1757. It should be quite clear that Sweden", rg did not I bring forward these references to the state of David and Paul after dealt into liii published works. Of Paul's Epistles he everywhere speaks with approval, as of course of I )avid's psalms. It will appear, therefore, that there is no need for me to explain "the apparent injustice of the unequal system of rewards and punishments revealed by the Seer." The system of rewards and punishments revealed by Swedenborg is that of the Scriptures, viz.: that a man is judged by his works, not by their outward apparent quality as seen by man but by their real inward quality, as seen by the Lord. Of the eternal state of anyone we nose of us know any- thing. The Christian world has assumed because al things written and works done by them that Lae XIV. and Ger rge 11. are in bell, and David ard Paul</p>
        <p>are in heaven. No-one knows. What Swedenborg says is his private diary of his experiences in the spiritual world may cause ua to luestion the accur.rey of this asaumptlm, but it in no way effects the validity of the d ctrine which the lord has made known through him. That stands cr falls by the test of Scriptu e, reason and commonsense. As the translator of the earlle7 volumes of the Spiritual Diary, Rev. Geo. Bush, M.A., Professor of Hebrew and Oriental 1 .ngusges in the New York University, in giving Lis reasons for accepting Swedenbo•g's statements as true nays:—" The evid- ence of Swedenborg's general truth is so over- whelmingly strong as t leave their (it-. New Church) minds wholly unaffected by particular .points of assertion which may be attanded with difficulty. In this case they cannot feel so sure that David was a good man as they do that Swedenborg was a true " The stupendous claims" put forth by Swedenborg are contained in his published works, and stand or fall quite independently of the statements contained in the diary, though I have yet t be convinced that these latter in any way invAlidate the former. Apologizing for taking up AO much of your space. ' and trusting what I have written may be of some help to my cmrtecus interrogator.—Believe me, yours sincerely, r Baton.</p>
        <p>W. H. CLAXTON,</p>
        <p>The Formation of a Shaknoearean Society. Sir,—ln common with many others, no doubt, I heard for the first time of the meeting called f a. this purpose, at the Parr Hall on the occasion cf Miss Terry's recital, and from the announcement made by the President of the Philomaths, I assumed, again I think, in comm n with others, that all lovers of Shakespeare in Warriogton were desired to come together to assist in forming a Society which should I have for its chief aim and purpose,the Bystemater study of the works t f Shakespeare. My surprise on finding that the Society bad already been formed, fficers ' appointed, rules draws up, and the work of an entire session practically decided upon—was, I think, shared by many who were present —those of us who bad not previously known anything of the initial proceed- ings being as it appeared simp'y invited t 3 enroll eur- selves as subscribers—with the privilege of attending half a dozen meetings in a year to hear papal s read or to listen to readings from plays which sre being rehearsed by the members of a separate and privately organized Dramatic Club, for pr, sentation on the stage. To find also that the entire proceedings of this inaugural meeting had been mapped out in a cut and dried fashion, as evidently not contemplating the possibilityrof any prospective member desiring the opportunity of suggesting any variation in the consti- tution or of the methods to be adopted for effectively realizing the chief object of such a Society—namely, the cultivation of a critical habit of mind in examining the text and the co-ordination of the members in their deliberations opcia it—was, to say the least of it, dis- appointing. Professor Elton and Canon Morley Steven- son, wee, both of them, most clear and explicit in the advice they proffered to thosi who had it in their minds to take up the study of Shakespears. Not by the mere learning, by rote, or reciting with orthodox declamation, passages from the more familiar plays. could the student hope to derive for himself and still lees to convey to others the inmost purport of the dramatist. Had the promoters of the Society after these gentlemen's speeches made it clear to the audi- ence assembled that it was intended to adhere to a strict method of critical study and examination of the plays, every member taking an active part in it, and that, as Mr. Furnivall recommends, the members should "worry every line—eschewing the vice of wholesale emendation," they would have anti-fled many who were in doubt as to the probable value of the coerse sketched out in the programme or syllabus which they had submitted. The first noint I think on which light is needed is as to the number and character of the meetings to be held during a session. Whilst it may be eminently adapted to stimulate the interest of the large bo3y of members to read esqtys or treatises on particular plays or characters at monthly interva`,, especially if it be so arranged that ample opportunity be afforded, not alone for complimentary and eulogistic speeches on the work of the essayist, but also for the free expression of opinion and for candid and outspoken criticism by any or every student present on the opinions advanced in the paper which has been read - So that the collective wisdom of the members may be brought to bear on the elucidation of knotty points and disputed readings, etc. Still, the main design of a Shakespearean Society, if it is to effect any progress in the diffusion of know- ledge about Shakespeare and his writings to the rising generation, should be La afford opportunities for the younger, as well as the more earnest and persevering of its older members to meet together possibly in smaller cambers end separated into classes, much more freqnently for the systematic study of the lesser known plays and of all Shakespeare's wi iings. chnnologically at d as a whole. An act or even a single scene may be taken at a time, to be read and thoroughly discussed, all the difficulties encounteied b ag fought out in common. There in no greater delight to thorough-going lovers of Shakespeare than that which springs from the desire to import to others what has been acquired by study of this kind, and by the very act of communicating, "knowledge grows from more and more." How aptly and how beautifully does Tennyson express this is the stanza : " What delights can equal those That stir the spirit's inner deeps When one that loves, but knows not, reaps A truth from one that loves and knows." I am very jealous for the cause of Shakesperean study, and I am above all things anxious that erbst-1 ever is begun should be based on firm and enduring foundations, that we may not have to witness another of the many failures to quicken the iuterest in poetic and imaginative stndies in the town, a town which plumes itself as having at one time enjoyed a reputa- tion for being a "seat of learning." In view of the meeting on Tuesday evening , having been closed without any expression of opinion being asked for from the large body of people who came together in response to the invitation. I trust that the Committee will see the propriety of holdirg another</p>
      </div>
