      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>An Anti-Socialist Apology.</head>
        <p>An Anti-Socialist Apology.</p>
        <p>Sir,-1 am enclosing copy of letter I received from Liberal Assici.tion, Wallington, I am also sending letter from myself for your columns which I trust you will place in a good p sition.—Yours very truly, CHAS. MeNAIR.</p>
        <p>001.0 OF LETTER RECEIVED 51100 NTARRINGTON LIBERAL A•SOCIATIoN, DEWSEY STREET. Chas. McNair, Esq., Nov. 16th, 1011. 126, Roxburgh-street, Walton, Liverp 01, Dear sir,— My executive take a serious view of the unwarranted use by, you re your advert last Saturday (copy of which I enclose) of the wot ds : Tickets and Syllabus on application to the Secretary, Liberal Club,' and I am Instructed to ask you ID give the same publicity to an announcement stating that the same words were used without our knowledge or authority, to avoid further proceedings. I suggest that you send a letter with your signature making the necessary withdrawal, to the newspapers for Siturday next. Yours ti Signed JAS. T. LOCKER.</p>
        <p>COPY OF REPLY</p>
        <p>November ICth, 1911. The Secretary, Liberal Association, Bewsey-street, Warrington. Dear Sir,—l am in receipt of your favour of yester- day and much regret to know that the announcemeat in question has caused you and your executive any annoyance. I had not the slightest intention to take advantage 31 your name, as the lecture and our whole movement is strictly Non-Party, including as it does men of all parties except Socialists, and for this reason I wrote you on the 7th. inst , enclosing twenty tickets for dis- posal, which I thought you would not object to do, especially as you did not reply to my letter. This was the leasen why I made the announcement in question, it was purely a question of convenience to those who wished to know where to get the ticket-a, and nothing was further from my thoughts than to cause annoyance or pain to anyone. I much regret the occurrence and as requested I am ending you a letter and my reply to the local press.—Ycurs truly. CHARLES 11,NAIR.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>PICTUREDROME</head>
        <p>THE PICTUREDROME</p>
        <p>A Classical Pictorial Play.</p>
        <p>The enterprising management of the Picturedrome, Sankey-street, announce a remarkable attraction L,r next week. In addition to the usual programme at the evening performances only, the great classical pictorial play. " Dante's Inferno ' or " A vision of Hades " will be presented. The picture is founded upon Gustave Dor6s famous paintings in the Dor«.. Gallery, London, and it is said to " beggar description." It will be shown for the whole of the week. On Monday, Tuesday and Wednesday the great Ambrosia war story " After fifty years," which won the gold medal for the best cinematograph production of the year, will be exhibifed. On Thursday, Friday and Satur- day evenings there will be a realistic dramatic pictnre Wages of War." On and after Monday, November 27th, the manage- ment will adopt the system, which has proved popular in many places, of continuous performances every evening from 6 to 10.30. Persons entering the theat.e at any time from 6 to 0 p.m. may be certain of witnessing a full programme. The matinee,: will be held as usual every afternoon at 3 o'clock. and tea will be served free of charge in the 61. and Is. seats.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>ROYAL COURT THEATRE. " The Sins of London."</head>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE. " The Sins of London."</p>
        <p>One of the most popular of the Melville dramas 13106 of London " is the attraction at the Royal Court Theatre this week. Though the story is conceived on somewhat orthodox lines it is so cleverly worked out as to excite an unusual degree of interest and some of the situations are invested with an intense realism which holds the audience as bye spell. There is also much that is notable in the way of stage craft, the" effects "in the shipwreck scene being remark- ably vivid. The company presenting the play is a strong one and some capital character studies receive careful and convincing presentment. Mr. Wilford Bailey secures a large share of the honours with his masterly portrayal ' of Samuel Owen, proving himself an actor of no mean talent. The attractive parts of the hero and heroine are well sustained by Mr. Carl F. Kerzo and Mies Ethel Bracewell, who soon established them- selves warm favourites. Miss Bilda Glynn does full justice t the important role of the f Acina,ing Gertie Coleman. who glories in her failings as if they were virtues. An acceptable humorous element is introduced by Mr. Charles D Cleveland whose efforts to arouse mirth ate always successful. The orchestra, under Mr. Tom Boothroyd's conductorship, submits a capital light progt amme. NEXT WEEK.--" TRILBY."</p>
        <p>An attraction of more than usual interest is promised for next week when Mr. Henry Renonf presents the ever-welcome " Trilby," an adaptation fr. m George Du Manner's famous novel. Mr. Henouf, who is remembered here for his rowerful portrayal of John Storm in "The Christian" will appear as Seengali and Miss Norah Vaughan will impersonate Trilby. The play, which is fascinating, humorous, thrilling and wierd is certain to attract large" houses." and a thoroughly adequate perform- ande may confidently be expected.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>WARRINGTON MUSICAL SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON MUSICAL SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>Next Weeks Concert.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Musical Sociity will .rive its first subscription concert of the @imam in the Parr Hall on Wednesday evening rext when a programme embracing features of special interest will be pre- sented. The chief item will bo Sir Edward Elgar's cantata for chorus and orchestra "The Black Knight." The following notes compiled from Mr. Ernest New- II an's book on " Eiger" in " The Music of the Masters "series will be .read with interest :</p>
        <p>" Ti e Black Knight " is one of Elgar's eatliest choral works and was first produced at Worcester in 1893. It is a settling of Longfellow's translation of Uhland's weird poem " Der Schwarza Ritter." The , music is very modern in style, the, chorus being only ' part of the means for obtaining tone-colour. Ihe orchestra is treated with great skill, and the whole work is dramatic, au iking. pictureeque,and is marked throughout with a distinct individuality. It is worthy of tote that Flgar calls " The Black Knight" • symphony for chorus and orchestra. The story is briefly as follows :—An old king is celebrating " Pentecost, the Feast of Gladness," among his children and knights. His son is every- where victorious in the tourney until a sable knight rides to the barrier and tbscomfits him in the first encounter. The scene then changes to the ball where the guests are dancing. The black figure again enters and dances with the king's daughter ; at his chilling touch the flowers in her hair and breast fall faded to</p>
        <p>.. the ground. At the banquet that follows the old king, all distraught," gazes at his son and daughter, lost in mournful reflection npcn their palenees. The black guest hands them a beaker of wins, telling them it will make them whole ; their faces grow colourless when they have drainee it, and they die before the eyes of their " fear-stricken father." Ile appeals to the Black Knight to take him too • but the "grim guest' only replies, "Roses in the Spring I gather." So the story ends,—" Those whom the gods love best die young." The musical treatment of the first scene, describing the rejoicings and the tournament, is mainly based on a swinging theme that is typical of Eiger. The choral writing is uncommonly spirited and attractive, and the pictures very vividly drawn. Especially striking is the section descriptive of the tournament, with its vigorous vocal phrases and its fine orchestral colouring. In the second ecene the Black Ktight rides up to the barrier, the music commencing quietly and rather mysteriously and being gradually worked up with fine effect. The crowd demands his name and scutcheon. To their clamorous cry there succeeds a very effective silence that is ultimately broken by a quiet echo of the choral phrase in the orchestra. "Should I speak it here, Ye would stand aghast with fear," replies the Knight in sombre tones. Fragments of the opening theme of this scene are cleverly used to keep before us the picture of the Knight slowly and impressively advancing. The dreadful nature of the grim visitor is always more ond more definitely suggested as the theme develops. The succeeding passage, descriptive of the Knight riding into the lists and vanquishing the king's son, is ens of great dramatic strength and power, the scene ending in a quiet way that gives us a curious sensation of awe. The third scene (descriptive of the dance) is most melodious and full of charm. When the Knight dances with the king's daughter— " Dented is sable iron nark, Danced a measure weird and dark,"</p>
        <p>the new dance is a-e-t-in—an-WP;O-p-rri-a-te-ra-tidosphere of trouble and foreboding; one can literally see the scared courtiers huddling together and whispering their fears to each other: .</p>
        <p>The fourth scene (the banquet) opens with a broad and flowing melody and is very dramatically treated. A beautiful effect is obtained where the unaccompanied chorus describe the fainting children embracing their father. . .</p>
        <p>The work is rounded off with tender reminiscences of the buoyant theme with which it opened, and closes very quietly. In addition to the cantata, the programme promises i• surfieit of the good things. Chiefly notable is the concerto for pianoforte and orchestra in A minor (Grieg) in which Mr. T. W. Pearce, of Warrington, will figure as solo pianist. Mr. Pearce will also play " Eutde in F Major" (Chopin) and "Moto Perpetuo" (Weber). Miss Violet Elliott is the vocal soloist. She will sing" My heart is weary" (Gor)ng Thomas), " Beat of a passionate heart "(M Ph;ibps) and "Life's blessing" (P. Fletcher). The orchestra will play the overture " Rorsimonde " (Schubert) and the chorus will sing" By Babylon's wave" (Gonnod) sod" 0, by rivers" (Bishop). Seals may now be booked at Mr. R. H. Pearzoo's, Bridge-street.</p>
        <p>ADMIRAL TOGO AND THE " ALLI•NCE Japan the Canadian Pacific Railway is now popularly known as the" Alliance Route," and it is by this root. that Admiral Count Togo very appropriately elected to return home from the Coronation, which he attended as a member of the suite of the Imperial Japanese Delega- tion. By the instructions of Sir Thomas Shaughnessy, President of the railway, Admiral Togo was treated as a guest of the company throughout his journey. The Admiral took the private car "Canada " at Niagara Falls, proceeding to Banff end then to Vancouver, whence he telegraped to Sir Thomas his acknowledg- meats. Admiral Togo is only one in the long line of distinguished Japanese who have thus travelled over the Canadian Pacific Railway. The" Japan Gazette" contends there is no doubt that the broad spirit of hospitality shown by the Canadian Pscillc Railwaj on this and similar occasions makes materially for the promotion of pleasant international relations, and adds that it has the best reasons for saying that the attentions shown to one so much respected and beloved as Admiral Togo are deeply appreciated in the highest quarters in that country.</p>
        <p>The "Liverpool Weekly Post" presentation Sheet Almanac for 19l 2. will he Given Free with the issue of pest Saturday. (Nov. 25tb ). Beet coloured centrepiece yet issued. The same issue will contain opening of A daughter of Heaven," • stirring and topical sebrial of colliery life of to-day, by the great Laacashire writer, Allen Clarke. To save disappointment order your " Weekly Post" Almanac to-day. No reprint possible.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH LIBERALS.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH LIBERALS.</p>
        <p>MR. J. H. WHITWORTH'S ADDRESS ON THE INSURANCE BILL.</p>
        <p>The Tory Leadership.</p>
        <p>Mr. J.ll. Whitworth, who ccntested the Knaterord Divition in the Libei.al interest at the lent eletion, . .</p>
        <p>gave a.; eridne's-s rVir—the 1,1;:tio—n;l Insurance Bill at e meeting of He Liberals of Stockton Heath held in Providence schoolroom on Thuraday evening. Councillor H Roberta presided and seas supported on the platform by Messrs HO Roberta, E Lloyd Jones, S Albinson, J R Tomlinson and A Mounfield. The Chairman referred to the resignation of Mr. Balfour—a man who was respected all round—from the leadership of the Conservative party. Owing, as Mr. Austen Chamberlain had said, to the fact that there wan no an on the front Opposition bench who . .</p>
        <p>stood hoed and shoulders above et of.them, the</p>
        <p>two aspirants for the position—Messrs. Long and Chamberlain, who, if they had gone to the vote, would probably have created a very serious epl t in the party— bad, in the words of the Conservative Prose, patrioti- cally stood on one side nod another gentleman who only a few weeks ego was not even dreamed of far that important position Mr. lionar Law, had been elected. He (the speaker) was not going to prophesy, sod it did not concern Liberals very much how far the . . .</p>
        <p>s-elejio-n‘int Mr.Bonar Law ‘was j.naCified by sobsequpo%</p>
        <p>events. He was, however, boun'd to say Chat in regard to one of his letters of recommendation to Conserve- tine candidates in different parts of the country, air. Boner Law might have been a little more careful is the language he used with reference to the Insurance Rill. Writing to Lud Robert Cecil, the nwo leader charged the Government with attempting to rush the Bill through in a spirit of levity. He (the speaker) did not hesitate to any that a more</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>BUM INA IMF._ CH AR 9 It</head>
        <p>A BUM INA IMF._ CH AR 9 It</p>
        <p>had never been -Made aesinet the Liberal party. (Applause). If it was a spirit of levity whichrprorop- ted the Government to do all they possibly could to lessen the hours of misery and anxiety caused by sickness and unemployment, he was glad they were actuated by it. (Rear, hear). Long might they con- continue in such a spirit, in order that the sense of responsibility which they had with regard to those who suffered by the way, might be brought home to the nation at large. (Applause). He could not say he , was struck with admiration for the spirit which had characterised the Tory party in the by-elections in their treatment of this Bill. When it was brought forward "</p>
        <p>-s it received the blessing el the leaders of the Tory party who quite agreed with the principle that it was absolutely essential that something should be done in the direction the Bill proposed. But when it came to putting to the practical test how far the faith they professed in the house of Commons was to to carried into effect in the constituencies, what did they do ? What they had done at Oldham—appealed to the cupidity which still existed among a certain set of manufacturers, to the selfishness which waa innate in human nature, and tried to induce everybody they could, in order that pennies should not be forced out of their pockets to help the needy, to vote against this humane measure. For one reason, be was not sorry they had Mr. Bonar Law at the head of the Conserva- tive party. The time would come when they would have • straight fight on the most important question of toe hour (the Insurance BiL would then be i• opera- tion)—tariff reform. Although they had won three Important Parliamentary contests on that question, they had not finished with it. They were going to base it in the concrete next time. There could be</p>
        <p>NO SHILLY-SHALLYING</p>
        <p>about it when the Tory party put e: the head of its admire a man whose sole justifiestion for consideration RE their hands was the fact that he, more than any other man with the exception of Mr. Joseph Chamberlain, had forced the question of tariff reform upon the atten- tion of the Conservative party. The sooner they had the fight and cleared the air of that question once and for all, the better it would be for the country. (Applause ) On the fiscal question Councillor Roberts instanced an important concern associated with works in this country and on the Continent. Although the hours of labour at the Continental works were con- siderably longer and the wages per hour considerably lower than in this country, the firm could produce precisely the same article more cheaply in this country than its Protected competitots &amp;bread could. The moral of that was that everything which made for effithency on the part of the workman made fur the cheapening of the cost of production. That was why he recom- mended to their serious consideration the Insurance Bill. Everything ehich helped to make the worker a fitter, healthier, and stronger mita and to free him from the forebodings of poverty, resulting from sickness and unemployment, tended also to increase his efficiency as a workman. Therefore on grounds of political economy, apart from the humanitarian aide, it was a sound and wise policy that the insurance scheme should be' entered upon without any serious loss of time (Applause.) Mr. J. 11. Whitworth, in a very lucid address, pointed out that the Insurance Bill proposed to deal with two of the greatest evils which the working classes ever had to face. Let them ask any working man what were the two things he most dreaded and wished to fend against and they would had they were the fear of being ill or of losing his work. Those were the evils which the Bill proposed to deal with. Not only from the workers' point of view hut fuel that of the State, there was a great amount of evil done and money lost every year throegh preventible illness and the harm , which was done to families by the bread winner's inability to follow his work regularly and the conse- quent breaking up of homes. There were people who said that illness WIL•1 beyond human control and that nothing that the State could do could affect the health of the population. That was untrue, and it was significant that according to the report of Dr. Newsholm, chief inspector of the Local Government Board, during the last forty years the death rate in this country bed declined from 21 per 1,0110 to 15 per 1,000. The doctor attributed that very largely to the increased activity of local health cora- , mitteee, better sanitation and the improved conditions under which people lived to-day. Lust year there were 54,425 deaths from consumption. That was considerably lower than the average of preceding years and if the same death rate prevailed as thirty years ago, the deaths would have nembered 73,000. So that something was being done, even under present con- ditions, to check that great scourge. Consumption was largely a preventible diesel:se and, if dealt with in its early stages a very large percentage of cases cou:d be cured, lie believed that if Mr. Lloyd-George's scheme for sanatoria was in operation, there would not be third of the present number of deaths from that disease. (Applause.; Dealing with the unemployment question, Mr. Whitworth pointed out that people who were thrown out, of work for a long tote often sank Into the unemployable class. Thelon to the State caused by people being unable to keep their place in society owing to the pressure of circumstances and had trade was difficult adequately to estimate. his sub- mitted that the method by which the Insurance Bill pro posed to dead with both evils was a fair one—fair as between the various parties involved and an being the only practical method. It was for THE CRITICS OF THE RILL</p>
        <p>either to nay "-We deny thei existence of these evils," or to submit an alternative scheme. That would be the straightforward and manly course to pursue but the Tory party had not adopted it. Three times within the last six years had the Tory party met the Liberal piny upon the stricken field and each time the Tory party had been routed. They were therefore becoming rather shy at opposing Liberal legislation direct—(laughter) —and were resorting to guerilla warfare, sniping at outposts from behind hedges. (Laughtes). They dare' not meet the Government with regard to the principles on which the measure was founded or offer an elterna- tire method for dealing with the evils. They thought that if they swallowed the principles they were free from responsibility and had it licence to do as much damage as they could without being answerable for the consequences. Defending the payment of contributions by the three parties involved, Mr. Whitworth pointed out that it was only right that the workmen should poi their share svben they considered that at present Mite were between 4.000,000 and 5,600,00 members of the friendly societies who found it to their common Interest to contribute the whole amount to insure themselves against loss by sickness. Having referred to the benefits which would accrue to the employer and the State, Mr. Whitworth proceeded to outline the provie.rns of the Bill, lie believed that in the coons of tbe next ten or fifteen years they would see very largely incresaed benefits given under the Act. 'The present benefits were the very minimum, and the improvement might take the form of a reduction in the age limit for old age pensions, a reduced rate of Insurance contribution or tte payment of funeral benefits. The whole of the :noney would be espended for the benefit of the workers. (Applause). The friendly society system at present only touched about 5,000,000 people but as they wanted to touch 15,000,130 the only way they could do it was by bringing in a compulsory State scheme Ipotesi of leaving it toprivate tirtraitset.he AwsoiA'irn :nth Cilidadnvaotngt get out the nweg until h enue he was in arreare for an average of more than 13 weeks for each year of his membership. With regard to the unemployment part of the scheme, Mr. Whitworth expressed the view that in time it would be applied to all trades. The advocated of Drift reform claimed that it was a sort of insurance afainst unemployment. As smatter of fact, however, it would increase the cost of living 2s. ltd. or 3s. in the .6, while, judging from the experience of other nations, it was not stall Clear that it would give more work or safeguard ius against trade fluctriations. The Insurance Bill was offering a tiona-fide insurance against imemployrnent. at the cost not of 21. 64. but of nd. per week. Was it not better to have insurime against unemployment which really did what it pretended to do at a small cost, rather thew a system which would make dearer the food of the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>p -- – S</head>
        <p>p -- - S</p>
        <p>GLOBE</p>
        <p>Furnishing Co.</p>
        <p>(J. R. GRANT, Prop.) PEMBROKE L PLACE, iverpool</p>
        <p>purple and purport to do something which lari:l reform never had done, never would do and never could do. (Applause.) Mr NNlsitworth then answered a number of questions from the audience. He pointed out that the Workmen', Compensation Act would not become obsolete through the Insurance Bill. Where compensation was paid, however, the luminance would not be givrn in addition. The health authorities would be empowered to take steps to consider compensation for injured persons in disputed cases—a greet advantage to the workmae, as in many cases at the present time he was not able to enforce his claim. Mr. E. Lloyd Junes, in proposing a vote of thanks to Mr. Whitworth, emphasised the fact that the whole atheme was based upon aoiucial cilculations. Mr. H. G. Roberts seconded and said that lithe Act were in operation the work of Guild* of Help would be very materially assisted. The vote was heartily paused and a similar compli- ment to the Chairman concluded the meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>WARRINGTON BAND OF HOPE UNION.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BAND OF HOPE UNION.</p>
        <p>Temperance Sonday, November 12th, was strictly observed in a large number of places of worship in Warrington, several Sunday schools basing sent reports in as follow :</p>
        <p>BOLD-STREET WenetraN.—Temperance EeT2lOOB were preached in this church in the morning, by Rev. W. A. Beckett, and in the evening by Rev. Hugh Williamson, B.A. At the close of the lessons in the afternoon the Rev. H. Williamson, 11A , gave an address in Sunday school. Solos were rung by Miss F Wood and Sophie Cross. In the Men's Bible Class, a peper dealing with this subject was given by Mr. Stanley C. Jones. Appeals were made at all the services for pledges in church and school, a large number responding to this request. WORKINGNIEN,I. MIS,ION, BANK QUAY.—In the Sunday schools addresses were given to about 3CO scholars by Mr. W. A. Tilling and Mr. W. Worrell. In the evening Mr. W. Jones preached a Temperance. Sermon to a crowded congregation. EMMANUEL REFORMID CHURCFL—SeTMOTHI wen preached morning and evening by Rev. W. T. Jaye end an address given in Sunday school by Mr. 'F. J. Moos, Cockhedge Mission. An address was given in Sunday school in the afternoon, by Mr. Walter Carter and a sermon preached in the evening by Mr. Beswick. PFNKETHWESTETAN.—SpeciaI reference WWI made at both services in this chapel in the morning by I:ev. If. Middiewood and in the evsning by Rev. li. Morgan. In the afternoon a special service eats held in the Sunday school when the Superintendent, Mr. J. G. Withenshaw gave some interesting statements with regard tr. temperance amongst business men. The Rev. H. Middlewood also gave an admirable address to the scholars, pledge cards were distributed, and many took the advantage of signing the pledge. STOCKTON HEATH WKSLEYAN.—Iit the morning Mr. S. Moss was the preacher and made special reference to the evils of intemperance, and in the evening Mr. C. Ackerley of I,ymm was the preacher and in the course of a most able discourse appealed to Christian people to become total abstainers. In the afternoon Mr. Ackerley addressed the teachers and scholars In the Sunday school, many of the 'choler* signing the pledge and adult members of the school giving their names to be added to the Circuit Temperance Roll. HILL CLIFFE IdarrisT. —The pulpit was occupied morning and evening by the Rev. George Curtis, and an address was given in the Sunday school in the after- noon by Mr. J. Moss, superintendent. In many other places of worship special mention was made to this subject, the reports of which have not come to hand. and throughout the a hole district, eapecially in the Free Churches, Temperance Sanday was generally observed in conjunction a,tti similar services held throughout the country.</p>
      </div>
